{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788129501278,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"id":"prt_054d2e047001Kh9nsU6174rOQj","messageID":"msg_054d2d960001ZvhxHqLBUsA8hc","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788129502058,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-89e4a1290d8e68ed","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/questions.md","offset":470,"limit":90},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/questions.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n470: \n471: Worth being explicit that cheapness is not an argument. What is recorded here is that the cost of\n472: identity is lower than it looked, not that the language wants it.\n473: \n474: [The enum decision](../draft.md#decided-enums-nominal-and-json-native) has since answered every bullet for\n475: enums specifically: they are identities (exhaustiveness requires it), `enum` is the declaration\n476: form, and variant constructors land in the value namespace with bare-until-ambiguous\n477: resolution. Records are deliberately not carried along; the alias-or-identity question stays\n478: open for them, and this entry now tracks only that half.\n479: \n480: ### Q35. What are stdout and stderr, and does a program write or return?\n481: \n482: This document mentions `stdout` once and `stderr` never. For a language whose subject is\n483: transforming data on a command line, that is not deferral, it is an oversight, and it is recorded\n484: here rather than quietly fixed because the absence shaped things: every question about streams so\n485: far has been about values coming *in*.\n486: \n487: What exists is one answer by default. A program is an expression, its value is rendered by the\n488: type-driven printer, and that is the whole of output. It has served: line-oriented output needed\n489: no side effect, because a `Str` containing newlines already is line-oriented output.\n490: \n491: What it does not answer:\n492: \n493: - **Does a program write, or return?** Returning is what makes a program an expression and what\n494:   keeps `map` reorderable, since a write is an effect and an effect is an ordering constraint.\n495:   Writing is what a long-running filter over a stream has to do, because holding the output until\n496:   the input ends is the thing streaming exists to avoid. These are not obviously reconcilable.\n497: - **Is stderr in the language or under it?** Every backend refuses in its own words today, and the\n498:   agreement harness deliberately checks only *that* they refuse. Making the message part of the\n499:   language means six backends must agree on it.\n500: - **Does output have a type?** Input does, and it is checked. Output is whatever the body renders\n501:   to, which means the printer is the only specification of the format.\n502: - **Does a stream of outputs exist at all**, or does a program produce one value whose rendering\n503:   happens to be long? jq answers the first; the design so far assumes the second without saying\n504:   so.\n505: \n506: Blocked on the same thing as [Q5](#q5-stream-lowering-strategy-across-the-three-backends): a\n507: program that writes as it goes is a program with an effect layer.\n508: \n509: The fused `jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop has since made write-as-it-goes real at the backend\n510: level, and [the streams decision](../draft.md#decided-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed) gave the effect\n511: layer a type -- so the blockage above is gone, and the question is sharpened rather than\n512: answered. What was decided there about output is deliberately minimal: `jsonlines` is a sink,\n513: legal only as the program's outermost expression, with no result type. That removes the old\n514: placeholder (`jsonlines(...) : Str`, a type claiming the whole output exists as one value)\n515: without deciding whether stdout is a value, an effect, or something a program returns into.\n516: Everything in the bullet list above remains open.\n517: \n518: ### Q36. Does a real module system need imports, multiple files, and enforced privacy?\n519: \n520: One file exists: `prelude.toy`, always merged in whole, `pub` picking which of its definitions a\n521: program receives. What it does not have: a way to name what a program wants rather than receiving\n522: all of it; a way for a program's own file to export something another file imports; more than one\n523: file to import from at all; and enforced privacy, since a non-`pub` prelude definition today is\n524: not \"private to the prelude,\" it is simply never compiled, which forecloses a `pub` function\n525: calling a private helper.\n526: \n527: None of these were needed to get one function (`unlines`) out of six backends' worth of hand-\n528: written codegen, and building them speculatively risks shaping them around a prelude that has\n529: exactly one function in it. What would force an answer: a second prelude function needing an\n530: internal helper, at which point the non-`pub`-is-simply-absent rule stops being free.\n531: \n532: ### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\n533: \n534: The representation is [decided](../draft.md#decided-float-is-javascripts-double): IEEE 754 binary64,\n535: JavaScript's number. Everything observable about it is not, and each piece has to survive the\n536: agreement harness, which checks bytes.\n537: \n538: - **Printing.** Every backend must render the same double to the same text, and their defaults\n539:   do not agree on shortest-roundtrip versus fixed formatting, or on `1e21`-style switchover\n540:   points. The printer is currently the only specification of output format\n541:   ([Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)), which makes\n542:   this a per-backend conformance rule to be stated by hand, the same lesson as\n543:   [backends can agree and still be wrong](../research-log/backends-can-agree-and-still-be-wrong.md).\n544: - **NaN and Infinity.** IEEE produces both; JSON can spell neither. A language whose values\n545:   are JSON-shaped either forbids them (a check on every producing operation), maps them to\n546:   something at the boundary (jq-style lossiness, the kind this design usually refuses), or\n547:   admits values its own output cannot carry.\n548: - **Division by zero.** The Int rule says a zero divisor is the only way arithmetic fails.\n549:   IEEE says `1.0 / 0.0` is `Infinity`, no failure at all. Keeping both means division's\n550:   behavior depends on its operand type; unifying means overriding one standard or the other.\n551: \n552: None of this blocks anything else, so it waits for `Float` to be forced by a real program the\n553: way `inputs` and `jsonlines` were.\n\n(End of file - total 553 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\nWorth being explicit that cheapness is not an argument. What is recorded here is that the cost of\nidentity is lower than it looked, not that the language wants it.\n\n[The enum decision](../draft.md#decided-enums-nominal-and-json-native) has since answered every bullet for\nenums specifically: they are identities (exhaustiveness requires it), `enum` is the declaration\nform, and variant constructors land in the value namespace with bare-until-ambiguous\nresolution. Records are deliberately not carried along; the alias-or-identity question stays\nopen for them, and this entry now tracks only that half.\n\n### Q35. What are stdout and stderr, and does a program write or return?\n\nThis document mentions `stdout` once and `stderr` never. For a language whose subject is\ntransforming data on a command line, that is not deferral, it is an oversight, and it is recorded\nhere rather than quietly fixed because the absence shaped things: every question about streams so\nfar has been about values coming *in*.\n\nWhat exists is one answer by default. A program is an expression, its value is rendered by the\ntype-driven printer, and that is the whole of output. It has served: line-oriented output needed\nno side effect, because a `Str` containing newlines already is line-oriented output.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/questions.md","text":"\nWorth being explicit that cheapness is not an argument. What is recorded here is that the cost of\nidentity is lower than it looked, not that the language wants it.\n\n[The enum decision](../draft.md#decided-enums-nominal-and-json-native) has since answered every bullet for\nenums specifically: they are identities (exhaustiveness requires it), `enum` is the declaration\nform, and variant constructors land in the value namespace with bare-until-ambiguous\nresolution. Records are deliberately not carried along; the alias-or-identity question stays\nopen for them, and this entry now tracks only that half.\n\n### Q35. What are stdout and stderr, and does a program write or return?\n\nThis document mentions `stdout` once and `stderr` never. For a language whose subject is\ntransforming data on a command line, that is not deferral, it is an oversight, and it is recorded\nhere rather than quietly fixed because the absence shaped things: every question about streams so\nfar has been about values coming *in*.\n\nWhat exists is one answer by default. A program is an expression, its value is rendered by the\ntype-driven printer, and that is the whole of output. It has served: line-oriented output needed\nno side effect, because a `Str` containing newlines already is line-oriented output.\n\nWhat it does not answer:\n\n- **Does a program write, or return?** Returning is what makes a program an expression and what\n  keeps `map` reorderable, since a write is an effect and an effect is an ordering constraint.\n  Writing is what a long-running filter over a stream has to do, because holding the output until\n  the input ends is the thing streaming exists to avoid. These are not obviously reconcilable.\n- **Is stderr in the language or under it?** Every backend refuses in its own words today, and the\n  agreement harness deliberately checks only *that* they refuse. Making the message part of the\n  language means six backends must agree on it.\n- **Does output have a type?** Input does, and it is checked. Output is whatever the body renders\n  to, which means the printer is the only specification of the format.\n- **Does a stream of outputs exist at all**, or does a program produce one value whose rendering\n  happens to be long? jq answers the first; the design so far assumes the second without saying\n  so.\n\nBlocked on the same thing as [Q5](#q5-stream-lowering-strategy-across-the-three-backends): a\nprogram that writes as it goes is a program with an effect layer.\n\nThe fused `jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop has since made write-as-it-goes real at the backend\nlevel, and [the streams decision](../draft.md#decided-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed) gave the effect\nlayer a type -- so the blockage above is gone, and the question is sharpened rather than\nanswered. What was decided there about output is deliberately minimal: `jsonlines` is a sink,\nlegal only as the program's outermost expression, with no result type. That removes the old\nplaceholder (`jsonlines(...) : Str`, a type claiming the whole output exists as one value)\nwithout deciding whether stdout is a value, an effect, or something a program returns into.\nEverything in the bullet list above remains open.\n\n### Q36. Does a real module system need imports, multiple files, and enforced privacy?\n\nOne file exists: `prelude.toy`, always merged in whole, `pub` picking which of its definitions a\nprogram receives. What it does not have: a way to name what a program wants rather than receiving\nall of it; a way for a program's own file to export something another file imports; more than one\nfile to import from at all; and enforced privacy, since a non-`pub` prelude definition today is\nnot \"private to the prelude,\" it is simply never compiled, which forecloses a `pub` function\ncalling a private helper.\n\nNone of these were needed to get one function (`unlines`) out of six backends' worth of hand-\nwritten codegen, and building them speculatively risks shaping them around a prelude that has\nexactly one function in it. What would force an answer: a second prelude function needing an\ninternal helper, at which point the non-`pub`-is-simply-absent rule stops being free.\n\n### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\n\nThe representation is [decided](../draft.md#decided-float-is-javascripts-double): IEEE 754 binary64,\nJavaScript's number. Everything observable about it is not, and each piece has to survive the\nagreement harness, which checks bytes.\n\n- **Printing.** Every backend must render the same double to the same text, and their defaults\n  do not agree on shortest-roundtrip versus fixed formatting, or on `1e21`-style switchover\n  points. The printer is currently the only specification of output format\n  ([Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)), which makes\n  this a per-backend conformance rule to be stated by hand, the same lesson as\n  [backends can agree and still be wrong](../research-log/backends-can-agree-and-still-be-wrong.md).\n- **NaN and Infinity.** IEEE produces both; JSON can spell neither. A language whose values\n  are JSON-shaped either forbids them (a check on every producing operation), maps them to\n  something at the boundary (jq-style lossiness, the kind this design usually refuses), or\n  admits values its own output cannot carry.\n- **Division by zero.** The Int rule says a zero divisor is the only way arithmetic fails.\n  IEEE says `1.0 / 0.0` is `Infinity`, no failure at all. Keeping both means division's\n  behavior depends on its operand type; unifying means overriding one standard or the other.\n\nNone of this blocks anything else, so it waits for `Float` to be forced by a real program the\nway `inputs` and `jsonlines` were.","lineStart":470,"lineEnd":553,"totalLines":553,"truncated":false}},"title":"plans/questions.md","time":{"start":1788129502052,"end":1788129502056}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_054d2e058001jWJyyk92yDSWw3","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","messageID":"msg_054d2d960001ZvhxHqLBUsA8hc"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788129502065,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"id":"prt_054d2e36f001bHFiwup6W8l9ui","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","messageID":"msg_054d2d960001ZvhxHqLBUsA8hc","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":25575,"input":7301,"output":98,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":18176}},"cost":0.000783021}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788129503157,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"id":"prt_054d2e7b2001aW0hwSNcipwumP","messageID":"msg_054d2e3790016bEL8mrqd5N4dq","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","snapshot":"5a1012ab7eb7388a8de2a55a732be7fdbf823735","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788129505059,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8b4b160cafab4cda","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/docs/adr/0001-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/docs/adr/0001-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: status: accepted\n3: ---\n4: \n5: # Stream is the effect layer, typed\n6: \n7: The fused `jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop made programs stream for real while the checker still\n8: typed `inputs` as `Vec<T>` and `jsonlines(...)` as `Str`: whether a program streamed was a\n9: backend-side pattern match, and a program one shape away from the pattern silently fell back to\n10: materializing all of stdin. We decided `Stream<T>` enters the surface type grammar as the type\n11: of effect-layer multiplicity: born only at sources (`inputs`, `lines`), spellable in function\n12: signatures, consumed exactly once, dying only at `collect` (reify spelled as a word) or at the\n13: top-level-only sink `jsonlines`, and never stored in a record, a `Vec`, or another `Stream`.\n14: This settles draft.md's Q1 as \"evaluation-level, but typed\": the silent streaming cliff becomes\n15: a checked property, and eager consumption of stdin gets a visible spelling, `collect(inputs)`.\n16: \n17: ## Considered options\n18: \n19: - First-class `Stream<T>` values, storable and nestable. Rejected: a held value of genuinely\n20:   unknown extent is exactly what Q13's lean rules out, and it is the one irreversible option --\n21:   once programs store streams, restricting back is a breaking change, while lifting a\n22:   second-class restriction later is additive.\n23: - Checker-internal streaming: surface signatures stay `Vec`-based and the checker classifies\n24:   which functions are stream-safe. Rejected because a `Vec -> Vec` signature silently meaning\n25:   two different things is the same implicitness the decision exists to remove.\n26: - Status quo, the recognizer as a silent optimization. Rejected: the silent eager fallback is\n27:   the defect, not an acceptable cost.\n28: - For `jsonlines`: keep the `Str` result (a type asserting the whole output exists as one\n29:   value -- a lie under streaming), or introduce an `Out`/unit type (answers Q35 as a side\n30:   effect, without the argument that question deserves). Top-level-only sink keeps Q35 open.\n31: \n32: ## Consequences\n33: \n34: - The linearity and containment rules generalize machinery the monomorphic `Lines` type\n35:   already had (single use, banned from records and Vecs, unprintable); `Lines` itself becomes\n36:   `Stream<Str>`.\n37: - `length` stays `Vec`-only, keeping its no-fold promise; stream reducers are future work.\n38: - `recognize_fusion`'s job shifts from guessing program shapes to reading types; an eager\n39:   fallback for a stream-typed program becomes a compiler bug rather than a silent behavior.\n40: - New rejection tests are required (nested `jsonlines`, twice-consumed stream, `Stream` in a\n41:   record), since the output-equality corpus cannot observe any of this.\n\n(End of file - total 41 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\nstatus: accepted\n---\n\n# Stream is the effect layer, typed\n\nThe fused `jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop made programs stream for real while the checker still\ntyped `inputs` as `Vec<T>` and `jsonlines(...)` as `Str`: whether a program streamed was a\nbackend-side pattern match, and a program one shape away from the pattern silently fell back to\nmaterializing all of stdin. We decided `Stream<T>` enters the surface type grammar as the type\nof effect-layer multiplicity: born only at sources (`inputs`, `lines`), spellable in function\nsignatures, consumed exactly once, dying only at `collect` (reify spelled as a word) or at the\ntop-level-only sink `jsonlines`, and never stored in a record, a `Vec`, or another `Stream`.\nThis settles draft.md's Q1 as \"evaluation-level, but typed\": the silent streaming cliff becomes\na checked property, and eager consumption of stdin gets a visible spelling, `collect(inputs)`.\n\n## Considered options\n\n- First-class `Stream<T>` values, storable and nestable. Rejected: a held value of genuinely\n  unknown extent is exactly what Q13's lean rules out, and it is the one irreversible option --","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/docs/adr/0001-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed.md","text":"---\nstatus: accepted\n---\n\n# Stream is the effect layer, typed\n\nThe fused `jsonlines(f(inputs))` loop made programs stream for real while the checker still\ntyped `inputs` as `Vec<T>` and `jsonlines(...)` as `Str`: whether a program streamed was a\nbackend-side pattern match, and a program one shape away from the pattern silently fell back to\nmaterializing all of stdin. We decided `Stream<T>` enters the surface type grammar as the type\nof effect-layer multiplicity: born only at sources (`inputs`, `lines`), spellable in function\nsignatures, consumed exactly once, dying only at `collect` (reify spelled as a word) or at the\ntop-level-only sink `jsonlines`, and never stored in a record, a `Vec`, or another `Stream`.\nThis settles draft.md's Q1 as \"evaluation-level, but typed\": the silent streaming cliff becomes\na checked property, and eager consumption of stdin gets a visible spelling, `collect(inputs)`.\n\n## Considered options\n\n- First-class `Stream<T>` values, storable and nestable. Rejected: a held value of genuinely\n  unknown extent is exactly what Q13's lean rules out, and it is the one irreversible option --\n  once programs store streams, restricting back is a breaking change, while lifting a\n  second-class restriction later is additive.\n- Checker-internal streaming: surface signatures stay `Vec`-based and the checker classifies\n  which functions are stream-safe. Rejected because a `Vec -> Vec` signature silently meaning\n  two different things is the same implicitness the decision exists to remove.\n- Status quo, the recognizer as a silent optimization. Rejected: the silent eager fallback is\n  the defect, not an acceptable cost.\n- For `jsonlines`: keep the `Str` result (a type asserting the whole output exists as one\n  value -- a lie under streaming), or introduce an `Out`/unit type (answers Q35 as a side\n  effect, without the argument that question deserves). Top-level-only sink keeps Q35 open.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- The linearity and containment rules generalize machinery the monomorphic `Lines` type\n  already had (single use, banned from records and Vecs, unprintable); `Lines` itself becomes\n  `Stream<Str>`.\n- `length` stays `Vec`-only, keeping its no-fold promise; stream reducers are future work.\n- `recognize_fusion`'s job shifts from guessing program shapes to reading types; an eager\n  fallback for a stream-typed program becomes a compiler bug rather than a silent behavior.\n- New rejection tests are required (nested `jsonlines`, twice-consumed stream, `Stream` in a\n  record), since the output-equality corpus cannot observe any of this.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":41,"totalLines":41,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/adr/0001-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed.md","time":{"start":1788129505053,"end":1788129505058}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now, Q35 is still open. But there's an important part: \"the streams decision gave the effect layer a type -- so the blockage above is gone\". Let me read the streams decision and ADR 0001 (stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed). Let me also read the streams.md plan.\n\nLet me look at the draft.md sections on streams, sinks, and the effect model.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_054d2ecec001HmOeWlWFEW5SvZ","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","messageID":"msg_054d2e3790016bEL8mrqd5N4dq"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788129505657,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-9d942f0db0a97073","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/streams.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/streams.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Typed streams: making the checker stop lying\n2: \n3: Implements draft.md's \"DECIDED: Stream is the effect layer, typed\" and ADR 0001. The design is\n4: settled; what follows is build order. The point of the whole feature, kept in view throughout:\n5: today the checker types `inputs` as `Vec<T>` and `jsonlines(...)` as `Str`, and whether a\n6: program streams is decided by `tir::recognize_fusion`'s structural guess -- a program one shape\n7: away from the pattern silently materializes all of stdin. At the end, the types say what\n8: streams, the eager spelling is a greppable `collect`, and a stream-typed program that fails to\n9: stream is a compiler bug rather than a silent behavior.\n10: \n11: The implementation template is `Lines`: the checker already has a monomorphic second-class\n12: stream type -- unprintable, banned from records and Vecs, unspellable in signatures, single-use,\n13: with `collect` as its one exit. Nearly every step below generalizes something `Lines` already\n14: does rather than inventing machinery. The genuinely new pieces are two: `Stream` becoming\n15: spellable (the one thing `Lines` deliberately withheld), and per-binding linearity (the\n16: `lines_used` flag is \"referenced at all, twice\" on one global; a stream-typed function\n17: parameter needs \"consumed exactly once\" per binding).\n18: \n19: ## Step 1: generalize Lines to Type::Stream, invisibly\n20: \n21: `Type::Lines` becomes `Type::Stream(Box<Type>)`; `lines` is born `Stream<Str>`; `collect`'s\n22: signature generalizes from `Lines -> Vec<Str>` to `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>` (checked the way the\n23: other polymorphic builtins are, argument synthesised first). The containment bans (record\n24: field, Vec element, printed result) and the mutual-exclusion and single-use checks carry over\n25: untouched. No surface syntax changes, no corpus case changes, every existing test stays green:\n26: this step is a refactor whose only observable is that error messages may now say \"a stream\"\n27: where they said `lines`.\n28: \n29: ## Step 2: make Stream spellable, and make it linear\n30: \n31: The type grammar accepts `Stream<T>` in function signatures. That un-does the load-bearing\n32: trick the `Lines` design recorded (\"a return annotation can never spell Lines\"), so what that\n33: trick guaranteed for free must now be checked for real:\n34: \n35: - A `Stream`-typed parameter is consumed exactly once in the function body. Zero uses is an\n36:   error (linear, not affine -- the decision's reversibility argument), two is the Python-\n37:   generator silent-empty-iterator mistake the single-use rule exists to prevent.\n38: - The containment bans apply to signature-spelled streams identically: `Vec<Stream<T>>` and a\n39:   `Stream`-typed record field are rejected in the type grammar itself, not just at value\n40:   construction sites.\n41: \n42: Rejection tests (step-style, insta): unused stream parameter, twice-consumed stream, `Stream`\n43: under a value constructor in an annotation, `Stream<Stream<T>>`. The corpus cannot see any of\n44: this; these snapshots are its only witness, the same blindness every checker feature hits.\n45: \n46: ## Step 3: mappers over streams\n47: \n48: `map`, `select`, and projection accept a `Stream` subject, yielding a `Stream` of the mapped\n49: element type -- the same subject-context mechanism that types them over `Vec` today, with the\n50: element drawn from `Stream`'s parameter instead. With this, `fn f(s: Stream<A>) -> Stream<B>`\n51: whose body is a pipeline over `s` checks end to end. A corpus case exercises a user-written\n52: stream-signature function on all seven backends (eagerly lowered is fine at this step; output\n53: is what the corpus can check).\n54: \n55: ## Step 4: retype inputs, and jsonlines becomes a sink\n56: \n57: `inputs` is born `Stream<T>`, element type inferred from use exactly as before. This is the\n58: breaking step, and the three affected corpus cases migrate to the honest spellings the\n59: decision records: the two mapper-shaped cases get `Stream` signatures, and the eager one\n60: becomes `total(collect(inputs))`. `extent` stays `Vec`-only -- its no-fold promise holds, and\n61: there are no stream reducers in this plan.\n62: \n63: `jsonlines` simultaneously stops having a result type: legal only as the program's outermost\n64: expression, accepting `Vec<T>` or `Stream<T>`. The placeholder `Str` typing dies, per the\n65: decision (\"a type claiming the whole output exists as one value\"). Rejection tests: a nested\n66: `jsonlines`, a program whose result is a bare unconsumed `Stream` (generalizing the \"contains\n67: `lines`, nothing to print\" error), `collect` of a `Vec`.\n68: \n69: ## Step 5: fusion reads types instead of guessing shapes\n70: \n71: `recognize_fusion`'s structural match retires. The backends compile a stream-typed pipeline\n72: ending in `jsonlines` as the fused read/transform/write loop they already know how to emit,\n73: driven by the types; a pipeline ending in `collect` materializes at the `collect` and stays\n74: eager past it. `lib.rs`'s `streams_inputs` gate becomes a type question. The invariant this\n75: step must land: for a stream-typed live program, the eager fallback path is unreachable --\n76: if the types say stream and no loop is emitted, that is a bug, not a behavior.\n77: \n78: `tests/streaming.rs` gains a liveness probe for a program the old recognizer would have\n79: rejected (a user-written `Stream -> Stream` function in the pipeline), which is the probe that\n80: proves the guess actually retired. The payoff corpus case pairs the features shipped this\n81: week: NDJSON of mixed messages, `inputs : Stream<SomeEnum>`, one exhaustive match inside\n82: `map`, fused, on all seven backends.\n83: \n84: ## What this plan does not include\n85: \n86: Stream reducers (a fold over a stream is real design work on `extent`'s no-fold promise);\n87: the pattern combinators of ADR 0008 (`Seq`/`Alt`/plus -- they wait for a source that produces\n88: a non-star pattern); Q35 (what stdout is -- the sink rule deliberately avoids answering);\n89: reflect (`.[]` as value-to-effect, still leaning \"does not exist\"); fan-out and concurrency\n90: (named non-goals of the pull decision). Each has its trigger recorded in the draft.\n\n(End of file - total 90 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Typed streams: making the checker stop lying\n\nImplements draft.md's \"DECIDED: Stream is the effect layer, typed\" and ADR 0001. The design is\nsettled; what follows is build order. The point of the whole feature, kept in view throughout:\ntoday the checker types `inputs` as `Vec<T>` and `jsonlines(...)` as `Str`, and whether a\nprogram streams is decided by `tir::recognize_fusion`'s structural guess -- a program one shape\naway from the pattern silently materializes all of stdin. At the end, the types say what\nstreams, the eager spelling is a greppable `collect`, and a stream-typed program that fails to\nstream is a compiler bug rather than a silent behavior.\n\nThe implementation template is `Lines`: the checker already has a monomorphic second-class\nstream type -- unprintable, banned from records and Vecs, unspellable in signatures, single-use,\nwith `collect` as its one exit. Nearly every step below generalizes something `Lines` already\ndoes rather than inventing machinery. The genuinely new pieces are two: `Stream` becoming\nspellable (the one thing `Lines` deliberately withheld), and per-binding linearity (the\n`lines_used` flag is \"referenced at all, twice\" on one global; a stream-typed function\nparameter needs \"consumed exactly once\" per binding).\n\n## Step 1: generalize Lines to Type::Stream, invisibly\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/plans/streams.md","text":"# Typed streams: making the checker stop lying\n\nImplements draft.md's \"DECIDED: Stream is the effect layer, typed\" and ADR 0001. The design is\nsettled; what follows is build order. The point of the whole feature, kept in view throughout:\ntoday the checker types `inputs` as `Vec<T>` and `jsonlines(...)` as `Str`, and whether a\nprogram streams is decided by `tir::recognize_fusion`'s structural guess -- a program one shape\naway from the pattern silently materializes all of stdin. At the end, the types say what\nstreams, the eager spelling is a greppable `collect`, and a stream-typed program that fails to\nstream is a compiler bug rather than a silent behavior.\n\nThe implementation template is `Lines`: the checker already has a monomorphic second-class\nstream type -- unprintable, banned from records and Vecs, unspellable in signatures, single-use,\nwith `collect` as its one exit. Nearly every step below generalizes something `Lines` already\ndoes rather than inventing machinery. The genuinely new pieces are two: `Stream` becoming\nspellable (the one thing `Lines` deliberately withheld), and per-binding linearity (the\n`lines_used` flag is \"referenced at all, twice\" on one global; a stream-typed function\nparameter needs \"consumed exactly once\" per binding).\n\n## Step 1: generalize Lines to Type::Stream, invisibly\n\n`Type::Lines` becomes `Type::Stream(Box<Type>)`; `lines` is born `Stream<Str>`; `collect`'s\nsignature generalizes from `Lines -> Vec<Str>` to `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>` (checked the way the\nother polymorphic builtins are, argument synthesised first). The containment bans (record\nfield, Vec element, printed result) and the mutual-exclusion and single-use checks carry over\nuntouched. No surface syntax changes, no corpus case changes, every existing test stays green:\nthis step is a refactor whose only observable is that error messages may now say \"a stream\"\nwhere they said `lines`.\n\n## Step 2: make Stream spellable, and make it linear\n\nThe type grammar accepts `Stream<T>` in function signatures. That un-does the load-bearing\ntrick the `Lines` design recorded (\"a return annotation can never spell Lines\"), so what that\ntrick guaranteed for free must now be checked for real:\n\n- A `Stream`-typed parameter is consumed exactly once in the function body. Zero uses is an\n  error (linear, not affine -- the decision's reversibility argument), two is the Python-\n  generator silent-empty-iterator mistake the single-use rule exists to prevent.\n- The containment bans apply to signature-spelled streams identically: `Vec<Stream<T>>` and a\n  `Stream`-typed record field are rejected in the type grammar itself, not just at value\n  construction sites.\n\nRejection tests (step-style, insta): unused stream parameter, twice-consumed stream, `Stream`\nunder a value constructor in an annotation, `Stream<Stream<T>>`. The corpus cannot see any of\nthis; these snapshots are its only witness, the same blindness every checker feature hits.\n\n## Step 3: mappers over streams\n\n`map`, `select`, and projection accept a `Stream` subject, yielding a `Stream` of the mapped\nelement type -- the same subject-context mechanism that types them over `Vec` today, with the\nelement drawn from `Stream`'s parameter instead. With this, `fn f(s: Stream<A>) -> Stream<B>`\nwhose body is a pipeline over `s` checks end to end. A corpus case exercises a user-written\nstream-signature function on all seven backends (eagerly lowered is fine at this step; output\nis what the corpus can check).\n\n## Step 4: retype inputs, and jsonlines becomes a sink\n\n`inputs` is born `Stream<T>`, element type inferred from use exactly as before. This is the\nbreaking step, and the three affected corpus cases migrate to the honest spellings the\ndecision records: the two mapper-shaped cases get `Stream` signatures, and the eager one\nbecomes `total(collect(inputs))`. `extent` stays `Vec`-only -- its no-fold promise holds, and\nthere are no stream reducers in this plan.\n\n`jsonlines` simultaneously stops having a result type: legal only as the program's outermost\nexpression, accepting `Vec<T>` or `Stream<T>`. The placeholder `Str` typing dies, per the\ndecision (\"a type claiming the whole output exists as one value\"). Rejection tests: a nested\n`jsonlines`, a program whose result is a bare unconsumed `Stream` (generalizing the \"contains\n`lines`, nothing to print\" error), `collect` of a `Vec`.\n\n## Step 5: fusion reads types instead of guessing shapes\n\n`recognize_fusion`'s structural match retires. The backends compile a stream-typed pipeline\nending in `jsonlines` as the fused read/transform/write loop they already know how to emit,\ndriven by the types; a pipeline ending in `collect` materializes at the `collect` and stays\neager past it. `lib.rs`'s `streams_inputs` gate becomes a type question. The invariant this\nstep must land: for a stream-typed live program, the eager fallback path is unreachable --\nif the types say stream and no loop is emitted, that is a bug, not a behavior.\n\n`tests/streaming.rs` gains a liveness probe for a program the old recognizer would have\nrejected (a user-written `Stream -> Stream` function in the pipeline), which is the probe that\nproves the guess actually retired. The payoff corpus case pairs the features shipped this\nweek: NDJSON of mixed messages, `inputs : Stream<SomeEnum>`, one exhaustive match inside\n`map`, fused, on all seven backends.\n\n## What this plan does not include\n\nStream reducers (a fold over a stream is real design work on `extent`'s no-fold promise);\nthe pattern combinators of ADR 0008 (`Seq`/`Alt`/plus -- they wait for a source that produces\na non-star pattern); Q35 (what stdout is -- the sink rule deliberately avoids answering);\nreflect (`.[]` as value-to-effect, still leaning \"does not exist\"); fan-out and concurrency\n(named non-goals of the pull decision). Each has its trigger recorded in the draft.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":90,"totalLines":90,"truncated":false}},"title":"plans/streams.md","time":{"start":1788129505650,"end":1788129505655}},"id":"prt_054d2ef1e001VCa6vGGZuDcD8X","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","messageID":"msg_054d2e3790016bEL8mrqd5N4dq"}}
