{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788129665262,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a806aed342b6e8f1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git log --oneline -30"},"output":"7c4cc86 Accept snapshots after merging issue-140\n08b0e56 Integrate issue-140: sum and max builtins over Vec<Int>/Vec<Int64> (gh:140 ruling)\n3b8c0d7 Add sum and max as builtin reductions\n920bd33 Integrate issue-135 and issue-141: the slow-fragment tier, js/py tail calls\nef15c7f Lower self-tail-calls to loops on js and py\nd6a91e5 Grow the docs harness a slow-fragment tier\nb8ad5b6 Integrate issue-133: restore the Euler 8/11/13/18 docs pages\nba9dff7 Restore the Euler 8/11/13/18 docs pages\nd9443ff Migrate draft.md's intro sections to a docs overview page\n411e55f Migrate draft.md's intro sections to an overview page\nddbbe5c Refuse a cycle between recursive-enum printers on jq\n6c70381 Board: dispatch 8 ready builds into the now-free lanes\n3483daa Board: draft.md is a shared footprint across every draft-split migration row\n4c63b6b Ticks notice decide starvation and compose grill rounds unprompted\n7091b2d Board: archive euler-data-problems-unblock (gh:129); add gh:132/gh:133 follow-ups\naa66d3a Merge issue-129: opt-in check for Euler 8/11/13/18 against real puzzle data\n940364c euler_real_data.rs: name the case tuple type (clippy type_complexity)\ne31986d Restore the executable bit os.replace dropped from drive-tick.sh\nbc13a59 Lane cap 5 -> 8, and decide rows stop crowding builds out of the ready set\nb2a5984 Research delegation: deep dives are worker work, ticks only route\n9c626e1 Opt-in check: run the Euler 8/11/13/18 programs against real puzzle data (gh:129)\n946aebf Board: archive builtin-renames (gh:98) as landed\nfec9ea0 Merge issue-98: rename extent -> length, unlines -> join_lines\nbac7313 Rename extent -> length, unlines -> join_lines (gh:98)\n2642909 drive-tick: say so when yielding the lock instead of exiting silently\n6fb7bce Worker escalation is a committed ESCALATION.md, not a GitHub issue\n1ca3a32 Board: csv-inputs-idea back to decide -- worker diagnosed a design gap, not a build\nfadf2a1 drive-tick: unbreak the prompt string -- a nested quote made <brief> a redirect\n04648db Board: mark csv-inputs-idea, euler-data-problems-unblock, builtin-renames delegated\nea1bb2e justfile: one-line drive comment so just --list reads right\n","metadata":{"output":"7c4cc86 Accept snapshots after merging issue-140\n08b0e56 Integrate issue-140: sum and max builtins over Vec<Int>/Vec<Int64> (gh:140 ruling)\n3b8c0d7 Add sum and max as builtin reductions\n920bd33 Integrate issue-135 and issue-141: the slow-fragment tier, js/py tail calls\nef15c7f Lower self-tail-calls to loops on js and py\nd6a91e5 Grow the docs harness a slow-fragment tier\nb8ad5b6 Integrate issue-133: restore the Euler 8/11/13/18 docs pages\nba9dff7 Restore the Euler 8/11/13/18 docs pages\nd9443ff Migrate draft.md's intro sections to a docs overview page\n411e55f Migrate draft.md's intro sections to an overview page\nddbbe5c Refuse a cycle between recursive-enum printers on jq\n6c70381 Board: dispatch 8 ready builds into the now-free lanes\n3483daa Board: draft.md is a shared footprint across every draft-split migration row\n4c63b6b Ticks notice decide starvation and compose grill rounds unprompted\n7091b2d Board: archive euler-data-problems-unblock (gh:129); add gh:132/gh:133 follow-ups\naa66d3a Merge issue-129: opt-in check for Euler 8/11/13/18 against real puzzle data\n940364c euler_real_data.rs: name the case tuple type (clippy type_complexity)\ne31986d Restore the executable bit os.replace dropped from drive-tick.sh\nbc13a59 Lane cap 5 -> 8, and decide rows stop crowding builds out of the ready set\nb2a5984 Research delegation: deep dives are worker work, ticks only route\n9c626e1 Opt-in check: run the Euler 8/11/13/18 programs against real puzzle data (gh:129)\n946aebf Board: archive builtin-renames (gh:98) as landed\nfec9ea0 Merge issue-98: rename extent -> length, unlines -> join_lines\nbac7313 Rename extent -> length, unlines -> join_lines (gh:98)\n2642909 drive-tick: say so when yielding the lock instead of exiting silently\n6fb7bce Worker escalation is a committed ESCALATION.md, not a GitHub issue\n1ca3a32 Board: csv-inputs-idea back to decide -- worker diagnosed a design gap, not a build\nfadf2a1 drive-tick: unbreak the prompt string -- a nested quote made <brief> a redirect\n04648db Board: mark csv-inputs-idea, euler-data-problems-unblock, builtin-renames delegated\nea1bb2e justfile: one-line drive comment so just --list reads right\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git log --oneline -30","time":{"start":1788129665257,"end":1788129665260}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Confirmed: the corpus requires all 7 backends to agree, or all refuse. There's no \"some backends run, some refuse\" option.\n\nNow the fundamental issue: jq cannot spawn subprocesses. So a `pipe_through` corpus case with `output` would require jq to run subprocesses too. This is a genuine design conflict.\n\nOptions:\n1. `pipe_through` corpus cases are `refuses: true` — meaning ALL backends refuse. But then it's not \"implemented\", it's just a refusal everywhere. That doesn't match \"Cover all backends\" meaningfully, and refuses means all refuse.\n2. Implement pipe_through on all backends including jq. jq cannot spawn processes. Unless... the \"subprocess\" could be faked. But that violates the spirit.\n3. The corpus case's program doesn't use `input`/`lines` — maybe the corpus case could use a `snapshot` approach.\n\nWait, actually — let me reconsider. Maybe the intended design is that `pipe_through` is NOT a builtin that runs in the corpus. Let me re-read the issue:\n\n\"Design and build a streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive for shell-out calls\"\n\nAnd the brief: \"Cover all backends and add corpus tests.\"\n\nHmm, \"Cover all backends\" could mean: implement the emission on all backends (each emits appropriate code), but jq... can't. OR it could mean the design doc should address all backends.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether jq can actually do subprocess. jq is a pure filter language with no syscalls. It truly cannot spawn processes.\n\nBut actually — hold on. Let me reconsider the whole approach. What if `pipe_through` is implemented as a **sink-side or stream transform that runs the subprocess on the HOST**? No, the generated code runs on each backend's runtime.\n\nHmm, but wait — for Lua, the backend runs embedded in-process via mlua (run_lua). The emitted Lua code calls `io.popen`? No, Lua can do `io.popen` in the standard library! Actually luajit / lua 5.x `io.popen` can run shell commands. mlua — does it support `io.popen`? Lua's `io.popen` is part of the standard library, mlua includes `io` library. So Lua CAN spawn a subprocess via `io.popen`.\n\nBut jq genuinely cannot. So the agreement harness would break: jq can't run a pipe_through program, and the corpus requires all 7 to agree on output.\n\nSo how do we satisfy \"add corpus tests\" + \"cover all backends\"?\n\nPossibility: The corpus case is `refuses: true`, and ALL backends refuse... no wait, if we implement pipe_through on 6 backends, then those 6 would RUN it (not refuse), and the harness would flag \"RAN lua ... instead of refusing\".\n\nSo that doesn't work either.\n\nLet me look again at the agreement harness. For Refusal, it checks `if let Ok(out) = toylang::run_on(...) { failures.push(\"RAN ... instead of refusing\") }`. So if 6 backends run it successfully and jq refuses, the harness reports 6 \"RAN\" failures.\n\nSo a `refuses: true` corpus case only works if ALL backends refuse, i.e., pipe_through is a checker-level refusal on every backend. That would be \"design the primitive but refuse everywhere\", which isn't building it.\n\nHmm. So there's a real tension. Let me think about what the maintainer intends.\n\nWait — maybe the answer is that this is a design+build task and the design decision (which is genuinely open, since Q35 is open and the board row is still `decide`) should be ESCALATED. The brief says: \"If something needs deciding that this brief does not settle, write ESCALATION.md at the worktree root -- the question, the alternatives, their costs -- COMMIT it on this branch, take the most conservative continuation, and keep going; never stop to wait for a human.\"\n\nThe corpus-vs-jq problem is exactly such a thing. But before escalating, let me check: is there really no way? Let me look at how jq runs and whether there's some `@sh` mechanism... Actually jq has `@sh` for shell-quoting strings but no exec.\n\nLet me also reconsider: maybe the corpus test for pipe_through uses `input` from a fixture and the program is `lines | pipe_through(...) | collect`, and the six backends that can run it produce the same output, but jq — the harness would report jq as \"BROKEN: could not run\". That fails the test.\n\nSo the corpus can't contain a pipe_through program that runs, given jq's constraint, unless we add per-backend support to the corpus harness (a new field like `skip: [jq]` or `runs_only: [...]`). But AGENTS.md says: \"Unknown keys, unknown backend names... are all errors.\" Adding a new key is a design change to the corpus format — significant.\n\nAlternatively, maybe jq CAN run a subprocess through a different route: jq's `env` builtin... no. `input` reads stdin only. There's no process spawn.\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider the whole thing. Maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me re-read the task brief once more:\n\n\"Build a streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive for shell-out calls per the gh:158 ruling: full streaming, e.g. lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [...]) | collect. Depends on Q35's stdout/stderr effect model -- check draft.md and the ADRs for that model before designing the primitive's signature. Cover all backends and add corpus tests.\"\n\nHmm \"Cover all backends\" — maybe the intent is: implement it on the backends that CAN (Lua, JS, Go, Py, Rust, Native/LLVM), and jq... Let me actually check what jq CAN do. Actually — jq CAN'T. But wait, is there a mechanism in the repo where a corpus case specifies which backends a program runs on? Let me grep the corpus cases and docs for any \"backends:\" or similar key.\n\nActually the Fields struct has `deny_unknown_fields`, so no. But maybe there's a different harness — `tests/backend_jq.rs` etc. Let me check those.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about what \"cover all backends\" realistically means and what the minimal-but-correct approach is.\n\nLet me look at the actual board/drive status. This is issue 158 on branch issue-158, assigned to me as a delegated worker. The board row `shell-out-design` is a `decide` row that hasn't been delegated. This looks like a coordination gap: the issue records a ruling but the design row hasn't advanced. However, my brief explicitly says to build per the ruling. The ruling is \"full streaming pipe-to-subprocess\" with shape `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"]) | collect`.\n\nGiven the conflict (corpus requires jq agreement), the honest options are:\n\nOption A: Implement pipe_through for all backends except jq, and add a corpus case that would break. Not viable without harness change.\n\nOption B: Implement for all backends except jq, add corpus cases with a new corpus key (e.g., `exclude: [jq]` or make refuses per-backend). This changes the corpus format — needs design decision + docs updates. This is a real design decision.\n\nOption C: Make jq refuse with a clear message (refuses: true) and... no, all must refuse then.\n\nOption D: Design-only: write the design (docs/ADR + reference page + questions.md update), and implement a checker-level refusal on all backends (\"pipe_through is designed but not yet implemented\") with `refuses: true` corpus cases. Then \"cover all backends\" = each backend has a defined refusal. This satisfies \"add corpus tests\" (refuses cases) and covers all backends (they all refuse uniformly). But it doesn't \"build\" the feature.\n\nHmm, but the brief says \"Definition of done: implementation complete\". So Option D doesn't satisfy.\n\nOption E: Implement on all backends that can spawn, and for jq implement via... hmm.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider. Can jq run a subprocess via its `@json`/... no. jq has no IO to processes. Definitely not.\n\nLet me look at how the tests handle backends that can't do something. Look at tests/backend_jq.rs and streaming.rs and jsonlines.rs. Maybe there's an established pattern for \"jq can't do X\". Let me also check whether there's any precedent for a builtin that jq refuses.\n\nActually, let me look at how `lines` works on jq — jq does read stdin. Fine.\n\nLet me look at git log to see the recent commit history and understand the state. Also, let me check if there's a design doc already partially written, like docs/reference/builtins/pipe_through.md.\n\nLet me search more broadly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_054d55f95001eQiL3H9RYVECVG","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","messageID":"msg_054d4a911001R1qZ1zqRVKB5Wg"}}
