{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788129678663,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-95fdd79a0d4a2ffc","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/prelude.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/prelude.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The rudimentary beginning of a module system: one file, `prelude.toy`, whose `pub`\n2: //! definitions are always available to every program. There is no import statement yet -- a\n3: //! program does not name what it wants, it just gets all of it -- and no way for a program's own\n4: //! file to be imported by another. What already exists: `pub fn` marks a definition as part of\n5: //! that always-available set; a non-`pub` one is parsed but never included in any compiled\n6: //! program, so it cannot yet serve as a private helper for a `pub` one either.\n7: //!\n8: //! `join_lines` used to be a `tir::Builtin`, needing its own codegen in all six backends. Written as\n9: //! ordinary toylang source instead, it is checked and compiled exactly the way a program's own\n10: //! functions are, so getting it right once in the checker is getting it right everywhere.\n11: //!\n12: //! Every `pub` definition is always merged in, whether the program uses it or not; what keeps an\n13: //! unused one from cluttering output and `tags::node_types` is `check::check`'s reachability\n14: //! pass, which prunes any function -- prelude or the program's own -- that the program's body\n15: //! never calls, directly or transitively.\n16: \n17: use crate::ast::Module;\n18: \n19: const PRELUDE_SRC: &str = include_str!(\"../prelude.toy\");\n20: \n21: // Defines `checked()`, constructing prelude.toy's already-checked `pub` functions directly\n22: // rather than parsing or type-checking anything: build.rs runs that once, against this same\n23: // source, and writes the result here as plain Rust (kantord/toylang#73).\n24: include!(concat!(env!(\"OUT_DIR\"), \"/prelude_checked.rs\"));\n25: \n26: /// Every `pub` declaration in `prelude.toy`.\n27: pub fn module() -> Module {\n28:     let module = crate::parse::parse_module(PRELUDE_SRC).expect(\"prelude.toy is valid toylang\");\n29:     Module {\n30:         defs: module.defs.into_iter().filter(|d| d.is_pub).collect(),\n31:         enums: module.enums.into_iter().filter(|e| e.is_pub).collect(),\n32:     }\n33: }\n34: \n35: /// Prepends the prelude's declarations to `file`, so a program can call any of them. Prepended\n36: /// rather than appended, so a program that redefines the same name is the one flagged as the\n37: /// duplicate -- its span is the one worth showing.\n38: pub fn inject(file: &mut crate::ast::File) {\n39:     let mut module = module();\n40:     module.defs.append(&mut file.defs);\n41:     file.defs = module.defs;\n42:     module.enums.append(&mut file.enums);\n43:     file.enums = module.enums;\n44: }\n\n(End of file - total 44 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The rudimentary beginning of a module system: one file, `prelude.toy`, whose `pub`\n//! definitions are always available to every program. There is no import statement yet -- a\n//! program does not name what it wants, it just gets all of it -- and no way for a program's own\n//! file to be imported by another. What already exists: `pub fn` marks a definition as part of\n//! that always-available set; a non-`pub` one is parsed but never included in any compiled\n//! program, so it cannot yet serve as a private helper for a `pub` one either.\n//!\n//! `join_lines` used to be a `tir::Builtin`, needing its own codegen in all six backends. Written as\n//! ordinary toylang source instead, it is checked and compiled exactly the way a program's own\n//! functions are, so getting it right once in the checker is getting it right everywhere.\n//!\n//! Every `pub` definition is always merged in, whether the program uses it or not; what keeps an\n//! unused one from cluttering output and `tags::node_types` is `check::check`'s reachability\n//! pass, which prunes any function -- prelude or the program's own -- that the program's body\n//! never calls, directly or transitively.\n\nuse crate::ast::Module;\n\nconst PRELUDE_SRC: &str = include_str!(\"../prelude.toy\");\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/prelude.rs","text":"//! The rudimentary beginning of a module system: one file, `prelude.toy`, whose `pub`\n//! definitions are always available to every program. There is no import statement yet -- a\n//! program does not name what it wants, it just gets all of it -- and no way for a program's own\n//! file to be imported by another. What already exists: `pub fn` marks a definition as part of\n//! that always-available set; a non-`pub` one is parsed but never included in any compiled\n//! program, so it cannot yet serve as a private helper for a `pub` one either.\n//!\n//! `join_lines` used to be a `tir::Builtin`, needing its own codegen in all six backends. Written as\n//! ordinary toylang source instead, it is checked and compiled exactly the way a program's own\n//! functions are, so getting it right once in the checker is getting it right everywhere.\n//!\n//! Every `pub` definition is always merged in, whether the program uses it or not; what keeps an\n//! unused one from cluttering output and `tags::node_types` is `check::check`'s reachability\n//! pass, which prunes any function -- prelude or the program's own -- that the program's body\n//! never calls, directly or transitively.\n\nuse crate::ast::Module;\n\nconst PRELUDE_SRC: &str = include_str!(\"../prelude.toy\");\n\n// Defines `checked()`, constructing prelude.toy's already-checked `pub` functions directly\n// rather than parsing or type-checking anything: build.rs runs that once, against this same\n// source, and writes the result here as plain Rust (kantord/toylang#73).\ninclude!(concat!(env!(\"OUT_DIR\"), \"/prelude_checked.rs\"));\n\n/// Every `pub` declaration in `prelude.toy`.\npub fn module() -> Module {\n    let module = crate::parse::parse_module(PRELUDE_SRC).expect(\"prelude.toy is valid toylang\");\n    Module {\n        defs: module.defs.into_iter().filter(|d| d.is_pub).collect(),\n        enums: module.enums.into_iter().filter(|e| e.is_pub).collect(),\n    }\n}\n\n/// Prepends the prelude's declarations to `file`, so a program can call any of them. Prepended\n/// rather than appended, so a program that redefines the same name is the one flagged as the\n/// duplicate -- its span is the one worth showing.\npub fn inject(file: &mut crate::ast::File) {\n    let mut module = module();\n    module.defs.append(&mut file.defs);\n    file.defs = module.defs;\n    module.enums.append(&mut file.enums);\n    file.enums = module.enums;\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":44,"totalLines":44,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/prelude.rs","time":{"start":1788129678655,"end":1788129678661}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"There's no prior design work on pipe_through. The most recent similar work is issue-140 (sum/max builtins). Let me study how issue-140 was implemented, since it's the closest precedent: it added two builtins (Sum, Max), with a \"ruling\" recorded in gh:140, and the work was \"Integrate issue-140\". Let me look at that commit to see the full shape of adding a builtin.\n\nLet me look at:\n1. The diff of commit 08b0e56 (integrate issue-140) — how builtins were added across checker + backends + docs\n2. prelude.rs — how builtins are declared\n3. check/mod.rs — how builtins are typed, the sink special-casing\n4. emit_*.rs — how builtins are emitted\n\nThis is a big task. Let me look at the issue-140 integration to see the pattern, and read the core checker/emit files. Given size, let me read check/mod.rs fully (it's large) and prelude.rs.\n\nActually, before diving into implementation details, I need to resolve the fundamental design questions:\n\n1. **Typing**: What's the signature of `pipe_through`? `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"]) | collect` — pipe_through takes 2 args (Str program, Vec<Str> args) and maps Stream<Str> -> Stream<Str>. But toylang builtins are unary! \"Each is unary, and so is every user function: something wanting two arguments takes a record.\" So `pipe_through(program, args)` — how does a 2-arg call parse? Let me check how calls with 2 args parse. Actually, in the syntax `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` might parse as... hmm, two positional args? Let me check the parser.\n\nActually wait — the syntax in the issue is `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` — comma-separated args. Does toylang support multi-arg calls? Let me check the parse.rs and the bare_application docs.\n\n2. **Stream vs Vec**: pipe_through operates on the piped value. In `lines | pipe_through(...)`, the left side is `lines` = Stream<Str>. So pipe_through takes a Stream and produces a Stream. But could it also work on Vec? Design question.\n\n3. **jq problem**: the corpus requires all backends to agree. jq can't spawn. Need a decision.\n\nLet me examine how the syntax for multi-arg calls works. Let me read bare_application tests and the syntax reference.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_054d59317001Z23SlRuXXgZX3W","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","messageID":"msg_054d56588001GCjtCg7ZkkVIaU"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788129678932,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-9159cc5698dc37e1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/docs/reference/builtins/collect.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/docs/reference/builtins/collect.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # collect\n2: \n3: `collect(s)`, of type `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`: the one place a stream stops being a stream.\n4: What comes back is an ordinary value, exactly as sized as it needs to be, with no trace of\n5: how it arrived.\n6: \n7: A `Stream` is born at a source (`inputs`, `lines`), consumed exactly once, and cannot be\n8: printed, stored in a record, or indexed; `collect` is the explicit boundary where it becomes\n9: a `Vec` that can do all of those things. The cost is equally explicit: `collect` waits for\n10: the whole stream, so nothing downstream of it starts until stdin closes.\n11: \n12: ```toylang\n13: join_lines(collect(lines))\n14: ```\n15: \n16: ```input\n17: ada\n18: bo\n19: cy\n20: ```\n21: \n22: ```output\n23: ada\n24: bo\n25: cy\n26: ```\n27: \n28: Counting a stream is the shape that shows why the boundary is spelled out: the count cannot\n29: exist until everything has been read.\n30: \n31: ```toylang\n32: fn total(nums: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(nums)\n33: \n34: total(collect(inputs))\n35: ```\n36: \n37: ```input\n38: 1\n39: 2\n40: 3\n41: 4\n42: ```\n43: \n44: ```output\n45: 4\n46: ```\n47: \n48: A pipeline that never needs the whole value at once should stay a stream and end at a sink\n49: ([`jsonlines`](jsonlines.md)) instead: that shape processes one entry at a time, printing as\n50: it goes.\n\n(End of file - total 50 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# collect\n\n`collect(s)`, of type `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`: the one place a stream stops being a stream.\nWhat comes back is an ordinary value, exactly as sized as it needs to be, with no trace of\nhow it arrived.\n\nA `Stream` is born at a source (`inputs`, `lines`), consumed exactly once, and cannot be\nprinted, stored in a record, or indexed; `collect` is the explicit boundary where it becomes\na `Vec` that can do all of those things. The cost is equally explicit: `collect` waits for\nthe whole stream, so nothing downstream of it starts until stdin closes.\n\n```toylang\njoin_lines(collect(lines))\n```\n\n```input\nada\nbo\ncy\n```","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/docs/reference/builtins/collect.md","text":"# collect\n\n`collect(s)`, of type `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`: the one place a stream stops being a stream.\nWhat comes back is an ordinary value, exactly as sized as it needs to be, with no trace of\nhow it arrived.\n\nA `Stream` is born at a source (`inputs`, `lines`), consumed exactly once, and cannot be\nprinted, stored in a record, or indexed; `collect` is the explicit boundary where it becomes\na `Vec` that can do all of those things. The cost is equally explicit: `collect` waits for\nthe whole stream, so nothing downstream of it starts until stdin closes.\n\n```toylang\njoin_lines(collect(lines))\n```\n\n```input\nada\nbo\ncy\n```\n\n```output\nada\nbo\ncy\n```\n\nCounting a stream is the shape that shows why the boundary is spelled out: the count cannot\nexist until everything has been read.\n\n```toylang\nfn total(nums: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(nums)\n\ntotal(collect(inputs))\n```\n\n```input\n1\n2\n3\n4\n```\n\n```output\n4\n```\n\nA pipeline that never needs the whole value at once should stay a stream and end at a sink\n([`jsonlines`](jsonlines.md)) instead: that shape processes one entry at a time, printing as\nit goes.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":50,"totalLines":50,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/reference/builtins/collect.md","time":{"start":1788129678918,"end":1788129678930}},"id":"prt_054d59541001xVeQpJIe299GFE","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","messageID":"msg_054d56588001GCjtCg7ZkkVIaU"}}
