{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788129701913,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-963be49019178590","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/docs/reference/operators/pipe.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/docs/reference/operators/pipe.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # The pipe\n2: \n3: `x | f` feeds the left side to the right side, and inside the right side `.` is the value\n4: that arrived. Stages chain left to right, so a transformation reads in the order it\n5: happens:\n6: \n7: ```toylang\n8: [1, 2, 3] | select(. >= 2) | map(. * 10)\n9: ```\n10: \n11: ```output\n12: [20,30]\n13: ```\n14: \n15: Three forms take their subject from the pipe rather than from an argument:\n16: [`select`](../builtins/select.md), [`map`](../builtins/map.md), and the\n17: [match](match.md)'s arm chain. Everything else -- `length`, `str`, a user function -- takes\n18: its data as an ordinary argument, and inside a pipe stage is applied to whatever expression\n19: mentions `.`:\n20: \n21: ```toylang\n22: [10, 20, 30][1]! | str(.)\n23: ```\n24: \n25: ```output\n26: 20\n27: ```\n28: \n29: `.` rebinds at each boundary that introduces a subject: a pipe stage, a `map` or `select`\n30: body (where it is the current entry), a match arm over a bare payload (where it is the\n31: payload). A projection like `.age` inside those positions reaches into whatever `.`\n32: currently is.\n33: \n34: Piping is plumbing, not effect: on a `Vec` everything is ordinary values, and on a\n35: [`Stream`](../types/stream.md) the same spelling stays one fused chain from source to sink.\n\n(End of file - total 35 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# The pipe\n\n`x | f` feeds the left side to the right side, and inside the right side `.` is the value\nthat arrived. Stages chain left to right, so a transformation reads in the order it\nhappens:\n\n```toylang\n[1, 2, 3] | select(. >= 2) | map(. * 10)\n```\n\n```output\n[20,30]\n```\n\nThree forms take their subject from the pipe rather than from an argument:\n[`select`](../builtins/select.md), [`map`](../builtins/map.md), and the\n[match](match.md)'s arm chain. Everything else -- `length`, `str`, a user function -- takes\nits data as an ordinary argument, and inside a pipe stage is applied to whatever expression\nmentions `.`:\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/docs/reference/operators/pipe.md","text":"# The pipe\n\n`x | f` feeds the left side to the right side, and inside the right side `.` is the value\nthat arrived. Stages chain left to right, so a transformation reads in the order it\nhappens:\n\n```toylang\n[1, 2, 3] | select(. >= 2) | map(. * 10)\n```\n\n```output\n[20,30]\n```\n\nThree forms take their subject from the pipe rather than from an argument:\n[`select`](../builtins/select.md), [`map`](../builtins/map.md), and the\n[match](match.md)'s arm chain. Everything else -- `length`, `str`, a user function -- takes\nits data as an ordinary argument, and inside a pipe stage is applied to whatever expression\nmentions `.`:\n\n```toylang\n[10, 20, 30][1]! | str(.)\n```\n\n```output\n20\n```\n\n`.` rebinds at each boundary that introduces a subject: a pipe stage, a `map` or `select`\nbody (where it is the current entry), a match arm over a bare payload (where it is the\npayload). A projection like `.age` inside those positions reaches into whatever `.`\ncurrently is.\n\nPiping is plumbing, not effect: on a `Vec` everything is ordinary values, and on a\n[`Stream`](../types/stream.md) the same spelling stays one fused chain from source to sink.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":35,"totalLines":35,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/reference/operators/pipe.md","time":{"start":1788129701904,"end":1788129701910}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_054d5ef24001YLedI9JTGkO5Mn","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","messageID":"msg_054d5e89b001r3mOi8hrzy1X7z"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788129702603,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a8a78e1f5ab7bd7c","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/docs/reference/syntax/functions.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/docs/reference/syntax/functions.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Functions\n2: \n3: `fn name(param: Type) -> Type = body`. A function takes at most one parameter and returns one\n4: result, and its body is one expression. Named functions declare their types fully; nothing\n5: about a signature is inferred.\n6: \n7: ```toylang\n8: # fmt: syntax-example\n9: fn double(x: Int) -> Int = x * 2\n10: \n11: double 21\n12: ```\n13: \n14: ```output\n15: 42\n16: ```\n17: \n18: A function may also take no parameter, written `fn name() -> Type = body` and called\n19: `name()`:\n20: \n21: ```toylang\n22: fn greeting() -> Str = \"hello\"\n23: \n24: greeting()\n25: ```\n26: \n27: ```output\n28: hello\n29: ```\n30: \n31: Several things travel as one record, and a record-literal argument may drop its parens, so\n32: `area {w: 3, h: 4}` reads as named arguments:\n33: \n34: ```case\n35: call_without_parens\n36: ```\n37: \n38: Functions can call forward and can recurse; signatures are collected before any body is\n39: checked:\n40: \n41: ```case\n42: forward_reference\n43: ```\n44: \n45: A real cycle between two or more named functions runs on six of the seven backends. jq is the\n46: exception: its `def` sees only itself and whatever is already defined above it, with no forward\n47: declaration to bridge a cycle, so `toylang build`/`emit` refuses cleanly for that target rather\n48: than emitting jq source that would fail to compile.\n49: \n50: What a signature cannot say: a `Stream` result without a `Stream` parameter (a stream is\n51: born only at a source; see [Stream](../types/stream.md)). A function is not a value -- it\n52: cannot be stored, passed, or returned -- and the nine [builtin names](../builtins/str.md)\n53: cannot be redefined.\n54: \n55: Bare application, `f x`, is the default call form for a function that takes one argument;\n56: since a function is never variadic, parens never said which argument is which -- only where\n57: the argument starts and ends -- and `f(x)` is the same call with the argument grouped. Chains\n58: read right-to-left: `str double 21` is `str(double(21))`. Reach for the parens when the bare\n59: form would read differently: `-` starts subtraction rather than an argument (`f -1` is\n60: `f - 1`), and `.` and `[` bind tighter as [projection](../operators/projection.md) and\n61: indexing, so a projection or Vec-literal argument is spelled `map(.name)` or `some([4, 5])`.\n62: An argument must also start on the same line as its function; to call across lines, use the\n63: parens. A nullary function has no bare form -- `name` alone is a reference the checker would\n64: have to disambiguate from a call -- so it is always called `name()`.\n\n(End of file - total 64 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Functions\n\n`fn name(param: Type) -> Type = body`. A function takes at most one parameter and returns one\nresult, and its body is one expression. Named functions declare their types fully; nothing\nabout a signature is inferred.\n\n```toylang\n# fmt: syntax-example\nfn double(x: Int) -> Int = x * 2\n\ndouble 21\n```\n\n```output\n42\n```\n\nA function may also take no parameter, written `fn name() -> Type = body` and called\n`name()`:\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/docs/reference/syntax/functions.md","text":"# Functions\n\n`fn name(param: Type) -> Type = body`. A function takes at most one parameter and returns one\nresult, and its body is one expression. Named functions declare their types fully; nothing\nabout a signature is inferred.\n\n```toylang\n# fmt: syntax-example\nfn double(x: Int) -> Int = x * 2\n\ndouble 21\n```\n\n```output\n42\n```\n\nA function may also take no parameter, written `fn name() -> Type = body` and called\n`name()`:\n\n```toylang\nfn greeting() -> Str = \"hello\"\n\ngreeting()\n```\n\n```output\nhello\n```\n\nSeveral things travel as one record, and a record-literal argument may drop its parens, so\n`area {w: 3, h: 4}` reads as named arguments:\n\n```case\ncall_without_parens\n```\n\nFunctions can call forward and can recurse; signatures are collected before any body is\nchecked:\n\n```case\nforward_reference\n```\n\nA real cycle between two or more named functions runs on six of the seven backends. jq is the\nexception: its `def` sees only itself and whatever is already defined above it, with no forward\ndeclaration to bridge a cycle, so `toylang build`/`emit` refuses cleanly for that target rather\nthan emitting jq source that would fail to compile.\n\nWhat a signature cannot say: a `Stream` result without a `Stream` parameter (a stream is\nborn only at a source; see [Stream](../types/stream.md)). A function is not a value -- it\ncannot be stored, passed, or returned -- and the nine [builtin names](../builtins/str.md)\ncannot be redefined.\n\nBare application, `f x`, is the default call form for a function that takes one argument;\nsince a function is never variadic, parens never said which argument is which -- only where\nthe argument starts and ends -- and `f(x)` is the same call with the argument grouped. Chains\nread right-to-left: `str double 21` is `str(double(21))`. Reach for the parens when the bare\nform would read differently: `-` starts subtraction rather than an argument (`f -1` is\n`f - 1`), and `.` and `[` bind tighter as [projection](../operators/projection.md) and\nindexing, so a projection or Vec-literal argument is spelled `map(.name)` or `some([4, 5])`.\nAn argument must also start on the same line as its function; to call across lines, use the\nparens. A nullary function has no bare form -- `name` alone is a reference the checker would\nhave to disambiguate from a call -- so it is always called `name()`.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":64,"totalLines":64,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/reference/syntax/functions.md","time":{"start":1788129702588,"end":1788129702600}},"id":"prt_054d5f0120016owXHwHvkMD26D","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","messageID":"msg_054d5e89b001r3mOi8hrzy1X7z"}}
