{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788129750425,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-bfe9cfe799d05ba0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/check/mod.rs","limit":120},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/check/mod.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: use std::cell::{Cell, RefCell};\n2: use std::collections::{HashMap, HashSet};\n3: \n4: use crate::ast::{BinOp, Expr, FieldsPattern, File, MatchArm, Param, Pattern, Span};\n5: use crate::error::Error;\n6: use crate::tir::{self, Kind, LocalId, Tir};\n7: use crate::ty::{self, Sig, Type};\n8: \n9: mod linearity;\n10: mod types;\n11: \n12: use linearity::{StreamBinding, check_linear, field_used, param_used, prune_unreachable};\n13: use types::{TypeEnv, alias_map, enum_map, resolve, resolve_enum, signatures};\n14: \n15: struct Ctx<'a> {\n16:     sigs: &'a HashMap<String, Sig>,\n17:     /// Every declared enum, resolved. Keyed by name, which is the identity.\n18:     enums: &'a HashMap<String, Type>,\n19:     /// Which enums declare each variant name, in declaration order. A bare variant use resolves\n20:     /// through this while exactly one enum claims the name; two claimants are a loud error\n21:     /// naming both, with `Shape.circle` as the qualified way out.\n22:     variant_owners: &'a HashMap<String, Vec<String>>,\n23:     /// Named bindings. At most one, since functions are unary and there is no `let`.\n24:     scope: Vec<(String, Type)>,\n25:     /// Names a match arm's record pattern bound, innermost last: reading one reads that field\n26:     /// off the arm's payload local, so nothing beyond ordinary `Field` nodes reaches the\n27:     /// backends. Each entry is the bound name, the payload's record type, and its local.\n28:     arm_fields: Vec<(String, Type, LocalId)>,\n29:     /// What `.` refers to here, if anything: its type and the local holding it.\n30:     subject: Option<(Type, LocalId)>,\n31:     /// The type `input` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used.\n32:     input: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n33:     /// The element type `inputs` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used. A\n34:     /// separate cell from `input`'s: one records a whole value's type, the other one line's.\n35:     inputs: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n36:     /// Whether `lines` has already been read. There is only ever one real stdin, so a second\n37:     /// read is refused rather than silently handed nothing, the way a second pass over an\n38:     /// already-consumed iterator would be in a language that let it compile.\n39:     lines_used: &'a Cell<bool>,\n40:     /// Whether checking is inside a mapper's body (`map`'s, or `select`'s predicate), which\n41:     /// runs once per element: a source read there would drain stdin on the first element and\n42:     /// hand every later one nothing, so `lines` and `inputs` are refused in that position.\n43:     in_mapper: bool,\n44:     /// The name of the `fn` whose body is being checked; `None` in the program's own body. A\n45:     /// source is legal only in the program body (see `source_in_fn`), so `lines` and `inputs`\n46:     /// are refused whenever this is set.\n47:     in_fn: Option<&'a str>,\n48:     next_local: &'a Cell<LocalId>,\n49: }\n50: \n51: impl Ctx<'_> {\n52:     fn with(&self, subject: Option<(Type, LocalId)>) -> Ctx<'_> {\n53:         Ctx {\n54:             sigs: self.sigs,\n55:             enums: self.enums,\n56:             variant_owners: self.variant_owners,\n57:             scope: self.scope.clone(),\n58:             arm_fields: self.arm_fields.clone(),\n59:             subject,\n60:             input: self.input,\n61:             inputs: self.inputs,\n62:             lines_used: self.lines_used,\n63:             in_mapper: self.in_mapper,\n64:             in_fn: self.in_fn,\n65:             next_local: self.next_local,\n66:         }\n67:     }\n68: \n69:     fn fresh(&self) -> LocalId {\n70:         let id = self.next_local.get();\n71:         self.next_local.set(id + 1);\n72:         id\n73:     }\n74: }\n75: \n76: pub fn check(file: &File) -> Result<tir::Program, Error> {\n77:     let lines_used = Cell::new(false);\n78:     let aliases = alias_map(&file.aliases)?;\n79:     let env = TypeEnv {\n80:         aliases,\n81:         enums: enum_map(&file.enums)?,\n82:     };\n83:     for e in &file.enums {\n84:         if env.aliases.contains_key(&e.name) {\n85:             return Err(Error::new(\n86:                 e.span,\n87:                 format!(\"type `{}` is defined twice\", e.name),\n88:             ));\n89:         }\n90:     }\n91:     // Resolved eagerly so a broken declaration is an error even when nothing uses it, and so a\n92:     // cycle is found here rather than wherever it happened to be reached from.\n93:     for a in &file.aliases {\n94:         resolve(&a.ty, &env, &mut vec![(a.name.clone(), Vec::new())])?;\n95:     }\n96:     let mut enums: HashMap<String, Type> = HashMap::new();\n97:     for e in &file.enums {\n98:         enums.insert(\n99:             e.name.clone(),\n100:             resolve_enum(e, &env, &mut Vec::new(), None)?,\n101:         );\n102:     }\n103:     let mut variant_owners: HashMap<String, Vec<String>> = HashMap::new();\n104:     for e in &file.enums {\n105:         for v in &e.variants {\n106:             variant_owners\n107:                 .entry(v.name.clone())\n108:                 .or_default()\n109:                 .push(e.name.clone());\n110:         }\n111:     }\n112:     let sigs = signatures(&file.defs, &env)?;\n113:     let input = RefCell::new(None);\n114:     let inputs = RefCell::new(None);\n115:     let next_local = Cell::new(0);\n116:     let ctx = Ctx {\n117:         sigs: &sigs,\n118:         enums: &enums,\n119:         variant_owners: &variant_owners,\n120:         scope: Vec::new(),\n\n(Showing lines 1-120 of 2828. Use offset=121 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"use std::cell::{Cell, RefCell};\nuse std::collections::{HashMap, HashSet};\n\nuse crate::ast::{BinOp, Expr, FieldsPattern, File, MatchArm, Param, Pattern, Span};\nuse crate::error::Error;\nuse crate::tir::{self, Kind, LocalId, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Sig, Type};\n\nmod linearity;\nmod types;\n\nuse linearity::{StreamBinding, check_linear, field_used, param_used, prune_unreachable};\nuse types::{TypeEnv, alias_map, enum_map, resolve, resolve_enum, signatures};\n\nstruct Ctx<'a> {\n    sigs: &'a HashMap<String, Sig>,\n    /// Every declared enum, resolved. Keyed by name, which is the identity.\n    enums: &'a HashMap<String, Type>,\n    /// Which enums declare each variant name, in declaration order. A bare variant use resolves\n    /// through this while exactly one enum claims the name; two claimants are a loud error","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/check/mod.rs","text":"use std::cell::{Cell, RefCell};\nuse std::collections::{HashMap, HashSet};\n\nuse crate::ast::{BinOp, Expr, FieldsPattern, File, MatchArm, Param, Pattern, Span};\nuse crate::error::Error;\nuse crate::tir::{self, Kind, LocalId, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Sig, Type};\n\nmod linearity;\nmod types;\n\nuse linearity::{StreamBinding, check_linear, field_used, param_used, prune_unreachable};\nuse types::{TypeEnv, alias_map, enum_map, resolve, resolve_enum, signatures};\n\nstruct Ctx<'a> {\n    sigs: &'a HashMap<String, Sig>,\n    /// Every declared enum, resolved. Keyed by name, which is the identity.\n    enums: &'a HashMap<String, Type>,\n    /// Which enums declare each variant name, in declaration order. A bare variant use resolves\n    /// through this while exactly one enum claims the name; two claimants are a loud error\n    /// naming both, with `Shape.circle` as the qualified way out.\n    variant_owners: &'a HashMap<String, Vec<String>>,\n    /// Named bindings. At most one, since functions are unary and there is no `let`.\n    scope: Vec<(String, Type)>,\n    /// Names a match arm's record pattern bound, innermost last: reading one reads that field\n    /// off the arm's payload local, so nothing beyond ordinary `Field` nodes reaches the\n    /// backends. Each entry is the bound name, the payload's record type, and its local.\n    arm_fields: Vec<(String, Type, LocalId)>,\n    /// What `.` refers to here, if anything: its type and the local holding it.\n    subject: Option<(Type, LocalId)>,\n    /// The type `input` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used.\n    input: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n    /// The element type `inputs` was checked against, filled in the first time it is used. A\n    /// separate cell from `input`'s: one records a whole value's type, the other one line's.\n    inputs: &'a RefCell<Option<Type>>,\n    /// Whether `lines` has already been read. There is only ever one real stdin, so a second\n    /// read is refused rather than silently handed nothing, the way a second pass over an\n    /// already-consumed iterator would be in a language that let it compile.\n    lines_used: &'a Cell<bool>,\n    /// Whether checking is inside a mapper's body (`map`'s, or `select`'s predicate), which\n    /// runs once per element: a source read there would drain stdin on the first element and\n    /// hand every later one nothing, so `lines` and `inputs` are refused in that position.\n    in_mapper: bool,\n    /// The name of the `fn` whose body is being checked; `None` in the program's own body. A\n    /// source is legal only in the program body (see `source_in_fn`), so `lines` and `inputs`\n    /// are refused whenever this is set.\n    in_fn: Option<&'a str>,\n    next_local: &'a Cell<LocalId>,\n}\n\nimpl Ctx<'_> {\n    fn with(&self, subject: Option<(Type, LocalId)>) -> Ctx<'_> {\n        Ctx {\n            sigs: self.sigs,\n            enums: self.enums,\n            variant_owners: self.variant_owners,\n            scope: self.scope.clone(),\n            arm_fields: self.arm_fields.clone(),\n            subject,\n            input: self.input,\n            inputs: self.inputs,\n            lines_used: self.lines_used,\n            in_mapper: self.in_mapper,\n            in_fn: self.in_fn,\n            next_local: self.next_local,\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn fresh(&self) -> LocalId {\n        let id = self.next_local.get();\n        self.next_local.set(id + 1);\n        id\n    }\n}\n\npub fn check(file: &File) -> Result<tir::Program, Error> {\n    let lines_used = Cell::new(false);\n    let aliases = alias_map(&file.aliases)?;\n    let env = TypeEnv {\n        aliases,\n        enums: enum_map(&file.enums)?,\n    };\n    for e in &file.enums {\n        if env.aliases.contains_key(&e.name) {\n            return Err(Error::new(\n                e.span,\n                format!(\"type `{}` is defined twice\", e.name),\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n    // Resolved eagerly so a broken declaration is an error even when nothing uses it, and so a\n    // cycle is found here rather than wherever it happened to be reached from.\n    for a in &file.aliases {\n        resolve(&a.ty, &env, &mut vec![(a.name.clone(), Vec::new())])?;\n    }\n    let mut enums: HashMap<String, Type> = HashMap::new();\n    for e in &file.enums {\n        enums.insert(\n            e.name.clone(),\n            resolve_enum(e, &env, &mut Vec::new(), None)?,\n        );\n    }\n    let mut variant_owners: HashMap<String, Vec<String>> = HashMap::new();\n    for e in &file.enums {\n        for v in &e.variants {\n            variant_owners\n                .entry(v.name.clone())\n                .or_default()\n                .push(e.name.clone());\n        }\n    }\n    let sigs = signatures(&file.defs, &env)?;\n    let input = RefCell::new(None);\n    let inputs = RefCell::new(None);\n    let next_local = Cell::new(0);\n    let ctx = Ctx {\n        sigs: &sigs,\n        enums: &enums,\n        variant_owners: &variant_owners,\n        scope: Vec::new(),","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":120,"totalLines":2828,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/check/mod.rs","time":{"start":1788129750418,"end":1788129750424}},"id":"prt_054d6ab550015Vqj3lW2zm9LcV","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","messageID":"msg_054d5f2fb001CD8CkBauGZrn1i"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788130091578,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-888c3f683aef950c","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Map emitter architecture","prompt":"I'm working in the toylang compiler repo at /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158. I need a detailed map of how the seven code-emitting backends are structured, so I can add a new builtin (`pipe_through`) across all of them.\n\nPlease explore these files thoroughly (medium thoroughness):\n- src/emit_jq.rs\n- src/emit_py.rs\n- src/emit_lua.rs\n- src/emit_js.rs\n- src/emit_go.rs\n- src/emit_rs.rs\n- src/emit_llvm.rs\n\nFor EACH backend file, report:\n1. How the `Builtin` enum variants are dispatched in the emitter (the function/match arm that handles `Kind::Builtin { which, arg }`). Quote the exact arm for one simple builtin like `Length` or `Collect` and for `JsonLines`.\n2. How the fused jsonlines streaming loop is emitted (the function that emits read-one/transform-one/write-one over `tir::fusion` / `Source::Inputs` / `Source::Lines`). Quote the signature and roughly how the per-element stages (Map/Select) are emitted.\n3. Whether the backend has any existing notion of spawning subprocesses or running external commands (search for \"subprocess\", \"process\", \"Command\", \"popen\", \"spawn\", \"exec\").\n4. For emit_jq.rs specifically: is there any way it could invoke an external program? What does it do for `lines` / raw input? Note anything about `@sh`, `env`, or `input`.\n5. How `lines` is emitted on each backend (the loop that reads stdin lines).\n\nAlso check src/emit_toylang.rs briefly: how does the formatter/emitter handle `Kind::Builtin` and how are builtins printed back to source? Report the function that renders a Builtin call and quote the arm for JsonLines and one simple builtin.\n\nDo NOT modify any files. Report findings as a structured summary keyed by backend. Be precise with file:line references and quote the relevant code snippets. This is research only.","subagent_type":"explore"},"output":"<task id=\"ses_fab2954b5fferk5Plr5jfoZr2B\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\nI now have everything needed. Here is the structured research summary.\n\n---\n\n# Builtin dispatch and fused jsonlines loops across the seven emitters\n\nAll paths are under `/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/`. The TIR shapes they all dispatch on are defined in `tir.rs`: `Kind::Builtin { which, arg }` (`tir.rs:135`), `enum Builtin` (`tir.rs:182`, with `JsonLines` at 202, `Collect` at 196, `Length` at 205), `enum Stage { Map, Select }` (`tir.rs:298`), `enum Source { Inputs, Lines }` (`tir.rs:305`), and `struct Fusion<'a> { source, stages }` produced by `tir::fusion(program)` (`tir.rs:404`). The top-level entry points that choose the fused vs eager path are each emitter's `emit` fn checking `if let Some(fusion) = tir::fusion(program)`.\n\n## emit_jq.rs (899 lines)\n\n**1. Builtin dispatch.** `fn expr(enums, t)` at `:466`; arm at `:518`:\n```rust\nKind::Builtin { which, arg } => match which {\n    Builtin::IntToStr => format!(\"({} | tostring)\", expr(enums, arg)),\n    ...\n    // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n    Builtin::Collect => expr(enums, arg),\n    Builtin::Length => format!(\"({} | length)\", expr(enums, arg)),\n    ...\n    Builtin::JsonLines => {\n        let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n        format!(\n            \"({} | [.[] | ({} | tojson)] | join(\\\"\\\\n\\\"))\",\n            expr(enums, arg),\n            canonical(enums, elem, \".\")\n        )\n    }\n```\n(`Length` at `:539`, `Collect` at `:538`, `JsonLines` at `:529-536`.)\n\n**2. Fused loop.** Uniquely, jq has no separate `fused_main` fn — the loop is inlined into `emit(program: &Program) -> Result<String, String>` itself (`:58`), at `:89-120`. It emits `inputs` (jq's own lazy generator) then chains each stage as a filter:\n```rust\nif let Some(fusion) = tir::fusion(program) {\n    out.push_str(\"inputs\");\n    for stage in &fusion.stages {\n        match stage {\n            tir::Stage::Map { param, body } => {\n                out.push_str(&format!(\" | . as {} | {}\", local(*param), expr(enums, body)));\n            }\n            tir::Stage::Select { param, pred } => {\n                out.push_str(&format!(\" | . as {} | select({})\", local(*param), expr(enums, pred)));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    let Kind::Builtin { arg, .. } = &program.body.kind else { unreachable!(...) };\n    let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(...);\n    out.push_str(&format!(\" | ({} | tojson)\\n\", canonical(enums, elem, \".\")));\n```\nso each record is printed via `tojson` as it arrives (`:95-118`).\n\n**3. Subprocesses.** None. `rg -i \"subprocess|process|command|popen|spawn|exec|@sh|env\"` over the file returns nothing.\n\n**4. Invoking an external program from jq / lines / raw input.** The emitted source never invokes an external program — no `@sh`, no jq `system`/`subprocess` filter, no `env`. What exists:\n- `Kind::Input` → `\"$t_input\"` (`:472`), bound by `. as $t_input |` in `emit` (`:123`).\n- `Kind::Inputs` → `\"$t_inputs\"` (`:473`), bound by `[inputs] as $t_inputs |` (`:128`).\n- `Kind::Lines` → `\"[ inputs ]\"` (`:477`) with the comment that this is raw-input mode, made available by `-n -R` on the invocation.\n- The raw/`-R` behavior is entirely host-side: `run_jq` in `lib.rs:449-481` runs `std::process::Command::new(\"jq\")` with `--unbuffered -c`, optional `-r` when the result is a `Str`, `-n` when nothing is in `.`, and `-R -n` when `uses_lines` (`lib.rs:476-477`). So the *only* subprocess in the jq path is the compiler itself running jq.\n\n**5. `lines` emission.** Eager: `[ inputs ]` in raw mode (`:477`). Fused (`Source::Lines`): the same `inputs` filter with no JSON parse per record — the `Source` distinction only affects what the `-R` flag makes `inputs` mean.\n\n## emit_py.rs (845 lines)\n\n**1. Builtin dispatch.** `fn expr(enums, t)` at `:397`; arm at `:450`:\n```rust\nKind::Builtin { which, arg } => match which {\n    Builtin::IntToStr => format!(\"str({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n    ...\n    Builtin::JsonLines => {\n        let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n        let e = \"e0\".to_string();\n        format!(\n            \"tl_jsonlines({}, lambda {e}: {})\",\n            expr(enums, arg),\n            show(enums, elem, &e, 1)\n        )\n    }\n    // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n    Builtin::Collect => expr(enums, arg),\n    Builtin::Length => format!(\"len({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n```\n(`JsonLines` at `:456-464`, `Collect` at `:466`, `Length` at `:467`.)\n\n**2. Fused loop.** `fn fused_main(program: &Program, fusion: &tir::Fusion) -> String` at `:295`. Emits `for _line in sys.stdin:` (`:298`), strips the trailing newline, then for `Source::Inputs` skips blank lines and `t_line = json.loads(_line)` (`:300-309`); for `Source::Lines` the raw line is the element. Per-stage emission (`:313-329`):\n```rust\ntir::Stage::Map { param, body } => {\n    out.push_str(&format!(\"    {} = {}\\n\", local(*param), current));\n    current = expr(enums, body);\n    current_ty = body.ty.clone();\n}\ntir::Stage::Select { param, pred } => {\n    out.push_str(&format!(\"    {} = {}\\n\", local(*param), current));\n    out.push_str(&format!(\"    if not ({}):\\n        continue\\n\", expr(enums, pred)));\n    current = local(*param);\n}\n```\nthen prints via `show(...)` + `.encode(\"utf-8\")` and an explicit `.flush()` (`:330-334`).\n\n**3. Subprocesses.** None in emitted code (no `subprocess`/`os.`).\n\n**4/5. `lines`.** Eager: `Kind::Lines => \"tl_collect_lines()\"` (`:407`); helper `tl_collect_lines` (`:110-115`) does `for line in sys.stdin`. Fused: loops over `sys.stdin` directly (`:298`).\n\nHelper inclusion is a textual scan of the emitted source (`uses(\"tl_jsonlines(\")`, `:245-274`) — adding a builtin whose spelling is a `tl_*` call is picked up automatically.\n\n## emit_lua.rs (995 lines)\n\n**1. Builtin dispatch.** `fn expr(enums, t)` at `:712`; arm at `:766`:\n```rust\nKind::Builtin { which, arg } => match which {\n    Builtin::IntToStr => format!(\"tostring({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n    ...\n    Builtin::JsonLines => {\n        let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n        let e = \"e0\".to_string();\n        format!(\n            \"tl_jsonlines({}, function({e}) return {} end)\",\n            expr(enums, arg),\n            show(enums, elem, &e, 1)\n        )\n    }\n    // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n    Builtin::Collect => expr(enums, arg),\n    Builtin::Length => format!(\"#{}\", expr(enums, arg)),\n```\n(`JsonLines` at `:772-780`, `Collect` at `:782`, `Length` at `:783`.) Helper dependencies are tracked structurally rather than by text scan: `builtin_helpers(which, arg_ty, used)` at `:576-587` sets one `Helpers` flag per builtin (e.g. `used.jsonlines |= which == Builtin::JsonLines`), driven by `used_helpers(program)` (`:589`), with the flags emitted at `:264-287`. A new builtin needing a helper must add a flag here and in the `Helpers` struct (`:521-540`).\n\n**2. Fused loop.** `fn fused_main(program: &Program, fusion: &tir::Fusion) -> String` at `:335`. `Source::Inputs` emits a `while true do` loop calling `local t_line = tl_next_input()` (the injected `NEXT_INPUT` global, `:13`), breaking on `nil` (`:340-348`); `Source::Lines` emits `for t_line in io.lines() do` (`:350`). Per-stage emission (`:354-370`): Map binds the local and reassigns `current = expr(enums, body)`; Select binds the local then `if not ({}) then goto tl_continue end`, with a `::tl_continue::` label after the print.\n\n**3. Subprocesses.** None — no `io.popen`, no `os.execute`; the only io use is `io.lines()`.\n\n**4/5. `lines`.** Eager: `Kind::Lines => \"tl_collect_lines()\"` (`:722`); helper `tl_collect_lines` (`:42-48`) uses `for line in io.lines()`. Fused: `io.lines()` directly (`:350`).\n\n## emit_js.rs (1070 lines)\n\n**1. Builtin dispatch.** `fn expr(enums, t)` at `:743`; arm at `:802`:\n```rust\nKind::Builtin { which, arg } => match which {\n    Builtin::IntToStr => format!(\"String({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n    ...\n    Builtin::JsonLines => {\n        let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n        let e = \"e0\".to_string();\n        format!(\n            \"tl_jsonlines({}, ({e}) => {})\",\n            expr(enums, arg),\n            show(enums, elem, &e, 1)\n        )\n    }\n    // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n    Builtin::Collect => expr(enums, arg),\n    Builtin::Length => format!(\"{}.length\", expr(enums, arg)),\n```\n(`JsonLines` at `:814-822`, `Collect` at `:824`, `Length` at `:825`.) Helpers via `used_helpers` walk at `:526` (`Kind::Lines => used.collect = true` at `:536`; `used.jsonlines |= *which == Builtin::JsonLines` at `:575`).\n\n**2. Fused loop.** `fn fused_main(program: &Program, fusion: &tir::Fusion) -> String` at `:278`. This one is the most elaborate: it emits a custom chunked line reader `tl_read_line` over `fs.readSync(0, ...)` into a 64 KB buffer (`:281-303`), then `for (;;)` reads one line, `Source::Inputs` does `const t_line = JSON.parse(t_line_raw)` after skipping empty lines, `Source::Lines` uses the raw line (`:305-320`). Per-stage emission (`:321-334`):\n```rust\ntir::Stage::Map { param, body } => {\n    out.push_str(&format!(\"  const {} = {};\\n\", local(*param), current));\n    current = expr(enums, body);\n    current_ty = body.ty.clone();\n}\ntir::Stage::Select { param, pred } => {\n    out.push_str(&format!(\"  const {} = {};\\n\", local(*param), current));\n    out.push_str(&format!(\"  if (!({})) continue;\\n\", expr(enums, pred)));\n    current = local(*param);\n}\n```\nthen `console.log(show(...))` (`:335-338`).\n\n**3. Subprocesses.** None — no `child_process`; the only module the emitted code requires is `fs`.\n\n**4/5. `lines`.** Eager: `Kind::Lines => \"tl_collect_lines()\"` (`:753`); helper `tl_collect_lines` (`:78-99`) reads fd 0 in `fs.readSync` chunks. Fused: the `tl_read_line` chunked reader (`:281-303`).\n\n## emit_go.rs (1369 lines)\n\n**1. Builtin dispatch.** `expr` method; arm at `:993`:\n```rust\nKind::Builtin { which, arg } => match which {\n    Builtin::IntToStr => format!(\"strconv.FormatInt(int64({}), 10)\", self.expr(arg)),\n    ...\n    Builtin::JsonLines => {\n        let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n        let e = \"e0\".to_string();\n        format!(\n            \"tlJsonlines({}, func({e} {}) string {{ return {} }})\",\n            self.expr(arg),\n            self.go_type(elem),\n            self.show(elem, &e, 1)\n        )\n    }\n    // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n    Builtin::Collect => self.expr(arg),\n    Builtin::Length => format!(\"int32(len({}))\", self.expr(arg)),\n```\n(`JsonLines` at `:998-1007`, `Collect` at `:1009`, `Length` at `:1010`.) There is also a separate usage-walk Builtin arm at `:663-688` that records `used.jsonlines`, `used.itoa`, and `jsonlines_has_scalar` (the last drives the `strconv` import, `:488-493`). Helpers are otherwise gated by textual `decls.contains(...)` (`:427-434`) and imports by a table (`:479-498`).\n\n**2. Fused loop.** `fn fused_main(&self, program: &Program, fusion: &tir::Fusion) -> String` at `:827`, emitted as `func main() {`. `Source::Inputs` uses `json.NewDecoder(os.Stdin)` in a `for { ... }` loop with `io.EOF` break (`:836-841`); `Source::Lines` uses `bufio.NewScanner(os.Stdin)` with `tlScanLines` (`:846-851`). Per-stage emission (`:855-880`): each stage binds `{local} := {current}` followed by `_ = {local}` (Go rejects an unused local), then Map sets `current = self.expr(body)` and Select emits `if !({}) { continue }`.\n\n**3. Subprocesses.** None — no `os/exec`, no `exec.Command`, no `os.StartProcess`. Only `bufio`, `bytes`, `json`, `io`, `os`, `fmt`, `strings`, `strconv`, `cmp`, `slices`, `reflect` as imports.\n\n**4/5. `lines`.** Eager: `Kind::Lines => \"tlCollectLines()\"` (`:925`); helpers `tlScanLines` (`:212-223`, a copy of `bufio.ScanLines` minus the `\\r` strip) and `tlCollectLines` (`:225-231`). Fused: the same scanner inline (`:846-851`).\n\n## emit_rs.rs (1466 lines)\n\n**1. Builtin dispatch.** `expr` method; arm at `:1124`:\n```rust\nKind::Builtin { which, arg } => match which {\n    Builtin::IntToStr => format!(\"({}).to_string()\", self.expr(arg)),\n    ...\n    Builtin::JsonLines => {\n        let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n        let e = \"e0\".to_string();\n        format!(\n            \"tl_jsonlines(&{}, |{e}: &{}| -> String {{ {} }})\",\n            self.expr(arg),\n            self.rs_type(elem),\n            self.show(elem, &format!(\"{e}.clone()\"), 1)\n        )\n    }\n    // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n    Builtin::Collect => self.expr(arg),\n    Builtin::Length => format!(\"(({}).len() as i32)\", self.expr(arg)),\n```\n(`JsonLines` at `:1129-1138`, `Collect` at `:1140`, `Length` at `:1141`.) The `used`-scan Builtin arm at `:781-786` only sets `self.used.jsonlines`; everything else is textually gated.\n\n**2. Fused loop.** `fn fused_main(&self, program: &Program, fusion: &tir::Fusion) -> String` at `:966`, emitting `fn main()` with locked stdin/stdout, then a `loop { line.clear(); stdin.read_line(&mut line)... }` (`:975-981`). `Source::Inputs` skips blank lines and calls `let t_line: T = tl_parse_line(&line, ...)` (`:988-993`); `Source::Lines` clones the raw line (`:997-999`). Per-stage emission (`:1002-1027`): Map binds `let {local}: {ty} = {current};` then `current = self.expr(body)`; Select binds the local, emits `if !({}) { continue; }`, and sets `current = format!(\"{}.clone()\", self.local(*param))`.\n\n**3. Subprocesses.** None in emitted code — the only `std::process` reference is `std::process::exit(1)` inside the `tl_fail` runtime helper (`:28`). No `Command`.\n\n**4/5. `lines`.** Eager: `Kind::Lines => \"tl_read_lines()\"` (`:1079`); helper `tl_read_lines` (`:171-182`) reads all stdin then splits on `\\n` (deliberately not `BufRead::lines`, which would strip `\\r`). Fused: `stdin.read_line` loop (`:975-981`).\n\n## emit_llvm.rs (2586 lines)\n\n**1. Builtin dispatch.** `expr` method; the arm at `:1423` evaluates the argument first (`let arg = self.expr(arg)?;`) then dispatches:\n```rust\nKind::Builtin { which, arg } => {\n    let elem_ty = crate::tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).cloned();\n    ...\n    let arg = self.expr(arg)?;\n    match which {\n        Builtin::IntToStr => self.call_rt(self.rt.int_to_str, &[arg], \"int_str\")?,\n        ...\n        Builtin::JsonLines => {\n            let elem = elem_ty.expect(\"checked to be a Vec\");\n            self.join_shown(arg, &elem, \"\", \"\\n\", \"\")?\n        }\n        // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n        Builtin::Collect => arg,\n        // Already tracked on the Vec header; nothing to compute.\n        Builtin::Length => self.call_rt(self.rt.vec_len, &[arg], \"length\")?,\n```\n(`JsonLines` at `:1436-1439`, `Collect` at `:1441`, `Length` at `:1443`.) Builtins mostly lower to C runtime calls (`self.rt.*`, declared as LLVM extern functions at `:147-220`). `join_shown` (`:894-939`) is the shared Vec rendering loop used by both the printer and jsonlines, differing only in open/sep/close punctuation.\n\n**2. Fused loop.** `fn fused_main(&mut self, program: &Program, fusion: &Fusion<'_>) -> Result<(), String>` at `:2308`. Emits IR basic blocks `fused.cond` / `fused.body` / `fused.end`, calling the C runtime `tl_read_one_input` (with a type descriptor, `Source::Inputs`) or `tl_read_one_line` (`Source::Lines`) into an alloca slot (`:2338-2359`). Per-stage emission (`:2370-2389`): Map inserts `self.locals.insert(*param, Slot::Value(current))` and recomputes `current = self.expr(body)?`; Select inserts the local, evaluates the predicate, and branches `keep`/`cond` (back to the top, i.e. `continue`). Prints via `self.rt.print` (`:2394-2397`).\n\n**3. Subprocesses.** None — no popen/system/fork in the emitter or in the C runtime.\n\n**4/5. `lines`.** Eager: `Kind::Lines => self.call_rt(self.rt.collect_lines, &[], \"lines\")?` (`:1340`); runtime `tl_collect_lines` in `runtime/toylang.c:983-994` loops `getline`. Fused: `tl_read_one_line` in `runtime/toylang.c:956-972` (`getline`, strips trailing `\\n`), called at `emit_llvm.rs:2349`. `Source::Inputs` fused uses `tl_read_one_input` (`runtime/toylang.c:922-950`), which skips blank/whitespace-only lines and parses JSON.\n\n---\n\n## emit_toylang.rs (the formatter, 731 lines)\n\nThis is the odd one out by design (module doc, `:1-4`): it walks `ast::Expr`, **not** `tir::Kind` — a grep for `Builtin`/`Kind::` in the file returns zero matches. There is no `Kind::Builtin` arm and no per-builtin rendering. A builtin call is just an ordinary `Expr::Call`, rendered at `:419-422`:\n```rust\nExpr::Call { func, arg, .. } => match arg {\n    None => format!(\"{func}()\"),\n    Some(a) => format!(\"{func}({})\", print_paren_arg(a)),\n},\n```\nSo `jsonlines(x)`, `length(x)`, and `collect(x)` all round-trip by printing the identifier plus parens — the builtin names are ordinary source tokens, and the formatter needs no change for a new builtin as long as it's a normal unary call `pipe_through(...)`. (The wrapped-call case for an overlong argument is `:609-614`, still just `format!(\"{func}{}\", wrap_delim(...))`.)\n\nOne adjacent file worth knowing: `tags.rs::builtin_tag` (`tags.rs:161-177`) maps each `Builtin` to a dotted tag like `\"jsonlines\"`, `\"length\"` (used by `tests/tag_corpus.rs` to build the corpus case tree) — a new builtin needs an arm there too, since `tag_corpus` exhaustively covers the enum.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-cutting observations for adding `pipe_through`\n\n- Every backend has exactly one `Kind::Builtin { which, arg }` match in its `expr` fn (line refs above), plus a parallel usage/helper scan: textual `contains` for py/go/rs, structural `Helpers` walk for lua/js, `self.used` walk for go/rs, and `uses_arith` for jq. Adding a builtin that needs emitted support means touching the dispatch arm and, depending on backend, the helper-inclusion mechanism.\n- The fused loops only ever process `Map`/`Select` stages (`tir::Stage`), and no builtin is legal inside them other than as an ordinary expression on the current element — a per-element builtin would slot in naturally as the `body`/`pred` expression, but a source-level builtin wrapping the whole stream would not be representable in the fused loop as written.\n- **No emitted code anywhere spawns a subprocess.** The only `std::process::Command` invocations in the repo are host-side in `lib.rs` (`run_subprocess`/`run_jq`/`run_py`/`run_go`, `lib.rs:343-480`), which compile/run the emitted artifact. For jq, even though jq itself *can* run external commands (`@sh`, `system`), the emitter never produces such code and there is no existing plumbing to do so. So `pipe_through` (if it means running an external command) has no existing substrate in any of the seven emitters — each would need new runtime support (a Python `subprocess` call, Lua `io.popen`/`os.execute`, JS `child_process`, Go `os/exec`, Rust `std::process::Command`, a new C runtime function for LLVM) or, for jq, an `@sh`-based emission plus the raw/`-R` interplay noted in point 4 above.\n</task_result>\n</task>","metadata":{"parentSessionId":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","sessionId":"ses_fab2954b5fferk5Plr5jfoZr2B","model":{"modelID":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731","providerID":"openrouter"},"truncated":false},"title":"Map emitter architecture","time":{"start":1788129749836,"end":1788130091572}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So the language is unary-only: \"A function takes at most one parameter.\" The issue's `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` uses a comma, but the language has no multi-arg call syntax. Something with two arguments takes a record.\n\nSo `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` — the comma form doesn't exist. It would have to be spelled differently, like `pipe_through({program: \"grep\", args: [\"foo\"]})` or a curried form. Hmm, but the issue explicitly gives the shape as `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])`.\n\nWait — maybe the parse does accept comma-separated arguments in parens as a Vec literal? Let me check parse.rs. Actually let me check what `(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` would parse as. In many languages, `f(a, b)` is multi-arg. Let me look at the parser.\n\nActually, the issue says the shape is \"PROPOSED/unverified\" — \"Shape ratified (from the round's preview, PROPOSED/unverified)\". So the shape is provisional. The design is genuinely still open in many ways.\n\nGiven the brief, I need to build \"per the gh:158 ruling: full streaming, e.g. lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [...]) | collect.\" The core requirement is full streaming pipe-to-subprocess. The exact spelling may need adjustment to fit the unary-call language (record arg or curried).\n\nOK let me take stock. This is a huge task with several genuine open design decisions:\n1. Call shape (2 args in a unary language)\n2. jq can't run subprocesses — corpus agreement impossible\n3. Q35 effect model (still open) — what stdout/stderr mean\n\nThe brief says: \"If something needs deciding that this brief does not settle, write ESCALATION.md ... COMMIT it on this branch, take the most conservative continuation, and keep going.\"\n\nBut also: \"never stop to wait for a human\" and \"take the most conservative continuation\".\n\nLet me think about the most conservative but complete implementation that satisfies \"implementation complete; just check green\".\n\nGiven the jq constraint is fatal for corpus output cases, and the corpus harness has no per-backend skip, I think the honest reading is:\n\nThe `pipe_through` primitive should be **designed and implemented**, with corpus tests being `refuses: true` cases where jq's refusal is expected... no wait.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. Let me re-read the corpus rules very carefully:\n\n\"Every case runs on every backend. `output` means they all print it and agree; `refuses: true` means they all decline to run it, and what each says while refusing is its own business.\"\n\nSo actually — for a `refuses: true` case, EVERY backend must refuse. If I implement pipe_through on all backends EXCEPT jq, then the non-jq backends RUN it (producing output), and the harness flags them. So that fails.\n\nIf I implement it on NO backend (checker-level refusal everywhere), then refuses: true works and \"covers all backends\" trivially. But that's not \"implementation complete.\"\n\nSo there's a hard conflict: the corpus mechanism can't express \"runs everywhere except jq\".\n\nWait — unless... what if jq CAN run it? Let me think harder about jq. jq cannot spawn processes. Period. There is no subprocess in jq.\n\nSo the options for the corpus:\n- Add a new corpus key to express per-backend behavior (e.g., `backends: [lua, js, native, go, py, rust]` or `exclude: [jq]`). This is a design change to the harness — needs docs updates, tests of the harness itself. AGENTS.md says unknown keys are errors — I'd be adding a known key.\n- Or make the corpus case use `refuses: true` but implement pipe_through as a **checker refusal on all backends** for now (design ratified, implemented as refusal). Then later when backends gain subprocess, migrate to output cases.\n\nHmm, but actually, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe I'm wrong that jq can't do it. Let me reconsider what \"pipe_through\" could mean at the backend level.\n\nWhat if `pipe_through` at the jq level is implemented as... hmm, no. There's genuinely no way.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the ENTIRE approach. What if pipe_through isn't a per-backend codegen thing, but a HOST-level primitive? Like, the Rust host (lib.rs) recognizes a `pipe_through` sink/pipeline and does the subprocess orchestration itself, feeding the backend's output into the subprocess and the subprocess's output back? \n\nNo — that doesn't make sense for \"streaming\": the whole point is that the generated code streams line-by-line to the subprocess. If the host does it, it's not streaming.\n\nBut wait — actually, maybe it could be a hybrid. Hmm, but the issue is about a language primitive; the generated code should do it.\n\nLet me look at how the backends actually emit code, to understand what's feasible. Let me look at emit_jq.rs and emit_py.rs and emit_lua.rs to understand the structure, especially how jsonlines sink and the fused loop are emitted.\n\nLet me also check: how does the checker special-case `jsonlines` as a sink? I need to see check/mod.rs's handling.\n\nThis is getting big. Let me read check/mod.rs fully. It's ~1600 lines based on the grep hits. Let me read it in chunks, focusing on builtins, sink, signature table, and call checking.\n\nActually, let me be efficient and use explore agents to map the emitter structure while I read the checker myself.\n\nLet me launch an explore agent for the emitters, and read check/mod.rs myself.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_054d663b90010ve0jajr4wC31v","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","messageID":"msg_054d5f2fb001CD8CkBauGZrn1i"}}
