{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788130146396,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8db38e5112fe63b5","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/docs/adr/0003-the-corpus-is-the-spec.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/docs/adr/0003-the-corpus-is-the-spec.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: status: accepted\n3: ---\n4: \n5: # The corpus is the spec, and it checks consistency, not conformance\n6: \n7: Recorded 2026-08-27, after the fact, from AGENTS.md and the research log; the harness predates\n8: most of the backends.\n9: \n10: The language's behavioral record is a corpus of YAML cases (`tests/corpus/`), one file per\n11: case: a program, optional input, and either the expected output or `refuses: true`. Every case\n12: runs on every backend, and the test is agreement -- all backends print the same bytes, or all\n13: refuse (each in its own words). There is no per-backend test suite and no reference\n14: implementation; the corpus plus the agreement requirement is what \"correct\" means day to day.\n15: Snapshots exist as a deliberate exception for claims output cannot carry, and programs that do\n16: not compile live in `step_*.rs` instead, since nothing about them differs per backend.\n17: \n18: Two limitations were discovered rather than designed, and both are load-bearing enough to\n19: record:\n20: \n21: - Agreement proves the backends say the same thing, not that the thing is what the language\n22:   means. The gap is widest where backends are similar: four targets once agreed on a wrong\n23:   answer because on that question they were one witness, quadrupled\n24:   ([backends can agree and still be wrong](../../research-log/backends-can-agree-and-still-be-wrong.md)).\n25:   Conformance has to be stated by hand; expectations are validated against independent tools,\n26:   not recorded from the compiler\n27:   ([a test that cannot fail is worse than no test](../../research-log/a-test-that-cannot-fail-is-worse-than-no-test.md)).\n28: - Output equality cannot see behavior. A fused streaming loop and a fully eager program print\n29:   identical bytes on finite input, so streaming needed its own liveness probes\n30:   (`tests/streaming.rs`), and checker-level rules need their own rejection tests. Any future\n31:   property that is not a function of the output needs its own test kind; the corpus will stay\n32:   green while it regresses.\n\n(End of file - total 32 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\nstatus: accepted\n---\n\n# The corpus is the spec, and it checks consistency, not conformance\n\nRecorded 2026-08-27, after the fact, from AGENTS.md and the research log; the harness predates\nmost of the backends.\n\nThe language's behavioral record is a corpus of YAML cases (`tests/corpus/`), one file per\ncase: a program, optional input, and either the expected output or `refuses: true`. Every case\nruns on every backend, and the test is agreement -- all backends print the same bytes, or all\nrefuse (each in its own words). There is no per-backend test suite and no reference\nimplementation; the corpus plus the agreement requirement is what \"correct\" means day to day.\nSnapshots exist as a deliberate exception for claims output cannot carry, and programs that do\nnot compile live in `step_*.rs` instead, since nothing about them differs per backend.\n\nTwo limitations were discovered rather than designed, and both are load-bearing enough to\nrecord:\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/docs/adr/0003-the-corpus-is-the-spec.md","text":"---\nstatus: accepted\n---\n\n# The corpus is the spec, and it checks consistency, not conformance\n\nRecorded 2026-08-27, after the fact, from AGENTS.md and the research log; the harness predates\nmost of the backends.\n\nThe language's behavioral record is a corpus of YAML cases (`tests/corpus/`), one file per\ncase: a program, optional input, and either the expected output or `refuses: true`. Every case\nruns on every backend, and the test is agreement -- all backends print the same bytes, or all\nrefuse (each in its own words). There is no per-backend test suite and no reference\nimplementation; the corpus plus the agreement requirement is what \"correct\" means day to day.\nSnapshots exist as a deliberate exception for claims output cannot carry, and programs that do\nnot compile live in `step_*.rs` instead, since nothing about them differs per backend.\n\nTwo limitations were discovered rather than designed, and both are load-bearing enough to\nrecord:\n\n- Agreement proves the backends say the same thing, not that the thing is what the language\n  means. The gap is widest where backends are similar: four targets once agreed on a wrong\n  answer because on that question they were one witness, quadrupled\n  ([backends can agree and still be wrong](../../research-log/backends-can-agree-and-still-be-wrong.md)).\n  Conformance has to be stated by hand; expectations are validated against independent tools,\n  not recorded from the compiler\n  ([a test that cannot fail is worse than no test](../../research-log/a-test-that-cannot-fail-is-worse-than-no-test.md)).\n- Output equality cannot see behavior. A fused streaming loop and a fully eager program print\n  identical bytes on finite input, so streaming needed its own liveness probes\n  (`tests/streaming.rs`), and checker-level rules need their own rejection tests. Any future\n  property that is not a function of the output needs its own test kind; the corpus will stay\n  green while it regresses.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":32,"totalLines":32,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/adr/0003-the-corpus-is-the-spec.md","time":{"start":1788130146387,"end":1788130146394}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the checker structure. Let me think hard about the design now, because there are genuinely open decisions that I need to resolve carefully. Let me reconsider what the right thing to do is here.\n\nThe task is fundamentally a design+build task for a feature that the issue itself says is blocked on Q35 (which is OPEN) and whose shape is \"PROPOSED/unverified\", on a board where the design row `shell-out-design` is still `todo` (a `decide` task that hasn't been through its grill round).\n\nThere are several hard blockers to a full implementation:\n\n1. **Q35 is open.** The effect model (does a program write or return? what is stdout/stderr?) is genuinely undecided. The issue says the feature \"explicitly inherits the open Q35 dependency\". The brief acknowledges this: \"Depends on Q35's stdout/stderr effect model -- check draft.md and the ADRs for that model before designing the primitive's signature.\"\n\n2. **jq cannot run subprocesses**, and the corpus requires all 7 backends to agree. So a corpus `output` case is impossible for pipe_through unless we change the corpus format or jq magically can.\n\n3. **The language is unary-only.** `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` with two args needs a design decision (record arg or currying).\n\nNow, the brief says: \"If something needs deciding that this brief does not settle, write ESCALATION.md at the worktree root -- the question, the alternatives, their costs -- COMMIT it on this branch, take the most conservative continuation, and keep going; never stop to wait for a human.\"\n\nAnd: \"If you genuinely cannot continue at all, ESCALATION.md plus exiting IS the correct done-state -- never commit broken work to look finished.\"\n\nBut also: \"Definition of done: implementation complete; `just check` green.\"\n\nSo what's the most conservative complete implementation I can do?\n\nLet me think about what CAN be done and what the \"most conservative continuation\" is.\n\nGiven the constraints, I believe the correct interpretation is:\n\n**Design the primitive properly** (docs: reference page, ADR or draft update, Q35 tie-in) AND **implement it as far as the constraints allow**:\n\nThe honest, complete, conservative scope:\n- Add `pipe_through` as a **reserved builtin name**.\n- Type it: `(Stream<T> -> Stream<U>)` — wait, what are T and U here?\n\nActually, let me think about the typing carefully. `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"]) | collect`:\n- `lines`: `Stream<Str>` (lines of stdin).\n- `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])`: a value that, given the stream on the left, runs grep and produces a `Stream<Str>`.\n- `collect`: `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`.\n\nSo pipe_through maps `Stream<Str> -> Stream<Str>`. The input element type is Str (fed as lines to the subprocess stdin), the output element type is Str (subprocess stdout lines). For generality, maybe `Vec<T> -> Vec<T>`? The subprocess reads text lines and writes text lines, so input/output are always Str at the boundary. So `pipe_through` is effectively `Str -> Stream<Str>`-ish... no.\n\nHmm. Let me think again. In `lines | pipe_through(...)`, what does pipe_through receive? The stream of lines. It spawns the subprocess with stdin = those lines, and produces a stream of stdout lines. So:\n\n`pipe_through(program: Str, args: Vec<Str>) : Stream<Str> -> Stream<Str>`\n\nBut unary... So `pipe_through` takes a record `{program: Str, args: Vec<Str>}` or it's a two-arg function somehow.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider the call syntax. Does the parser support `f(a, b)`? Let me check. The issue's example uses `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` with a comma. Let me check parse.rs whether a parenthesized argument list with commas parses as... probably not; the language has no tuples. Let me verify quickly.\n\nActually, let me check the AST and parser for how `Call` args are parsed — is there a Vec of args or a single optional arg?\n\nFrom functions.md: \"A function takes at most one parameter.\" And \"Several things travel as one record\". So multi-arg = record literal. `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` as written does not fit the language. The shape in the issue is PROPOSED/unverified, so I have latitude to adapt it, e.g. `pipe_through({program: \"grep\", args: [\"foo\"]})`. But hold on — that changes the ratified-ish shape.\n\nHmm wait, actually, maybe the intended reading: the issue's shape is from a grill-round preview and the \"maintainer picked full streaming pipe-to-subprocess over blocking-capture-only\". The core decision is full streaming. The exact spelling is unverified. So I have room.\n\nOK here's my problem: this is a large design+build task with genuinely open design decisions (call shape, jq handling, corpus handling, Q35 semantics). The brief tells me to escalate what it doesn't settle, take the most conservative continuation, and keep going — but ALSO says never commit broken work to look finished.\n\nLet me reconsider: what is the MINIMAL correct implementation that is complete and green, per the conservative continuation?\n\nI think the answer is: **implement `pipe_through` as a real, working builtin on the backends that can run subprocesses, and make jq refuse it with a clear message; and add corpus tests using `refuses: true` where ALL backends refuse** — no wait, that requires all to refuse.\n\nUgh, the jq problem again. Let me re-examine: is there truly no way to make the corpus harness express \"runs on 6, refuses on jq\"? \n\nLooking at the harness: for `Expect::Output`, every backend must run and agree. For `Expect::Refusal`, every backend must refuse. There's genuinely no middle ground.\n\nSo the two coherent options:\n(A) pipe_through works on 6 backends, jq refuses; NO corpus case can express it. Tests go in backend-specific test files (tests/backend_py.rs etc.) or a new tests/pipe_through.rs that runs only the capable backends. This means \"add corpus tests\" is impossible as-is; tests would be step-style or backend-style.\n(B) Add a corpus harness capability for per-backend expectations (new key). This is a design change to the harness.\n(C) pipe_through is checker-ratified but refused on all backends (design + refusal), corpus `refuses: true` cases. Not \"built\".\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider whether jq could actually do it after all. jq... no. There's `@sh` (quoting only), `env`, `input`, `inputs`, `recurse`, etc. No process spawn. Confirmed impossible.\n\nOK so let me now think about what \"Cover all backends\" means in the brief. \"Cover all backends and add corpus tests.\" Perhaps \"cover all backends\" means: ensure every backend has a defined, tested behavior for pipe_through (6 run it, jq refuses with a message tested in its own test file), and \"add corpus tests\" means add corpus cases that DO work — but the only corpus cases that work are `refuses: true` ones where all refuse...\n\nWait wait wait. Actually — hold on. Let me reconsider. What if pipe_through is implemented such that ALL backends including jq can run it — by making the subprocess happen at the HOST level for jq? No. jq's emitted code can't call back into Rust.\n\nAlternatively: what if the corpus case for pipe_through runs a subprocess that EXISTS in the corpus environment (like `cat` or `tr`), and jq — since it can't run subprocesses — refuses, causing the harness to flag \"BROKEN jq\". That fails `just check`.\n\nSo (A) requires either changing the harness or not having a corpus output case. \n\nGiven all this, I'm now fairly convinced the right move is:\n\n1. Write ESCALATION.md documenting the design decisions that the brief doesn't settle:\n   - Call shape (2-arg call in a unary language) — propose record-arg or the comma form.\n   - jq/corpus conflict: the corpus agreement harness cannot express \"runs on six backends, jq refuses\" — options and costs.\n   - Q35 dependency: what stdout means for pipe_through.\n2. Take the most conservative continuation that's still \"implementation complete\".\n\nWhat's the most conservative continuation? Let me think about what's genuinely implementable and green:\n\nGiven the corpus constraint, the most conservative fully-green implementation is:\n- Implement pipe_through end-to-end on the six subprocess-capable backends (Lua, JS, Go, Py, Rust, Native/LLVM), with real subprocess spawning and streaming.\n- jq: refuse with a clear message at emit/run time.\n- Corpus tests: `refuses: true` is impossible (6 would run). So instead: put the positive tests in a new step-style test file (tests/pipe_through.rs) that runs only the six capable backends, and jq's refusal in tests/backend_jq.rs or the new file.\n- This does NOT add corpus cases. That violates \"add corpus tests.\"\n\nHmm. Unless I change the harness. Let me look at whether there's precedent for harness changes being made by workers... The AGENTS.md is quite strict about the corpus: \"Unknown keys, unknown backend names, and a case with both or neither of `output` and `refuses` are all errors.\" But it also says \"Every rule earns its place...\" and the corpus section describes the current state. Adding a new capability (per-backend behavior) would be a real design change to \"the corpus is the spec\" (ADR 0003).\n\nActually, let me read ADR 0003 to understand the corpus's authority.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about the SCOPE more realistically. This task, done honestly and completely, is enormous:\n- New builtin across 6 backends' emitters + runtime support in each (Python subprocess, Lua io.popen, JS child_process, Go os/exec, Rust std::process, C runtime for LLVM).\n- Streaming semantics: feed lines as they arrive, read stdout as it arrives (not all at the end).\n- Checker work: new Builtin variant, typing, linearity (stream in + stream out), sink-or-not decisions.\n- jq refusal.\n- Tests: corpus + step tests + snapshots.\n- Docs: reference page + ADR + draft/questions update.\n\nAnd several genuine design decisions are open (Q35, call shape, corpus/jq conflict, stderr handling, exit-code handling, \"does pipe_through work on Vec too\", etc.).\n\nGiven \"never stop to wait for a human\" but also \"never commit broken work to look finished\" and \"ESCALATION.md plus exiting IS the correct done-state if you genuinely cannot continue at all\" — the question is whether I can \"continue at all\" meaningfully.\n\nI think I CAN make substantial, honest progress: implement the primitive on the six capable backends with real streaming, refuse on jq, and handle the corpus question by escalating it while adding corpus tests in a way that's green.\n\nWait, actually — here's a cleaner idea for the corpus that stays green and honest: Make the corpus cases `refuses: true` where the refusal is a *checker-level* refusal on all backends — no. That's not building.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider option (B): adding a per-backend key to the corpus. Let me check ADR 0003 to see how much authority it has.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing ONE more time. Let me re-read the exact brief: \"Cover all backends and add corpus tests.\"\n\nMaybe the intended design is more modest than I think. What if `pipe_through` is designed to work on ALL backends including jq by a different mechanism — namely, it's not spawning a real OS subprocess on jq, but... no.\n\nOR: what if the corpus test uses a program where the subprocess is run and all backends genuinely produce the same output because the harness itself provides the subprocess capability uniformly? Like, what if `pipe_through` is lowered to a host-level call that all backends route through? \n\nHmm, that's not how it works — each backend emits its own source which runs independently.\n\nOK. Let me look at ADR 0003 and think about whether modifying the harness is the \"derived\" thing to do.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_054dcb6e2001Hw4qRYekNBHhWJ","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","messageID":"msg_054dbf9a00014Wo3ufUrinp20M"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788130147101,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-85859459045ed883","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/tests/backend_jq.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/tests/backend_jq.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The jq backend against the things it alone can say.\n2: //!\n3: //! Behaviour lives in the corpus like every other backend. What is here is the two rules jq\n4: //! forced that the others did not.\n5: \n6: const FORWARD: &str = r#\"\n7: fn outer(x: Str) -> Str = inner(x) + \"!\"\n8: fn inner(x: Str) -> Str = \"[\" + x + \"]\"\n9: \n10: outer(\"hi\")\n11: \"#;\n12: \n13: /// jq resolves a `def` only against what is already defined and has no forward declaration, so\n14: /// definitions come out callee-first. The checker accepts the other order, which is a rule this\n15: /// target does not share.\n16: #[test]\n17: fn definitions_come_out_callee_first() {\n18:     let p = toylang::compile(FORWARD).unwrap();\n19:     let jq = toylang::emit_jq::emit(&p).unwrap();\n20:     let inner = jq.find(\"def v_inner\").expect(\"inner is defined\");\n21:     let outer = jq.find(\"def v_outer\").expect(\"outer is defined\");\n22:     assert!(inner < outer, \"callee must be defined first:\\n{jq}\");\n23:     insta::assert_snapshot!(jq);\n24: }\n25: \n26: /// jq's -r decides from the runtime value, so it would print a present Opt<Str> raw and an\n27: /// absent one as the word null. The rule here is the type's, as on every other backend.\n28: #[test]\n29: fn an_optional_string_prints_as_json() {\n30:     insta::assert_snapshot!(\n31:         toylang::run_on(r#\"[\"ada\", \"bo\"][0]\"#, None, toylang::Backend::Jq).unwrap()\n32:     );\n33: }\n34: \n35: /// `a` calls `b` calls `c` calls `a`: a real cycle between three named functions, the shape\n36: /// `plans/mini-parser-spike.md` found in a recursive-descent parser's own\n37: /// `expr`/`term`/`factor`/`group` chain (kantord/toylang#77, kantord/toylang#79). The checker\n38: /// accepts it -- signatures are collected before any body is checked, so a call to a function\n39: /// defined later, or back around a cycle, is no different from any forward reference.\n40: const CYCLE: &str = r#\"\n41: fn a(n: Int) -> Int = 0 if n <= 0 else 1 + b(n - 1)\n42: fn b(n: Int) -> Int = 0 if n <= 0 else 1 + c(n - 1)\n43: fn c(n: Int) -> Int = 0 if n <= 0 else 1 + a(n - 1)\n44: \n45: a(5)\n46: \"#;\n47: \n48: /// The six backends this cycle does not defeat: jq's `def` scoping is the one thing about this\n49: /// program that is backend-specific, so it cannot live in the corpus (kantord/toylang#79's own\n50: /// AGENTS.md rule -- every corpus case runs on every backend, and jq never can here). This pins\n51: /// the same \"every backend agrees\" claim by hand, over `Backend::ALL` minus `Jq`.\n52: #[test]\n53: fn mutual_recursion_runs_and_agrees_on_every_backend_but_jq() {\n54:     let mut outputs: Vec<(&str, String)> = Vec::new();\n55:     for backend in toylang::Backend::ALL {\n56:         if backend == toylang::Backend::Jq {\n57:             continue;\n58:         }\n59:         match toylang::run_on(CYCLE, None, backend) {\n60:             Ok(out) => outputs.push((backend.name(), out)),\n61:             Err(e) => panic!(\"{} could not run the cycle: {e}\", backend.name()),\n62:         }\n63:     }\n64:     let (_, first) = &outputs[0];\n65:     assert_eq!(first, \"5\\n\");\n66:     for (name, out) in &outputs {\n67:         assert_eq!(out, first, \"{name} disagreed with {}\", outputs[0].0);\n68:     }\n69: }\n70: \n71: /// The cycle jq alone cannot take: `ordered` cannot find any definition order where every\n72: /// function's callees are already in scope, so it refuses rather than emitting jq source that\n73: /// would fail to compile with an error naming a mangled internal name out of context\n74: /// (kantord/toylang#79).\n75: #[test]\n76: fn a_genuine_cycle_between_named_functions_is_refused_cleanly() {\n77:     let p = toylang::compile(CYCLE).unwrap();\n78:     let err = toylang::emit_jq::emit(&p).unwrap_err();\n79:     assert!(\n80:         err.contains('a') && err.contains('b') && err.contains('c'),\n81:         \"{err}\"\n82:     );\n83:     insta::assert_snapshot!(err);\n84: }\n85: \n86: /// Two enums that reach each other only through `Vec` type-check legally (kantord/toylang#94),\n87: /// but their printers form the same genuine cycle named functions can: `tl_show_A` calls\n88: /// `tl_show_B` calls `tl_show_A`, and jq's `def` has no forward declaration for that any more\n89: /// than it does for `a`/`b`/`c` above (kantord/toylang#116).\n90: const PRINTER_CYCLE: &str = r#\"\n91: enum A { a(Vec<B>) }\n92: enum B { b(Vec<A>) }\n93: \n94: {x: A.a([]), y: B.b([])}\n95: \"#;\n96: \n97: /// The printer-side counterpart to `a_genuine_cycle_between_named_functions_is_refused_cleanly`:\n98: /// before kantord/toylang#116, `printers()` emitted defs in DFS-discovery order with no cycle\n99: /// check, so this program would have failed with jq's own raw error naming a mangled internal\n100: /// name instead of toylang's clean refusal.\n101: #[test]\n102: fn a_genuine_cycle_between_printers_is_refused_cleanly() {\n103:     let p = toylang::compile(PRINTER_CYCLE).unwrap();\n104:     let err = toylang::emit_jq::emit(&p).unwrap_err();\n105:     assert!(\n106:         err.contains(\"tl_show_A\") && err.contains(\"tl_show_B\"),\n107:         \"{err}\"\n108:     );\n109:     insta::assert_snapshot!(err);\n110: }\n111: \n112: /// Direct self-recursion is not a cycle `ordered` ever gets stuck on: a function calling only\n113: /// itself is always immediately ready, so jq keeps running every corpus program that recurses\n114: /// this way (`join_lines`, `join`, and every self-recursive corpus case already do).\n115: #[test]\n116: fn self_recursion_alone_still_compiles() {\n117:     let p =\n118:         toylang::compile(\"fn count(n: Int) -> Int = 0 if n <= 0 else 1 + count(n - 1)\\n\\ncount(5)\")\n119:             .unwrap();\n120:     assert!(toylang::emit_jq::emit(&p).is_ok());\n121: }\n\n(End of file - total 121 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The jq backend against the things it alone can say.\n//!\n//! Behaviour lives in the corpus like every other backend. What is here is the two rules jq\n//! forced that the others did not.\n\nconst FORWARD: &str = r#\"\nfn outer(x: Str) -> Str = inner(x) + \"!\"\nfn inner(x: Str) -> Str = \"[\" + x + \"]\"\n\nouter(\"hi\")\n\"#;\n\n/// jq resolves a `def` only against what is already defined and has no forward declaration, so\n/// definitions come out callee-first. The checker accepts the other order, which is a rule this\n/// target does not share.\n#[test]\nfn definitions_come_out_callee_first() {\n    let p = toylang::compile(FORWARD).unwrap();\n    let jq = toylang::emit_jq::emit(&p).unwrap();\n    let inner = jq.find(\"def v_inner\").expect(\"inner is defined\");","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/tests/backend_jq.rs","text":"//! The jq backend against the things it alone can say.\n//!\n//! Behaviour lives in the corpus like every other backend. What is here is the two rules jq\n//! forced that the others did not.\n\nconst FORWARD: &str = r#\"\nfn outer(x: Str) -> Str = inner(x) + \"!\"\nfn inner(x: Str) -> Str = \"[\" + x + \"]\"\n\nouter(\"hi\")\n\"#;\n\n/// jq resolves a `def` only against what is already defined and has no forward declaration, so\n/// definitions come out callee-first. The checker accepts the other order, which is a rule this\n/// target does not share.\n#[test]\nfn definitions_come_out_callee_first() {\n    let p = toylang::compile(FORWARD).unwrap();\n    let jq = toylang::emit_jq::emit(&p).unwrap();\n    let inner = jq.find(\"def v_inner\").expect(\"inner is defined\");\n    let outer = jq.find(\"def v_outer\").expect(\"outer is defined\");\n    assert!(inner < outer, \"callee must be defined first:\\n{jq}\");\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(jq);\n}\n\n/// jq's -r decides from the runtime value, so it would print a present Opt<Str> raw and an\n/// absent one as the word null. The rule here is the type's, as on every other backend.\n#[test]\nfn an_optional_string_prints_as_json() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::run_on(r#\"[\"ada\", \"bo\"][0]\"#, None, toylang::Backend::Jq).unwrap()\n    );\n}\n\n/// `a` calls `b` calls `c` calls `a`: a real cycle between three named functions, the shape\n/// `plans/mini-parser-spike.md` found in a recursive-descent parser's own\n/// `expr`/`term`/`factor`/`group` chain (kantord/toylang#77, kantord/toylang#79). The checker\n/// accepts it -- signatures are collected before any body is checked, so a call to a function\n/// defined later, or back around a cycle, is no different from any forward reference.\nconst CYCLE: &str = r#\"\nfn a(n: Int) -> Int = 0 if n <= 0 else 1 + b(n - 1)\nfn b(n: Int) -> Int = 0 if n <= 0 else 1 + c(n - 1)\nfn c(n: Int) -> Int = 0 if n <= 0 else 1 + a(n - 1)\n\na(5)\n\"#;\n\n/// The six backends this cycle does not defeat: jq's `def` scoping is the one thing about this\n/// program that is backend-specific, so it cannot live in the corpus (kantord/toylang#79's own\n/// AGENTS.md rule -- every corpus case runs on every backend, and jq never can here). This pins\n/// the same \"every backend agrees\" claim by hand, over `Backend::ALL` minus `Jq`.\n#[test]\nfn mutual_recursion_runs_and_agrees_on_every_backend_but_jq() {\n    let mut outputs: Vec<(&str, String)> = Vec::new();\n    for backend in toylang::Backend::ALL {\n        if backend == toylang::Backend::Jq {\n            continue;\n        }\n        match toylang::run_on(CYCLE, None, backend) {\n            Ok(out) => outputs.push((backend.name(), out)),\n            Err(e) => panic!(\"{} could not run the cycle: {e}\", backend.name()),\n        }\n    }\n    let (_, first) = &outputs[0];\n    assert_eq!(first, \"5\\n\");\n    for (name, out) in &outputs {\n        assert_eq!(out, first, \"{name} disagreed with {}\", outputs[0].0);\n    }\n}\n\n/// The cycle jq alone cannot take: `ordered` cannot find any definition order where every\n/// function's callees are already in scope, so it refuses rather than emitting jq source that\n/// would fail to compile with an error naming a mangled internal name out of context\n/// (kantord/toylang#79).\n#[test]\nfn a_genuine_cycle_between_named_functions_is_refused_cleanly() {\n    let p = toylang::compile(CYCLE).unwrap();\n    let err = toylang::emit_jq::emit(&p).unwrap_err();\n    assert!(\n        err.contains('a') && err.contains('b') && err.contains('c'),\n        \"{err}\"\n    );\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(err);\n}\n\n/// Two enums that reach each other only through `Vec` type-check legally (kantord/toylang#94),\n/// but their printers form the same genuine cycle named functions can: `tl_show_A` calls\n/// `tl_show_B` calls `tl_show_A`, and jq's `def` has no forward declaration for that any more\n/// than it does for `a`/`b`/`c` above (kantord/toylang#116).\nconst PRINTER_CYCLE: &str = r#\"\nenum A { a(Vec<B>) }\nenum B { b(Vec<A>) }\n\n{x: A.a([]), y: B.b([])}\n\"#;\n\n/// The printer-side counterpart to `a_genuine_cycle_between_named_functions_is_refused_cleanly`:\n/// before kantord/toylang#116, `printers()` emitted defs in DFS-discovery order with no cycle\n/// check, so this program would have failed with jq's own raw error naming a mangled internal\n/// name instead of toylang's clean refusal.\n#[test]\nfn a_genuine_cycle_between_printers_is_refused_cleanly() {\n    let p = toylang::compile(PRINTER_CYCLE).unwrap();\n    let err = toylang::emit_jq::emit(&p).unwrap_err();\n    assert!(\n        err.contains(\"tl_show_A\") && err.contains(\"tl_show_B\"),\n        \"{err}\"\n    );\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(err);\n}\n\n/// Direct self-recursion is not a cycle `ordered` ever gets stuck on: a function calling only\n/// itself is always immediately ready, so jq keeps running every corpus program that recurses\n/// this way (`join_lines`, `join`, and every self-recursive corpus case already do).\n#[test]\nfn self_recursion_alone_still_compiles() {\n    let p =\n        toylang::compile(\"fn count(n: Int) -> Int = 0 if n <= 0 else 1 + count(n - 1)\\n\\ncount(5)\")\n            .unwrap();\n    assert!(toylang::emit_jq::emit(&p).is_ok());\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":121,"totalLines":121,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/backend_jq.rs","time":{"start":1788130147086,"end":1788130147099}},"id":"prt_054dcb857001JrYzpCSS0c8GDu","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","messageID":"msg_054dbf9a00014Wo3ufUrinp20M"}}
