{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788130194517,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a0356bd73b3364fe","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/check/mod.rs","pattern":"Expr::Pipe|Expr::Select|Expr::Map|\"select\"|\"map\"|fn check_expr|fn synth|fn check_call"},"output":"Found 8 matches\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/check/mod.rs:\n  Line 403:     \"map\",\n\n  Line 407:     \"select\",\n\n  Line 1258: fn synth(ctx: &Ctx, expr: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n\n  Line 1399:         Expr::Pipe { lhs, rhs, .. } => match pipe(ctx, lhs, rhs, None)? {\n\n  Line 1590:     if func == \"select\" {\n\n  Line 1595:     if func == \"map\" {\n\n  Line 2712:     if let Expr::Pipe { lhs, rhs, .. } = expr {\n\n  Line 2720:         && let Some(arg) = call_named(expr, \"map\")\n","metadata":{"matches":8,"truncated":false},"title":"Expr::Pipe|Expr::Select|Expr::Map|\"select\"|\"map\"|fn check_expr|fn synth|fn check_call","time":{"start":1788130194490,"end":1788130194513}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"There's the exact precedent I need! `tests/backend_jq.rs` has `mutual_recursion_runs_and_agrees_on_every_backend_but_jq` — \"This pins the same 'every backend agrees' claim by hand, over `Backend::ALL` minus `Jq`.\"\n\nSo there IS an established pattern for \"runs on every backend but jq\": a hand-written test that iterates `Backend::ALL` skipping Jq. And ADR 0003 explicitly says: \"the corpus plus the agreement requirement is what 'correct' means day to day\" and that things that differ per-backend don't live in the corpus (they live in step tests / backend tests). Also: \"Any future property that is not a function of the output needs its own test kind.\"\n\nSo the \"Cover all backends and add corpus tests\" from my brief should be interpreted in light of the repo's actual conventions:\n- The corpus cannot carry a pipe_through case (jq can't run it). \n- Following the jq-cycle precedent, positive pipe_through tests go in a hand-written test iterating Backend::ALL minus Jq (and jq's refusal is pinned in its own test).\n- But wait — the brief says \"add corpus tests\". Hmm.\n\nActually, re-reading AGENTS.md corpus rules and the jq-cycle precedent, the honest move for corpus tests with jq excluded... there's no corpus mechanism. So \"corpus tests\" can't literally happen for pipe_through unless all backends can run it.\n\nHmm, wait. Unless... let me reconsider. What if pipe_through's design makes it work on ALL backends — including jq — by having jq REFUSE at emit time (emit_jq::emit returns Err), and the corpus case be `refuses: true`... no, then all must refuse.\n\nLet me look at the corpus again. Is there maybe a per-case field I'm missing, like how `snapshot` is a list of backends? No — `snapshot` just pins emitted code for listed backends; the case still must run on all backends (Output) or refuse on all (Refusal).\n\nOK, so given the repo's own conventions, the faithful implementation is:\n\n1. Implement `pipe_through` on the six subprocess-capable backends.\n2. jq refuses at emit time with a clean message (like the function-cycle refusal).\n3. Tests: a hand-written agreement test over `Backend::ALL` minus Jq (following `mutual_recursion_runs_and_agrees_on_every_backend_but_jq`), plus backend-specific tests, plus jq refusal test. No corpus case, with a comment explaining why (same reason as the cycle test: \"every corpus case runs on every backend, and jq never can here\").\n\nBut the brief explicitly says \"add corpus tests\". This is a genuine conflict between the brief and the repo's architecture. This is exactly the kind of thing to escalate. But wait — the brief ALSO says \"take the most conservative continuation, and keep going\" and \"never stop to wait for a human\". And ESCALATION.md is for \"something needs deciding that this brief does not settle\".\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider the design once more. Is there a way to make pipe_through work on ALL seven backends including jq, so corpus tests ARE possible?\n\nWhat if `pipe_through` doesn't spawn a real OS subprocess on any backend, but is instead... no, the whole point is real shell-out.\n\nWhat if the corpus case runs a subprocess that jq can fake? No — jq cannot fake running grep.\n\nSo no. jq fundamentally cannot. The design decision \"jq cannot run pipe_through, so it can't be a corpus case\" is forced by the architecture, not a free choice. Following the established jq-cycle precedent is the \"derived\" choice.\n\nNow, the bigger question: is implementing this across 6 backends + checker + docs + tests within what I can reliably do and get green? It's a lot, but let me think about the design carefully and whether there's a smarter, simpler shape.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider the DESIGN of the primitive itself, because there might be a much simpler and more honest shape that the maintainer would actually want, given the \"full streaming\" requirement and Q35.\n\nKey design questions to settle:\n1. **What does pipe_through do exactly?** It runs `program args...` as a subprocess. Its stdin is fed the piped-in value (as text lines), and its stdout becomes the piped-out value (as a stream of lines). \n\n2. **Input side**: `lines | pipe_through(...)` — input is Stream<Str>. But what about `[1,2,3] | pipe_through(...)`? Each element would need serializing to a line. The simplest honest design: pipe_through takes `Stream<Str> -> Stream<Str>` and `Vec<Str> -> Vec<Str>`? Or is it Str-only at the boundary?\n\nGiven Q35 is open and the primitive's semantics are genuinely undecided, the most conservative design that satisfies \"full streaming\" and is implementable:\n\n`pipe_through(program: Str, args: Vec<Str>)` applied to a `Stream<Str>` yields a `Stream<Str>`: each input line is written to the child's stdin as it arrives; each line the child writes to stdout becomes a stream element; `collect` materializes. And also work on `Vec<Str>` (eager): feed all lines, read all output lines into a Vec. Actually the example is `lines | pipe_through(...) | collect` — stream in, collect out. But should Vec work? For corpus... no corpus anyway.\n\n3. **Call shape**: The language is unary. `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` — two args. Options:\n   a. Record arg: `pipe_through({program: \"grep\", args: [\"foo\"]})`.\n   b. Curried/bare: not possible (unary).\n   c. Change the language to support multi-arg calls — way out of scope.\n   \n   The issue's comma shape doesn't parse in toylang. So I need to pick (a) record-arg, which matches \"several things travel as one record\" — the established convention for multi-arg functions.\n\nHmm, but actually, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe pipe_through should be designed as taking the program and args separately via TWO chained applications... no, unary.\n\nActually, maybe the cleanest: `pipe_through` is a builtin with signature `(program: Str, args: Vec<Str>)` expressed as a record `{program: Str, args: Vec<Str>}`, i.e. `pipe_through({program: \"grep\", args: [\"foo\"]}) : Stream<Str> -> Stream<Str>`.\n\nHmm, but hold on — is pipe_through itself \"Stream -> Stream\", i.e., a function you pipe INTO? In toylang, `x | f` pipes x into f. So `lines | pipe_through(...)` means pipe_through(record) is applied to lines. So pipe_through's type is `Stream<Str> -> Stream<Str>`. But then `pipe_through({...})` alone (without a pipe) is a function value — but \"a function is not a value\". Hmm, how do `map` and `select` handle this? `map(f)` — map takes a function argument. Let me check how map is typed. map is special: `[1,2,3] | map(. * 2)`. Inside, `.` is the element. map's signature — let me look at synth's map arm.\n\nActually the pipe mechanism: `x | f` desugars to binding `.` to x and applying f with `.` in scope. Let me check how the pipe is checked. The right side `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` is an expression referencing `.`... but wait, pipe_through doesn't take an explicit `.` argument — it takes (program, args). How would the piped stream reach pipe_through?\n\nIn the pipe.md: \"Three forms take their subject from the pipe rather than from an argument: select, map, and the match's arm chain.\" Everything else \"takes its data as an ordinary argument, and inside a pipe stage is applied to whatever expression mentions `.`\".\n\nSo `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` — for pipe_through to receive the lines, it must reference `.` in its expression. But `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` doesn't mention `.`! So it would NOT receive the piped value. That's a problem.\n\nHmm. So how does `x | f(...)` work for ordinary functions? \"applied to whatever expression mentions `.`\". So `x | str(.)` — the stage is `str(.)`. The pipe binds `.` to x, and the right side is an expression; if it mentions `.`, the whole right side is applied to x? Let me look at how the pipe actually lowers.\n\nLet me read the pipe lowering in check/mod.rs. Let me grep for how `|` (Pipe) is handled.\n\nActually from the pipe.md example: `[10, 20, 30][1]! | str(.)` gives 20. So `.` = 20, and `str(.)` = str(20). So the pipe just binds `.` in the right-side expression. So `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` would bind `.` to lines, but pipe_through(...) ignores `.`. So that spelling doesn't feed lines to pipe_through.\n\nSo for pipe_through to consume the stream, the design must make pipe_through's argument BE the stream, e.g.:\n- `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"], lines)` — but 3 args, no.\n- The record form doesn't help either.\n\nHmm, so how should the issue's example actually work? `lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"]) | collect`.\n\nOption: `pipe_through` is special like `map`/`select` — a form that takes its subject from the pipe. I.e., the checker special-cases `pipe_through` in pipe position: `x | pipe_through(...)` means pipe_through receives x as an additional implicit argument. But then how is it spelled NOT in a pipe? It'd be a builtin whose signature is `Stream<Str> -> Stream<Str>` created by `pipe_through(program, args)`? That's like a partially-applied... no, functions aren't values.\n\nWait — actually maybe pipe_through IS designed as `(Stream<Str>, Str, Vec<Str>) -> Stream<Str>` conceptually, and in pipe position the subject is the stream. Like `select` and `map` which take their subject from the pipe. map's signature is `(Vec<T>, (T -> U)) -> Vec<U>` but spelled `v | map(f)` where f is the argument. Similarly pipe_through would be `stream | pipe_through(program, args)` where (program, args) are the arguments and the subject comes from the pipe.\n\nHmm, but map/select work because the function argument creates a binding for `.` in the body. pipe_through wouldn't need that.\n\nLet me look at how `select` and `map` are checked — how the subject-from-pipe works. Let me read the pipe and map/select checking code. Let me search check/mod.rs for how Pipe/Select/Map are lowered.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_054dd71dc001doaq1lrNF1nWSe","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","messageID":"msg_054dcbb400013FuJe65FkIqWml"}}
