{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788130200487,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-94357b7ebf2137b8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/check/mod.rs","limit":230,"offset":1570},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/check/mod.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1570:             let base = expect(ctx, base, &Type::Bool)?;\n1571:             Ok(Tir::new(Type::Bool, Kind::Not(Box::new(base))))\n1572:         }\n1573:     }\n1574: }\n1575: \n1576: /// `synth`'s `Expr::Call` arm, pulled out on its own: a call is one of `select`/`map` (special\n1577: /// forms rebinding `.`), a fixed sink or polymorphic builtin, or an ordinary user function or\n1578: /// payload-constructor lookup, each with its own argument shape to check.\n1579: fn call(\n1580:     ctx: &Ctx,\n1581:     func: &str,\n1582:     func_span: Span,\n1583:     arg: &Option<Box<Expr>>,\n1584:     span: Span,\n1585: ) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n1586:     // `select` and `map` are not special syntax, only special names: they are ordinary calls\n1587:     // whose argument is checked with `.` rebound to the subject's element type instead of\n1588:     // evaluated in the enclosing scope, which no ordinary function needs and is why they cannot\n1589:     // be defined as one (see `signatures`).\n1590:     if func == \"select\" {\n1591:         return select_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?, span);\n1592:     }\n1593:     // The one way to produce a new element value. `select` removes elements and a field access\n1594:     // reads a field; neither can turn a Vec<Int> into a Vec<Str>.\n1595:     if func == \"map\" {\n1596:         return map_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?, span, None);\n1597:     }\n1598:     // A sink, not a function: `check` handles the one legal position (the program's outermost\n1599:     // expression) before `synth` ever runs, so reaching it here means it is nested inside\n1600:     // something that would need its result -- and it has none. The old `Str` typing was a\n1601:     // placeholder asserting the opposite of what the fused loop does (a type claiming the whole\n1602:     // output exists as one value).\n1603:     if func == \"jsonlines\" {\n1604:         return Err(Error::new(\n1605:             span,\n1606:             \"`jsonlines` is a sink, legal only as the program's outermost expression\".to_string(),\n1607:         ));\n1608:     }\n1609:     // `length`, `tail`, and `flatten` are polymorphic over the element type, the same reason\n1610:     // `jsonlines` is checked here rather than through `builtin()`'s fixed table.\n1611:     if func == \"length\" {\n1612:         return length_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n1613:     }\n1614:     // `None` on an empty Vec, the same way `Index` turns reaching past what's there into `Opt`\n1615:     // rather than a runtime failure.\n1616:     if func == \"tail\" {\n1617:         return tail_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n1618:     }\n1619:     // Flattens a Vec<Vec<T>> into a Vec<T>, the way jq's `add` flattens a list of arrays.\n1620:     // Joining a fixed, known count of Vecs is `+` (kantord/toylang#97); this is for when the\n1621:     // outer Vec's length is not known at the call site.\n1622:     if func == \"flatten\" {\n1623:         return flatten_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n1624:     }\n1625:     // The one exit a stream has: `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`, polymorphic over the element type like\n1626:     // `length` and the others above, so the argument is synthesised first.\n1627:     if func == \"collect\" {\n1628:         return collect(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?, None);\n1629:     }\n1630:     // Polymorphic over which record, the same reason `length` is checked here: the return type\n1631:     // (`Vec<Str>`) is fixed, but the argument's shape is not.\n1632:     if func == \"fields\" {\n1633:         return fields_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n1634:     }\n1635:     // Blocking, one Vec in and one Vec out with no Stream instance (kantord/toylang#86, Q20 in\n1636:     // draft.md), so both are checked here rather than through `select`'s subject-context\n1637:     // mechanism.\n1638:     if func == \"sort\" {\n1639:         return sort_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n1640:     }\n1641:     if func == \"reverse\" {\n1642:         return reverse_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n1643:     }\n1644:     // The two reductions (kantord/toylang#140): `sum` folds `+` at the element width, `max`\n1645:     // returns `Opt` because an empty Vec has no maximum. Both are checked here rather than\n1646:     // through `builtin()`'s fixed table because the return type is the element type's, which\n1647:     // no fixed signature can express.\n1648:     if func == \"sum\" {\n1649:         return sum_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n1650:     }\n1651:     if func == \"max\" {\n1652:         return max_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n1653:     }\n1654:     if let Some((which, sig)) = builtin(func) {\n1655:         let param_ty = sig\n1656:             .param\n1657:             .as_ref()\n1658:             .expect(\"every builtin in the fixed table is unary\");\n1659:         let arg = expect(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?, param_ty)?;\n1660:         return Ok(Tir::new(\n1661:             sig.ret,\n1662:             Kind::Builtin {\n1663:                 which,\n1664:                 arg: Box::new(arg),\n1665:             },\n1666:         ));\n1667:     }\n1668:     let Some(sig) = ctx.sigs.get(func) else {\n1669:         // A payload constructor is ordinary application (the Q34 path), so `circle{r: 1}` lands\n1670:         // here; it resolves as a variant only after the function namespace declines.\n1671:         if let Some(owners) = ctx.variant_owners.get(func) {\n1672:             let enum_ty = sole_owner(ctx, func, owners, func_span)?.clone();\n1673:             return construct(ctx, &enum_ty, func, func_span, arg.as_deref(), None);\n1674:         }\n1675:         return Err(Error::new(func_span, format!(\"`{func}` is not a function\")));\n1676:     };\n1677:     let arg = call_arg(ctx, func, span, &sig.param, arg)?;\n1678:     Ok(Tir::new(\n1679:         sig.ret.clone(),\n1680:         Kind::Call {\n1681:             func: func.to_string(),\n1682:             arg,\n1683:         },\n1684:     ))\n1685: }\n1686: \n1687: /// A user function's argument against its (optionally nullary) signature: the four arity/type\n1688: /// combinations a call and a signature can be in relative to each other.\n1689: fn call_arg(\n1690:     ctx: &Ctx,\n1691:     func: &str,\n1692:     span: Span,\n1693:     param_ty: &Option<Type>,\n1694:     arg: &Option<Box<Expr>>,\n1695: ) -> Result<Option<Box<Tir>>, Error> {\n1696:     match (param_ty, arg) {\n1697:         (Some(param_ty), Some(arg)) => Ok(Some(Box::new(expect(ctx, arg, param_ty)?))),\n1698:         (None, None) => Ok(None),\n1699:         (Some(param_ty), None) => Err(Error::new(\n1700:             span,\n1701:             format!(\"`{func}` needs an argument of {param_ty}, but was called with none\"),\n1702:         )),\n1703:         (None, Some(arg)) => Err(Error::new(\n1704:             arg.span(),\n1705:             format!(\"`{func}` takes no argument\"),\n1706:         )),\n1707:     }\n1708: }\n1709: \n1710: /// Cardinality-polymorphic: the same subject-context mechanism types `select` over a Vec and\n1711: /// over a Stream, with the element drawn from either's parameter. Stream in, stream out.\n1712: fn select_call(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr, span: Span) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n1713:     let Some((subject, id)) = ctx.subject.clone() else {\n1714:         return Err(Error::new(\n1715:             span,\n1716:             \"`select` needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\",\n1717:         ));\n1718:     };\n1719:     let Some(elem) = mapper_elem(&subject) else {\n1720:         return Err(Error::new(\n1721:             span,\n1722:             format!(\"`select` needs a Vec or a stream, found {subject}\"),\n1723:         ));\n1724:     };\n1725:     let param = ctx.fresh();\n1726:     let inner = mapper_ctx(ctx, elem, param);\n1727:     let pred = expect(&inner, arg, &Type::Bool)?;\n1728:     let source = Tir::new(subject.clone(), Kind::Local(id));\n1729:     Ok(Tir::new(\n1730:         subject,\n1731:         Kind::Select {\n1732:             source: Box::new(source),\n1733:             param,\n1734:             pred: Box::new(pred),\n1735:         },\n1736:     ))\n1737: }\n1738: \n1739: fn length_call(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n1740:     let arg_span = arg.span();\n1741:     let arg = synth(ctx, arg)?;\n1742:     if arg.ty.elem().is_none() {\n1743:         return Err(Error::new(\n1744:             arg_span,\n1745:             format!(\"`length` needs a Vec, found {}\", arg.ty),\n1746:         ));\n1747:     }\n1748:     Ok(Tir::new(\n1749:         Type::Int,\n1750:         Kind::Builtin {\n1751:             which: tir::Builtin::Length,\n1752:             arg: Box::new(arg),\n1753:         },\n1754:     ))\n1755: }\n1756: \n1757: /// `fields(r)`, the declared names of `r`'s fields in the order its type carries them\n1758: /// (kantord/toylang#60: that order is metadata, not part of the type's identity, but it is\n1759: /// exactly the order this reads off). A record's field set is closed, so this needs no runtime\n1760: /// support once checked: every backend can bake the names in as a literal.\n1761: fn fields_call(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n1762:     let arg_span = arg.span();\n1763:     let arg = synth(ctx, arg)?;\n1764:     if !matches!(arg.ty, Type::Record(_)) {\n1765:         return Err(Error::new(\n1766:             arg_span,\n1767:             format!(\"`fields` needs a record, found {}\", arg.ty),\n1768:         ));\n1769:     }\n1770:     Ok(Tir::new(\n1771:         Type::Vec(Box::new(Type::Str)),\n1772:         Kind::Builtin {\n1773:             which: tir::Builtin::Fields,\n1774:             arg: Box::new(arg),\n1775:         },\n1776:     ))\n1777: }\n1778: \n1779: /// `None` on an empty Vec, the same way `Index` turns reaching past what's there into `Opt`\n1780: /// rather than a runtime failure.\n1781: fn tail_call(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n1782:     let arg_span = arg.span();\n1783:     let arg = synth(ctx, arg)?;\n1784:     let Some(elem) = arg.ty.elem().cloned() else {\n1785:         return Err(Error::new(\n1786:             arg_span,\n1787:             format!(\"`tail` needs a Vec, found {}\", arg.ty),\n1788:         ));\n1789:     };\n1790:     Ok(Tir::new(\n1791:         opt_of(ctx, Type::Vec(Box::new(elem))),\n1792:         Kind::Builtin {\n1793:             which: tir::Builtin::Tail,\n1794:             arg: Box::new(arg),\n1795:         },\n1796:     ))\n1797: }\n1798: \n1799: /// Flattens a Vec<Vec<T>> into a Vec<T>, the way jq's `add` flattens a list of arrays.\n\n(Showing lines 1570-1799 of 2828. Use offset=1800 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"            let base = expect(ctx, base, &Type::Bool)?;\n            Ok(Tir::new(Type::Bool, Kind::Not(Box::new(base))))\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// `synth`'s `Expr::Call` arm, pulled out on its own: a call is one of `select`/`map` (special\n/// forms rebinding `.`), a fixed sink or polymorphic builtin, or an ordinary user function or\n/// payload-constructor lookup, each with its own argument shape to check.\nfn call(\n    ctx: &Ctx,\n    func: &str,\n    func_span: Span,\n    arg: &Option<Box<Expr>>,\n    span: Span,\n) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    // `select` and `map` are not special syntax, only special names: they are ordinary calls\n    // whose argument is checked with `.` rebound to the subject's element type instead of\n    // evaluated in the enclosing scope, which no ordinary function needs and is why they cannot\n    // be defined as one (see `signatures`).","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-158/src/check/mod.rs","text":"            let base = expect(ctx, base, &Type::Bool)?;\n            Ok(Tir::new(Type::Bool, Kind::Not(Box::new(base))))\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// `synth`'s `Expr::Call` arm, pulled out on its own: a call is one of `select`/`map` (special\n/// forms rebinding `.`), a fixed sink or polymorphic builtin, or an ordinary user function or\n/// payload-constructor lookup, each with its own argument shape to check.\nfn call(\n    ctx: &Ctx,\n    func: &str,\n    func_span: Span,\n    arg: &Option<Box<Expr>>,\n    span: Span,\n) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    // `select` and `map` are not special syntax, only special names: they are ordinary calls\n    // whose argument is checked with `.` rebound to the subject's element type instead of\n    // evaluated in the enclosing scope, which no ordinary function needs and is why they cannot\n    // be defined as one (see `signatures`).\n    if func == \"select\" {\n        return select_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?, span);\n    }\n    // The one way to produce a new element value. `select` removes elements and a field access\n    // reads a field; neither can turn a Vec<Int> into a Vec<Str>.\n    if func == \"map\" {\n        return map_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?, span, None);\n    }\n    // A sink, not a function: `check` handles the one legal position (the program's outermost\n    // expression) before `synth` ever runs, so reaching it here means it is nested inside\n    // something that would need its result -- and it has none. The old `Str` typing was a\n    // placeholder asserting the opposite of what the fused loop does (a type claiming the whole\n    // output exists as one value).\n    if func == \"jsonlines\" {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            span,\n            \"`jsonlines` is a sink, legal only as the program's outermost expression\".to_string(),\n        ));\n    }\n    // `length`, `tail`, and `flatten` are polymorphic over the element type, the same reason\n    // `jsonlines` is checked here rather than through `builtin()`'s fixed table.\n    if func == \"length\" {\n        return length_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    // `None` on an empty Vec, the same way `Index` turns reaching past what's there into `Opt`\n    // rather than a runtime failure.\n    if func == \"tail\" {\n        return tail_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    // Flattens a Vec<Vec<T>> into a Vec<T>, the way jq's `add` flattens a list of arrays.\n    // Joining a fixed, known count of Vecs is `+` (kantord/toylang#97); this is for when the\n    // outer Vec's length is not known at the call site.\n    if func == \"flatten\" {\n        return flatten_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    // The one exit a stream has: `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`, polymorphic over the element type like\n    // `length` and the others above, so the argument is synthesised first.\n    if func == \"collect\" {\n        return collect(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?, None);\n    }\n    // Polymorphic over which record, the same reason `length` is checked here: the return type\n    // (`Vec<Str>`) is fixed, but the argument's shape is not.\n    if func == \"fields\" {\n        return fields_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    // Blocking, one Vec in and one Vec out with no Stream instance (kantord/toylang#86, Q20 in\n    // draft.md), so both are checked here rather than through `select`'s subject-context\n    // mechanism.\n    if func == \"sort\" {\n        return sort_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    if func == \"reverse\" {\n        return reverse_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    // The two reductions (kantord/toylang#140): `sum` folds `+` at the element width, `max`\n    // returns `Opt` because an empty Vec has no maximum. Both are checked here rather than\n    // through `builtin()`'s fixed table because the return type is the element type's, which\n    // no fixed signature can express.\n    if func == \"sum\" {\n        return sum_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    if func == \"max\" {\n        return max_call(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?);\n    }\n    if let Some((which, sig)) = builtin(func) {\n        let param_ty = sig\n            .param\n            .as_ref()\n            .expect(\"every builtin in the fixed table is unary\");\n        let arg = expect(ctx, need_arg(arg, func, span)?, param_ty)?;\n        return Ok(Tir::new(\n            sig.ret,\n            Kind::Builtin {\n                which,\n                arg: Box::new(arg),\n            },\n        ));\n    }\n    let Some(sig) = ctx.sigs.get(func) else {\n        // A payload constructor is ordinary application (the Q34 path), so `circle{r: 1}` lands\n        // here; it resolves as a variant only after the function namespace declines.\n        if let Some(owners) = ctx.variant_owners.get(func) {\n            let enum_ty = sole_owner(ctx, func, owners, func_span)?.clone();\n            return construct(ctx, &enum_ty, func, func_span, arg.as_deref(), None);\n        }\n        return Err(Error::new(func_span, format!(\"`{func}` is not a function\")));\n    };\n    let arg = call_arg(ctx, func, span, &sig.param, arg)?;\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        sig.ret.clone(),\n        Kind::Call {\n            func: func.to_string(),\n            arg,\n        },\n    ))\n}\n\n/// A user function's argument against its (optionally nullary) signature: the four arity/type\n/// combinations a call and a signature can be in relative to each other.\nfn call_arg(\n    ctx: &Ctx,\n    func: &str,\n    span: Span,\n    param_ty: &Option<Type>,\n    arg: &Option<Box<Expr>>,\n) -> Result<Option<Box<Tir>>, Error> {\n    match (param_ty, arg) {\n        (Some(param_ty), Some(arg)) => Ok(Some(Box::new(expect(ctx, arg, param_ty)?))),\n        (None, None) => Ok(None),\n        (Some(param_ty), None) => Err(Error::new(\n            span,\n            format!(\"`{func}` needs an argument of {param_ty}, but was called with none\"),\n        )),\n        (None, Some(arg)) => Err(Error::new(\n            arg.span(),\n            format!(\"`{func}` takes no argument\"),\n        )),\n    }\n}\n\n/// Cardinality-polymorphic: the same subject-context mechanism types `select` over a Vec and\n/// over a Stream, with the element drawn from either's parameter. Stream in, stream out.\nfn select_call(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr, span: Span) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    let Some((subject, id)) = ctx.subject.clone() else {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            span,\n            \"`select` needs a subject, so it must follow `|`\",\n        ));\n    };\n    let Some(elem) = mapper_elem(&subject) else {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            span,\n            format!(\"`select` needs a Vec or a stream, found {subject}\"),\n        ));\n    };\n    let param = ctx.fresh();\n    let inner = mapper_ctx(ctx, elem, param);\n    let pred = expect(&inner, arg, &Type::Bool)?;\n    let source = Tir::new(subject.clone(), Kind::Local(id));\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        subject,\n        Kind::Select {\n            source: Box::new(source),\n            param,\n            pred: Box::new(pred),\n        },\n    ))\n}\n\nfn length_call(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    let arg_span = arg.span();\n    let arg = synth(ctx, arg)?;\n    if arg.ty.elem().is_none() {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            arg_span,\n            format!(\"`length` needs a Vec, found {}\", arg.ty),\n        ));\n    }\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        Type::Int,\n        Kind::Builtin {\n            which: tir::Builtin::Length,\n            arg: Box::new(arg),\n        },\n    ))\n}\n\n/// `fields(r)`, the declared names of `r`'s fields in the order its type carries them\n/// (kantord/toylang#60: that order is metadata, not part of the type's identity, but it is\n/// exactly the order this reads off). A record's field set is closed, so this needs no runtime\n/// support once checked: every backend can bake the names in as a literal.\nfn fields_call(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    let arg_span = arg.span();\n    let arg = synth(ctx, arg)?;\n    if !matches!(arg.ty, Type::Record(_)) {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            arg_span,\n            format!(\"`fields` needs a record, found {}\", arg.ty),\n        ));\n    }\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        Type::Vec(Box::new(Type::Str)),\n        Kind::Builtin {\n            which: tir::Builtin::Fields,\n            arg: Box::new(arg),\n        },\n    ))\n}\n\n/// `None` on an empty Vec, the same way `Index` turns reaching past what's there into `Opt`\n/// rather than a runtime failure.\nfn tail_call(ctx: &Ctx, arg: &Expr) -> Result<Tir, Error> {\n    let arg_span = arg.span();\n    let arg = synth(ctx, arg)?;\n    let Some(elem) = arg.ty.elem().cloned() else {\n        return Err(Error::new(\n            arg_span,\n            format!(\"`tail` needs a Vec, found {}\", arg.ty),\n        ));\n    };\n    Ok(Tir::new(\n        opt_of(ctx, Type::Vec(Box::new(elem))),\n        Kind::Builtin {\n            which: tir::Builtin::Tail,\n            arg: Box::new(arg),\n        },\n    ))\n}\n\n/// Flattens a Vec<Vec<T>> into a Vec<T>, the way jq's `add` flattens a list of arrays.","lineStart":1570,"lineEnd":1799,"totalLines":2828,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/check/mod.rs","time":{"start":1788130200479,"end":1788130200486}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me now read the `pipe` function and the `call` function (which handles select/map special-casing and the builtin dispatch). Let me read lines 1577-1750 and the pipe function around 930.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_054dd887d001o1n0jtdxTz3DBe","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4v