{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788130591070,"sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a5c301e3f32e7266","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cargo nextest run -E 'not test(every_fragment_is_a_real_program)' --no-capture 2>&1 | tail -40","timeout":900000},"output":"running 1 test\ntest ne_on_an_opt_suggests_the_unwrap ... ok\n\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 12 filtered out; finished in 0.03s\n\n        PASS [   0.060s] (307/311) toylang::unwrap ne_on_an_opt_suggests_the_unwrap\n       START [         ] (308/311) toylang::unwrap plus_does_not_mix_its_operands\n\nrunning 1 test\ntest plus_does_not_mix_its_operands ... ok\n\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 12 filtered out; finished in 0.02s\n\n        PASS [   0.042s] (308/311) toylang::unwrap plus_does_not_mix_its_operands\n       START [         ] (309/311) toylang::unwrap str_takes_an_int\n\nrunning 1 test\ntest str_takes_an_int ... ok\n\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 12 filtered out; finished in 0.02s\n\n        PASS [   0.039s] (309/311) toylang::unwrap str_takes_an_int\n       START [         ] (310/311) toylang::unwrap the_most_negative_int_is_writable_but_not_one_past_it\n\nrunning 1 test\ntest the_most_negative_int_is_writable_but_not_one_past_it ... ok\n\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 12 filtered out; finished in 0.03s\n\n        PASS [   0.041s] (310/311) toylang::unwrap the_most_negative_int_is_writable_but_not_one_past_it\n       START [         ] (311/311) toylang::unwrap unwrapping_changes_how_a_string_prints\n\nrunning 1 test\ntest unwrapping_changes_how_a_string_prints ... ok\n\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 12 filtered out; finished in 0.00s\n\n        PASS [   0.021s] (311/311) toylang::unwrap unwrapping_changes_how_a_string_prints\n────────────\n     Summary [ 120.174s] 311 tests run: 311 passed (1 slow), 2 skipped\n","metadata":{"output":"running 1 test\ntest ne_on_an_opt_suggests_the_unwrap ... ok\n\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 12 filtered out; finished in 0.03s\n\n        PASS [   0.060s] (307/311) toylang::unwrap ne_on_an_opt_suggests_the_unwrap\n       START [         ] (308/311) toylang::unwrap plus_does_not_mix_its_operands\n\nrunning 1 test\ntest plus_does_not_mix_its_operands ... ok\n\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 12 filtered out; finished in 0.02s\n\n        PASS [   0.042s] (308/311) toylang::unwrap plus_does_not_mix_its_operands\n       START [         ] (309/311) toylang::unwrap str_takes_an_int\n\nrunning 1 test\ntest str_takes_an_int ... ok\n\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 12 filtered out; finished in 0.02s\n\n        PASS [   0.039s] (309/311) toylang::unwrap str_takes_an_int\n       START [         ] (310/311) toylang::unwrap the_most_negative_int_is_writable_but_not_one_past_it\n\nrunning 1 test\ntest the_most_negative_int_is_writable_but_not_one_past_it ... ok\n\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 12 filtered out; finished in 0.03s\n\n        PASS [   0.041s] (310/311) toylang::unwrap the_most_negative_int_is_writable_but_not_one_past_it\n       START [         ] (311/311) toylang::unwrap unwrapping_changes_how_a_string_prints\n\nrunning 1 test\ntest unwrapping_changes_how_a_string_prints ... ok\n\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 12 filtered out; finished in 0.00s\n\n        PASS [   0.021s] (311/311) toylang::unwrap unwrapping_changes_how_a_string_prints\n────────────\n     Summary [ 120.174s] 311 tests run: 311 passed (1 slow), 2 skipped\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cargo nextest run -E 'not test(every_fragment_is_a_real_program)' --no-capture 2>&1 | tail -40","time":{"start":1788130591055,"end":1788130591066}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"OK. I now have a very thorough understanding of the codebase. Let me now commit to a concrete plan and design. \n\nGiven everything, here is my assessment:\n\n**The task is a large design+build with several genuinely open decisions.** I'll:\n\n1. Write the design into the docs (a reference page `docs/reference/builtins/pipe_through.md`, plus updates to the streams guide / ADR if appropriate).\n2. Implement the checker + TIR: new `Kind::PipeThrough` and `Builtin` handling, `pipe_through` reserved name, subject-from-pipe special-casing (like map/select), typing `Stream<Str> -> Stream<Str>`.\n3. Implement in the six subprocess-capable backends with real streaming.\n4. jq: refuse at emit time with a clean message.\n5. Tests: Backend::ALL-minus-Jq agreement test (following the jq-cycle precedent), jq refusal test, checker rejection tests, streaming liveness probe.\n6. Corpus tests: cannot carry a pipe_through case (jq can't run it) — I'll follow the jq-cycle precedent and NOT add a corpus case, documenting why. Actually — hmm, the brief explicitly says \"add corpus tests\". Let me reconsider once more...\n\nWait. Let me reconsider the corpus angle one final time, because the brief is explicit.\n\nWhat if pipe_through's corpus case uses `refuses: true`? No — all must refuse.\n\nWhat if I add a corpus *capability*? That's a design change to the harness (ADR 0003 territory). Too big and I'd be making a design decision the maintainer should make.\n\nHmm, wait, actually — let me reconsider whether jq can genuinely be made to work. Let me think really hard about jq.\n\njq runs as a filter. It has NO subprocess. BUT — the corpus harness (`run_on`) invokes jq as a subprocess. The emitted jq source is run by jq. There's no way for the emitted jq source to spawn a process.\n\nConfirmed: impossible. The jq-cycle precedent (`mutual_recursion_runs_and_agrees_on_every_backend_but_jq`) is the established repo pattern for exactly this situation, and it explicitly says \"it cannot live in the corpus (kantord/toylang#79's own AGENTS.md rule -- every corpus case runs on every backend, and jq never can here).\"\n\nSo the faithful, repo-consistent approach is: NO corpus case for pipe_through; a hand-written agreement test over Backend::ALL minus Jq. And I'll document this in the commit message and ESCALATION.md (or just in the reference page/commit message, since it's forced by the architecture, not a free choice — \"derived\").\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider the possibility that \"add corpus tests\" is satisfiable in a different way. What if pipe_through ALSO works on `lines`-free programs that don't involve subprocesses... no, pipe_through always involves a subprocess.\n\nHmm, what if the corpus test for pipe_through doesn't use jq-requiring behavior because... no.\n\nOK here's the thing though. Let me reconsider whether the whole \"corpus\" reading of the brief is even the right reading. The brief is auto-generated by the drive-loop coordinator. It says \"Cover all backends and add corpus tests.\" This is likely a boilerplate expectation (most build tasks add corpus tests). The repo's actual architecture makes a corpus case impossible for a subprocess-spawning primitive (jq). The jq-cycle precedent is the repo's answer. So I'll follow the precedent, and clearly note in the commit + a code comment why there's no corpus case.\n\nBut actually — hold on, I want to double check one more possibility: maybe there IS a way to have a corpus case where jq \"refuses\" while others run... by making the whole thing `refuses: true`? No.\n\nOR — what if I make pipe_through work on ALL backends by NOT spawning a subprocess but instead... no. The whole feature IS spawning.\n\nDecision: follow the jq-cycle precedent. No corpus case. Hand-written agreement test over ALL-minus-Jq. Document why.\n\nNow — the BIG decision: the actual implementation scope. Let me be realistic about what I can implement correctly and get green, versus what risks committing broken work.\n\nFull streaming pipe-through requires, per backend:\n1. **Python**: `subprocess.Popen([...], stdin=PIPE, stdout=PIPE)`. Write each input line as it arrives, close stdin, read stdout lines. For \"true\" concurrent streaming (output while input still flowing), need a thread or select. For the first honest cut, sequential (feed all, then drain) is simpler and still feeds incrementally.\n2. **Lua**: `io.popen` — but `io.popen` only gives one direction (read-only or write-only via mode string), NOT bidirectional pipes! Lua's `io.popen(cmd, mode)` with mode \"r\" gives read, \"w\" gives write. It CANNOT do both directions. So full bidirectional streaming is impossible with pure stdlib Lua. Hmm. This is a real problem for Lua.\n   - Actually mlua — does it expose anything else? The Lua standard library's `io.popen` is unidirectional. To do bidirectional, you'd need luaposix or a custom binding. mlua is embedded; the host (Rust) could inject a helper function `tl_pipe_through` that does the actual subprocess work in Rust! Since Lua runs embedded via mlua, the host can register a function that spawns the process and returns... but that breaks the \"generated code runs standalone\" story for Lua. However, Lua is ALREADY special: it runs embedded and its `print` is a host-injected function. So injecting a host function for pipe_through is consistent with how Lua already works! The host-side `run_lua` could register a `tl_pipe_through(program, args, lines_iterator)` function... but that's not streaming either (the iterator would need to be lazy).\n   \n   Hmm, this is getting complicated for Lua.\n\n3. **JS (node)**: `child_process.spawn(program, args)` with piped stdin/stdout, `readline` for stdout lines, write lines to stdin as they arrive. Node makes this easy with events. But emitted code currently is synchronous-ish. Node's readline + writeable works fine.\n4. **Go**: `os/exec.Command(program, args...)`, stdin = pipe, stdout = pipe. Feed lines, close, scan stdout. Go is straightforward.\n5. **Rust**: `std::process::Command` with piped stdio, spawn threads or sequential. Rust is straightforward.\n6. **Native/LLVM**: needs new C runtime functions using `fork`/`exec`/`pipe`/`dup2` — genuinely complex C, and the streaming/line-reading needs care. This is the hardest.\n\nThis is a LOT of runtime code, and the streaming semantics across 6 languages (each with its own quirks: Lua unidirectional popen, node async, C fork/exec) is a big risk of subtle bugs and deadlocks. And I'd need to write the streaming liveness probe for each.\n\nLet me reconsider the \"most conservative continuation\" once more. The brief: \"take the most conservative continuation, and keep going; never stop to wait for a human\" and \"If you genuinely cannot continue at all, ESCALATION.md plus exiting IS the correct done-state -- never commit broken work to look finished.\"\n\nGiven the genuine open design decisions (Q35, call shape, jq/corpus conflict, Lua popen limitation, exit-code semantics) AND the very large implementation surface with real risk of broken work, I believe the right move is:\n\n**Build the complete design + the full checker/TIR/typing + jq refusal + all the tests that CAN pass (checker rejections, typing, fusion/linearity, jq refusal) + docs — AND implement the actual subprocess execution on the backends where it's tractable and I can verify it green.**\n\nHmm, but that's still a huge risk.\n\nActually, let me step WAY back and reconsider the nature of this task. This is \"Design and build a streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive\". The board row `shell-out-design` is a DECIDE task that is still `todo` — it has NOT been grilled/ratified. The issue gh:158 records a ruling from a grill round but says the shape is \"PROPOSED/unverified\" and that it's \"blocked\" on Q35 which is OPEN.\n\nI think the honest truth is: **this task, as scoped by the brief, is asking me to do a job that the repo's own planning says is a `decide` task that hasn't happened yet, blocked on an open question.** The brief acknowledges the Q35 dependency. The genuinely correct \"done-state\" per the brief's own rules may well be: design the primitive thoroughly (docs), implement the parts that are settled and verifiable, escalate the open decisions, and if I can't complete it, ESCALATION.md + exit is the correct done-state.\n\nBut wait — I should make real progress, not bail early. Let me reconsider what's genuinely implementable and green:\n\n**The checker-level implementation is fully implementable and testable:**\n- `pipe_through` as a reserved name.\n- A new TIR node.\n- Typing: subject-from-pipe, `Stream<Str> -> Stream<Str>`.\n- Rejection tests: not in pipe position, wrong arg type, stream linearity.\n- jq: refuses at emit time with a clean message (like the function-cycle refusal).\n- Docs: reference page.\n\n**The runtime implementation on 6 backends is where the risk is.** But actually... let me reconsider. What's the simplest correct runtime implementation that is genuinely streaming (feeds incrementally) for each backend?\n\nThe KEY simplification I keep dancing around: the language-level stream here is `lines` (Stream<Str>). pipe_through feeds lines to the child's stdin and produces the child's stdout lines. The simplest correct implementation that satisfies \"full streaming\" (the ruling) at the language level:\n\nFor each backend, `pipe_through` lowers to a runtime helper `tl_pipe_through(input_lines, program, args)` that:\n1. Spawns the child with piped stdin/stdout.\n2. Writes each input line to the child's stdin as it arrives (incremental feed — this IS the streaming that matters for `lines | pipe_through`).\n3. Closes stdin.\n4. Returns an iterator/generator over the child's stdout lines (incremental drain — so `| collect` and further processing can stream).\n\nThis is genuinely streaming on both ends, and implementable:\n- Python: a generator function. `subprocess.Popen` + `write` lines + `yield` lines from stdout.\n- Go: a goroutine writing stdin; a channel or scanner for stdout.\n- Rust: a thread writing stdin; stdout read incrementally.\n- Node: spawn + write + readline.\n- Lua: PROBLEM — `io.popen` is unidirectional. But wait — Lua is embedded via mlua, and the host already injects `print` and `next_input`. I can inject a `tl_pipe_through` host function implemented in Rust (via mlua) that returns a Lua iterator function. The generated Lua calls `tl_pipe_through(program, args, input_iterator_or_array)` and gets back a function that yields lines. This is consistent with Lua's existing host-injection pattern. The streaming feed happens in Rust on the host. That works!\n- Native/LLVM: needs C runtime `tl_pipe_through`. Write a C function using `pipe`, `fork`, `exec`, and line buffering. Doable but the hardest; and streaming both directions in C needs two pipes and careful fd management.\n\nHmm, wait, but here's the thing about how the STREAM is consumed in the emitted code. The stream from pipe_through gets consumed by `collect` (eager) or by further `map`/`select` and finally `jsonlines` (fused). If pipe_through is inside a fused jsonlines pipeline, the fused_main loop of each backend needs to handle the pipe_through stage — that's the hard part, because the fused loop reads ONE line, transforms it, writes it — it can't have a subprocess mid-stream that spans all lines.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider. In `jsonlines(lines | pipe_through([...]))` — the fusion is: source=lines, stages=[pipe_through]. But the fused loop writes each element to stdout as it's produced. With pipe_through, the \"elements\" come from the child's stdout AFTER all input is fed. So the fused loop can't be a simple read-one/write-one loop; it's read-all-feeds-child, then read-child-output-write-one.\n\nThe fusion machinery (`tir::fusion`, `flatten`) currently only handles Map/Select stages. pipe_through is a stream-to-stream transform that isn't per-element. Adding it to fusion means each backend's fused_main needs a PipeThrough stage that: first drains the input (feeding the child), then loops reading child stdout and writing it.\n\nThis is getting really big. Let me reconsider the scope of the FIRST implementation.\n\n**Alternative, MUCH simpler design: pipe_through is Vec-to-Vec (and Stream-to-Stream only via collect).**\n\nWhat if, for the first implementation, pipe_through is typed `Vec<Str> -> Vec<Str>` AND `Stream<Str> -> Stream<Str>` (cardinality-polymorphic like map/select), where:\n- The example `lines | pipe_through([...]) | collect` works (stream in, stream out, collect).\n- The streaming behavior: input fed to child incrementally; output produced incrementally.\n- For the fused jsonlines case, pipe_through is NOT fused (it can't be, per the stage model) — meaning a jsonlines program containing pipe_through would need to materialize. But per the fusion rules, if the types say stream and no loop is emitted, that's a bug. So jsonlines(lines | pipe_through(...)) must still stream: feed lines to child as they arrive, then write child's output to jsonlines as it arrives. This means fusion needs to handle a PipeThrough stage after all.\n\nHmm, OR: keep pipe_through OUT of fusion by design — make the checker require that a pipe_through stream is immediately collected or is the direct subject of jsonlines... no, that's arbitrary.\n\nActually, you know what? Let me reconsider whether pipe_through should be Stream-based at all in its first version. \n\nRe-reading the issue: \"full streaming, e.g. lines | pipe_through(\\\"grep\\\", [...]) | collect\". The example USES lines and collect. The \"full streaming\" is about the pipe-to-subprocess being streaming (feed as you go, not buffer everything). The example ends in `collect` — so at the language level, the RESULT is materialized by collect.\n\nWhat if the simplest honest implementation is:\n- `pipe_through` is typed `Stream<Str> -> Stream<Str>` (subject from pipe, like map/select).\n- It is NOT part of the fused jsonlines stage model (fusion stays Map/Select-only). A program `jsonlines(x | pipe_through(...))` where the pipe_through stream reaches the sink... would that even typecheck? The pipe_through result is a Stream<Str>; jsonlines takes Stream<T>; yes it typechecks. And then fusion would try to flatten it: flatten sees Kind::PipeThrough — which is a stream-typed node that flatten's `unreachable!` would hit → compiler panic. That's bad.\n\nSo I need to handle pipe_through in flatten/fusion properly, OR prevent pipe_through streams from reaching jsonlines without collect, OR make pipe_through eager.\n\nUgh. OK let me look at how flatten handles a Builtin that's stream-typed. Currently no builtin except via Map/Select produces a Stream. `collect` produces a Vec. So there's no precedent for a stream-producing Builtin. pipe_through would be the first.\n\nLet me think about what the CLEANEST semantics is that satisfies the ruling and is implementable:\n\n**Design: pipe_through is stream-in, stream-out, and it participates in fusion as a \"two-phase\" stage.**\n\nActually — let me reconsider. Maybe simpler: make pipe_through a stage in fusion (like Map/Select), where the fused loop for a pipeline with a pipe_through stage becomes:\n1. Phase A: read all input, feed to child (this can still be read-one-feed-one, truly streaming).\n2. Phase B: read child stdout one line at a time, apply any downstream stages, write each.\n\nBut fusion's `Stage` is a simple enum and `flatten` builds a linear chain. A pipe_through in the middle (e.g. `lines | pipe_through([...]) | map(f) | jsonlines`) means: feed phase, then map/write phase. And stages BEFORE pipe_through apply in feed phase, stages AFTER apply in drain phase.\n\nThis is a real design. It's implementable but a substantial change to every fused_main.\n\nGiven the sheer size and risk, let me make a judgment call about what to actually DO. I'll reconsider the priorities:\n\nThe brief's definition of done: \"implementation complete; `just check` green from the worktree root... `just fmt` and `just clippy` clean on code you touched; work committed on this branch per AGENTS.md with the provenance line.\"\n\n\"just check green\" is non-negotiable. So whatever I implement must pass. The question is how much of the feature I can implement while keeping it green.\n\nI'm now going to make a decision: **I'll implement the full feature but with the simplest correct runtime semantics, and I'll verify each backend incrementally with `just check` as I go.** If a particular backend proves intractable (Lua popen, LLVM C), I'll refuse that backend cleanly at emit time (like jq) and document it — a backend can refuse cleanly; that's the repo's established pattern (jq refuses function cycles). Then the agreement test runs over the backends that support it.\n\nWait, but that changes \"cover all backends\". Hmm. The issue says \"Cover all backends\". But the repo's reality is that jq can't, and if Lua/native can't cleanly either, they'd refuse too. That's honest — it's exactly what jq does.\n\nHmm, wait, let me reconsider. Actually, let me reconsider the ENTIRE streaming complexity. What if I simplify the semantics to make it tractable and still \"full streaming\" per the ruling:\n\nThe ruling: \"full streaming pipe-to-subprocess over blocking-capture-only\". The distinction: blocking-capture-only = `capture(\"cmd\")` runs the whole command and returns all output as one string at the end (blocking, no streaming). Full streaming = the subprocess is a pipe stage: input flows in as produced, output flows out as produced.\n\nThe minimal honest implementation of \"full streaming\" at the language level:\n- Input side: the subject stream's lines are written to the child's stdin incrementally (one as it arrives), NOT collected-then-written. \n- Output side: the child's stdout lines become a Stream<Str> that is consumed incrementally (the child's output isn't all buffered before the program sees the first line).\n\nFor the example `lines | pipe_through(...) | collect` — the program reads all of stdin feeding grep line by line (incremental feed), and collect gathers grep's output. Since collect is eager, output streaming to the \"caller\" isn't observable, but the incremental feed IS (grep receives lines before stdin closes). Actually for the `lines` source, even \"incremental feed\" vs \"collect-then-feed\" is observable only via a liveness probe.\n\nOK you know what, I've spent enough cycles. Let me make a firm plan and START. I'll design it as:\n\n**pipe_through design (final):**\n\nSyntax: `lines | pipe_through({program: \"grep\", args: [\"foo\"]}) | collect`\n- Wait, or Vec form `pipe_through([\"grep\", \"foo\"])`. Let me decide: record form. Reasons: matches \"several things travel as one record\" convention; explicit; the issue's two separate pieces (program, args) preserved.\n\nHmm, actually, hold on. Let me reconsider the record form vs vec form vs keeping the issue's comma form. The issue's comma form `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])` — does it even PARSE? Let me check the parser quickly for parenthesized multi-arg. Actually functions.md is clear: unary only, `f(x)` groups one argument. `f(a, b)` would parse as `f(a)` then `,` is an error. So the comma form doesn't parse. It's PROPOSED/unverified, so I adapt it.\n\nLet me pick the record form: `pipe_through({program: \"grep\", args: [\"foo\"]})`. It's the most in-language spelling. I'll record this as agent-invented (escalated).\n\nHmm, wait — but actually, a record arg is a bit heavyweight for a shell command. Let me reconsider... `pipe_through([\"grep\", \"foo\"])` (vec form) is cleaner and maps directly to `execve` argv semantics (argv[0] is the program). Many languages' exec APIs use the argv-vector form (Python subprocess, Go exec.Command, Rust Command). And it's unary naturally.\n\nBut the record form distinguishes program from args, which matters if we want to allow e.g. `args` to be optional... \n\nYou know, both are defensible. I'll go with the **Vec<Str> command form** `pipe_through([\"grep\", \"foo\"])`: simplest, most direct mapping to exec argv, natural unary argument. Actually no wait — hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider ONE more time by thinking about what the maintainer would grill on. The issue preview: `pipe_through(\"grep\", [\"foo\"])`. The \"grep\" is separate from the args list `[\"foo\"]`. This separation is deliberate: the program name is its own thing (needed for PATH lookup semantics), args are separate. A vec `[\"grep\", \"foo\"]` conflates them but is the common exec convention (argv[0] = program). \n\nI'll go with the record form `{program: ..., args: [...]}` because it most faithfully preserves the issue's two-part shape in a unary language, and it's explicit. Decision made. Moving on.\n\nWait, no. Ugh. Let me think about which is more likely to survive a maintainer grill AND is simpler to implement. \n\nImplementation-wise: record form means the checker validates the record has exactly `program: Str` and `args: Vec<Str>` fields. Vec form means the checker just takes Vec<Str>. Both easy.\n\nFrom the language-design perspective, the repo strongly prefers explicit records for multi-thing arguments (functions.md: \"Several things travel as one record\"). Record form it is. FINAL.\n\n**Typing:** `pipe_through` is a special call form (like map/select) that takes its subject from the pipe. Signature conceptually: `(program: Str, args: Vec<Str>, subject: Stream<Str>) -> Stream<Str>`, spelled `subject | pipe_through({program: ..., args: [...]})`. Should it also work on Vec<Str>? map/select are cardinality-polymorphic. For simplicity and honesty in v1: **Stream-only**. The subject must be a Stream<Str> (which `lines` is). A Vec subject is refused. Actually — hmm, should it accept Vec<Str> too and return Vec<Str>? The ruling says \"full streaming\", so the Stream case is the point. Supporting Vec too is easy (eager: feed all, collect all) and matches map/select's polymorphism. But it adds surface. Let me make it cardinality-polymorphic like map/select: `Stream<Str> -> Stream<Str>` and `Vec<Str> -> Vec<Str>`. Wait, but then fusion... a Vec-typed pipe_through doesn't need fusion (eager). A Stream-typed one does. OK.\n\nHmm, actually, let me simplify: **Stream-only in v1.** `lines | pipe_through(...) | collect` and `inputs`? No — inputs are JSON values, not text lines. pipe_through operates on text lines (Str). So the subject must be `Stream<Str>`. A `Vec<Str>` subject is refused (\"pipe_through needs a stream of lines\"). This keeps semantics crisp: pipe_through is a text-line filter stage.\n\nBut wait, that means the ONLY valid subject is `lines` (or a stream-signature function over it, or a `map` producing Str from a stream... e.g. `inputs | map(expr -> Str) | pipe_through(...)`. map over a stream yields Stream<Str>, so that works). Good.\n\n**TIR:** new Kind:\n```rust\nPipeThrough {\n    source: Box<Tir>,     // the Stream<Str> subject (a Local(id) from the pipe)\n    program: Box<Tir>,    // Str\n    args: Box<Tir>,       // Vec<Str>\n},\n```\nty: Stream<Str>.\n\n**Checker:** \n- Add \"pipe_through\" to BUILTIN_NAMES (reserved).\n- In `call`, special-case `func == \"pipe_through\"`: requires subject from pipe (like map/select), requires the record arg, validates fields, produces the PipeThrough node. Subject must be Stream<Str>.\n- Linearity: the subject stream is consumed exactly once by pipe_through (check_linear via pipe's existing mechanism — the pipe function already checks linearity of the piped stream).\n- fusion/flatten: handle Kind::PipeThrough. Hmm. This is the hard part.\n\n**Fusion handling:** For `jsonlines(lines | pipe_through({...}))`, fusion must produce a loop that feeds all input to the child, then reads child stdout and writes. So fusion needs a way to represent a pipe_through stage that splits the loop into feed-phase and drain-phase.\n\nActually — simpler idea: make `pipe_through` NOT fusable in v1, by requiring the pipe_through result to be collected before reaching jsonlines. But that's a language restriction that's arbitrary and the typechecker would need to enforce \"a PipeThrough stream must be immediately collected\". Ugh.\n\nOR: support it in fusion properly. Let me look at how much work that is. The `Stage` enum is Map/Select. A pipe_through in the chain means the fusion has two phases. But ALSO: the pipe_through could have stages before it (map/select on the input) and after it (map/select on the output). E.g. `lines | select(...) | pipe_through({...}) | map(...) | jsonlines`.\n\nHmm, this is genuinely complex to emit in all backends. \n\n**Let me reconsider the scope boundary ONE more time and be decisive:**\n\nI'll implement pipe_through with these properties:\n1. Works on `Stream<Str>` subjects, produces `Stream<Str>`.\n2. Participates in fusion properly: a pipe_through in a fused pipeline splits it into a feed phase (all stages before it, ending by feeding the child) and a drain phase (reading child output, applying stages after it, writing).\n3. jq refuses at emit time.\n4. Six backends implement it with real subprocess streaming.\n5. Tests: agreement over ALL-minus-Jq, jq refusal, checker rejections, streaming liveness probe, fusion behavior.\n\nThat's the full thing. It's big but it's the real thing. Let me estimate the per-backend work:\n- **py**: fused_main needs a PipeThrough stage; eager expression needs `tl_pipe_through(...)`. Python's subprocess + a generator/thread. Manageable.\n- **go**: os/exec + goroutine feed + scan stdout. Manageable.\n- **rs**: std::process::Command + thread feed + read stdout lines. Manageable.\n- **js**: child_process.spawn + write + readline. Node emit is currently sync-ish but readline callback-based would need async handling... The existing fused_main is a sync for(;;) loop. Doing a streaming child in sync node is awkward (would need to feed all input, then read stdout synchronously via fs.readSync on the child's fd). Actually node can do synchronous reads from a child's stdout fd with fs.readSync — same as it reads stdin (fd 0) with fs.readSync! So: write all input to child.stdin (streaming as lines arrive), close, then read child.stdout fd (fd) with fs.readSync line reader. That reuses the existing tl_read_line machinery on a different fd. \n- **lua**: io.popen unidirectional. Options: (a) host-injected function via mlua (consistent with print/next_input injection), (b) refuse cleanly. Host-injection is actually clean: `run_lua` registers `tl_pipe_through(program, args, next_line)` returning an iterator. But then the \"streaming\" feed happens on the host between the Lua-provided iterator and the child. Since Lua is embedded, that's fine and honest — it's how Lua already does stdin (`next_input`).\n   Actually, wait — simpler for Lua: the generated Lua for `lines | pipe_through(...) | collect` — collect consumes the stream. If pipe_through is a host function returning a Lua iterator function (a closure) that lazily reads child stdout lines, and the feed happens eagerly... no, the feed needs to be streaming too. Hmm, but actually: if Lua collects the input stream eagerly (tl_collect_lines) and passes the array to the host function which writes it all, that's not streaming the input side.\n   \n   Let me reconsider: for Lua, make pipe_through a host-injected function `tl_pipe_through(program, args, input_iterator)` where input_iterator is a Lua function returning the next input line (or nil). The host function spawns the child and creates a Lua iterator that lazily feeds input lines and lazily yields output lines. Since it's all in Rust (mlua), I can implement it with threads. This keeps the generated Lua simple: `tl_pipe_through(program, args, function() return next input line end)`. Hmm, but that requires the emitted code to build the iterator over the input stream.\n\n   Actually, this is getting complicated. Let me simplify Lua: the emitted code collects the input lines eagerly (`tl_collect_lines()`) and passes the array + program + args to the host function, which writes them and returns an iterator over output lines. That's NOT streaming on the input side for Lua. But — hmm.\n   \n   Alternatively, refuse pipe_through on Lua cleanly, like jq. Then the agreement test runs over Go/Py/Rs/JS/Native. That's 5 backends. But \"cover all backends\"...\n\nOK I really need to just start and iterate. Let me reconsider the STREAMING requirement pragmatically for v1, because I think I've been overcomplicating:\n\n**The observable, testable claims:**\n1. **Output correctness** (corpus-style, but hand-run): `lines | pipe_through([...]) | collect` prints the subprocess's filtered output. Testable over capable backends.\n2. **Streaming (liveness)**: the program starts producing output before all input is consumed (for `lines` source: grep's first output line arrives after the first input line but before stdin closes — actually grep buffers... grep is line-buffered to a pipe? grep uses full buffering when stdout is a pipe! So a streaming probe with grep might not show incremental output. Better test tool: `cat` (no buffering), or `awk`/`sed -u`/`tr`. Actually `cat` is the classic: `cat` echoes each line as it reads it. So `lines | pipe_through([\"cat\"]) | jsonlines` would echo lines with zero buffering → perfect streaming probe. Hmm wait, but jsonlines wraps in JSON quotes; pipe_through is Str→Str so `cat` output lines become Str elements and jsonlines prints them as JSON strings.\n\nLet me design the streaming probe: feed one line to the child (pipe_through([\"cat\"])), assert its output arrives before EOF. That proves feed-and-drain streaming.\n\n3. **Fusion**: `jsonlines(lines | pipe_through([\"cat\"]))` — is it fused? If pipe_through is a fusion stage, then a `lines`-sourced program with pipe_through should stream (liveness probe proves it). If pipe_through is NOT a fusion stage, the whole program materializes stdin first (falls back to eager), which the liveness probe would catch as failing (no output before EOF).\n\nSo to honor \"full streaming\", pipe_through MUST be fused (in the type-driven sense): a `lines | pipe_through(...) | jsonlines` program must stream. This means fusion needs PipeThrough stage support. Confirmed necessary.\n\nOK so the fusion work is unavoidable for full streaming. Let me look at how fusion's `flatten` and the `Stage` enum would change:\n\nNew model: the fused pipeline is a sequence of stages. A pipe_through stage divides the pipeline. Before it: feed stages (Map/Select on input, then the input lines go to the child). After it: drain stages (Map/Select on child output lines, then print).\n\nFor a program like `jsonlines(lines | map(A) | pipe_through({...}) | select(B))`:\n- Feed phase: read line → map(A) → write line to child.\n- Drain phase: read child stdout line → select(B) → print.\n\nFor `jsonlines(lines | pipe_through({...}) | collect)`? No — collect isn't a sink. The sink is jsonlines. `pipe_through(...) | collect` ends in collect, not jsonlines, so NOT fused — it's eager: collect materializes the stream. Eager pipe_through: feed all input to child, then collect child output into a Vec. Streaming feed happens naturally if we write input lines as we read them (which eager `tl_collect_lines` then write-all wouldn't — hmm, for eager, we read all stdin lines then write all to child. That's not streaming feed).\n\nWait — but the example `lines | pipe_through(...) | collect` is EAGER (ends in collect). So even the main example doesn't need fusion! The streaming in that example is about... hmm, if collect is eager, then the whole thing reads stdin, feeds grep, reads grep's output. Whether the feed is \"as lines arrive\" or \"all at once\" — for an eager collect program, the input is fully read anyway (collect waits for the whole stream). So `lines | pipe_through([...]) | collect` reads all of stdin first regardless (collect semantics), then... but pipe_through receives the stream BEFORE collect. The stream is consumed by pipe_through (which feeds the child), then collect gathers child output. Since the stream is `lines`, pipe_through must consume it — the child gets lines as pipe_through consumes them. But pipe_through can't produce any output until it consumes ALL input and closes stdin (grep needs EOF to finish). So there's inherent serialization: feed everything, then drain.\n\nSo for the eager case, the natural implementation: pipe_through collects its input stream (read all lines), writes them to child stdin, closes, then reads child stdout into a Vec. The \"streaming\" is only about not buffering the CHILD's output... which for the eager collect case isn't observable anyway.\n\nThe fusion case (jsonlines sink) is where streaming is observable: `jsonlines(lines | pipe_through([\"cat\"]))` should print line-by-line. But even here, grep/cat need the input streamed to them. cat echoes line-by-line, so feed-one-line then immediately cat outputs it — but we don't read cat's output until... if we do feed-then-drain sequentially (feed all, then drain), the output is buffered in the pipe until we read it, but the total bytes are small. The liveness probe (output before EOF) FAILS if we feed-then-drain, because we haven't started reading cat's stdout until stdin EOF. cat's first output would sit in the pipe buffer (64KB) unread → no output before EOF → probe fails.\n\nSo true concurrent streaming (read child output WHILE feeding) is required for the liveness probe to pass. That means each backend needs a concurrent feed+drain (thread/goroutine/select). This is the real complexity.\n\nUgh, OK. So full honest streaming requires concurrency in each backend runtime. That's:\n- **Python**: `threading` + subprocess, or `select`/`selectors`. Python has `selectors`/`select` for cross-platform... on Linux `select` on pipes works. Doable but fiddly. Alternatively threads (simpler, fine on CPython).\n- **Go**: goroutine feed + main loop drain. Easy.\n- **Rust**: thread feed + main loop drain. Easy.\n- **Node**: write + readline events. Easy-ish (async).\n- **Native/C**: two pipes, one thread or select(). Doable with pthread or fork... complex.\n- **Lua**: host-injected Rust function with threads. Doable (mlua).\n\nThis is a LOT. And each backend's emitted code for the fused pipe_through stage needs to do this.\n\nI need to be honest with myself about the realistic outcome here. Implementing concurrent-streaming subprocess support in 6 languages' emitters + C runtime, with the fusion machinery extended, plus tests, plus docs — this is a multi-thousand-line change touching every backend, with high risk of subtle breakage, and I'm expected to keep `just check` green throughout and commit clean, provenance-labeled work.\n\nGiven the brief's explicit escape hatches (\"ESCALATION.md plus exiting IS the correct done-state -- never commit broken work to look finished\"; \"take the most conservative continuation\"), and that this is genuinely a `decide`-blocked design task per the repo's own board (shell-out-design row is `todo`, Q35 is OPEN), I think the right professional move is:\n\n**Do the full design work (docs + ADR + questions update + reference page), implement the checker/TIR/typing/jq-refusal end to end with rejection tests, implement the runtime on the tractable backends with real (concurrent) streaming, and escalate the decisions the brief doesn't settle (call shape, corpus/jq, Q35 semantics, exit-code handling, Lua/native viability).**\n\nBut given the risk, let me first PROVE the design end-to-end on ONE backend (Python — easiest) before committing to all six. Let me build incrementally:\n\n1. Checker + TIR + jq refusal + docs + rejection tests (no runtime yet).\n2. Python runtime + a hand agreement test over py only. Verify `just check`.\n3. Then extend to Go, Rust, JS, then Lua/native or refuse them.\n\nIf I hit a wall, I refuse that backend cleanly (repo precedent: jq refuses) and document.\n\nLet me ALSO reconsider — maybe I'm overestimating the \"concurrent\" requirement. Let me re-read the streaming probe design: it must prove output arrives before stdin EOF. With `pipe_through([\"cat\"])`, to get output before EOF, the backend must drain while feeding. So yes, concurrent.\n\nHmm, wait, actually — there's a subtlety. In the FUSED case, the program is `jsonlines(x | pipe_through([\"cat\"]))`. The stream `x` is `lines` (real stdin). The fused loop feeds each stdin line to cat's stdin. cat's stdout line for that line arrives immediately, but we (the program) only read it if we're concurrently draining. If the emitted code does \"for each stdin line: write to cat; then after EOF: read all cat output and print\", then during the write loop, cat has written to the pipe (which buffers), and we don't print anything until EOF → probe fails.\n\nTo pass the probe, the emitted code must read cat's output while still feeding. In a fused program, \"read cat output and print\" happens per-line in the same loop as feeding. So the fused loop for a pipe_through stage needs to interleave: read one stdin line, write to cat, then read cat's available output (non-blocking) and print it. Or use a thread.\n\nOK, decision: I'll implement concurrent streaming via threads/goroutines per backend, since that's the cleanest. Actually — for the FUSED case specifically, there's an even simpler model: use two threads. One thread feeds stdin (reads real stdin line by line, writes to cat), the other reads cat's stdout and prints each line (applying downstream stages). Since cat's stdout lines map 1:1 to input lines in order, and cat processes in order, a thread per direction preserves order. The program's main thread can do the drain+print while a helper thread does feed+read-stdin.\n\nLet me now check: does the current fused_main structure allow introducing a thread easily in each backend? \n- Python: yes (threading).\n- Go: yes (goroutine + main loop).\n- Rust: yes (std::thread).\n- Node: yes (child_process + readline, or worker). Actually node sync via fs.readSync on child fd is simpler: feed all input lines (writing to child.stdin sync), but that blocks drain. For concurrency in node sync... node can use `child.stdout.on('data')` async. The existing fused_main is sync. Mixing is possible (node main can be async via a top-level async IIFE). Hmm.\n- Lua: host function in Rust with threads.\n- C/native: pthread or fork.\n\nThis is a big undertaking. I'll start and see how far I reliably get, keeping check green.\n\nActually — let me reconsider the whole thing at a higher level one final time, because I keep going back and forth and I need to commit.\n\nGiven:\n- The issue is titled \"Design and build\" and is OPEN with no comments.\n- The board row is a `decide` task (still todo) — meaning the DESIGN hasn't been ratified by a grill round.\n- Q35 (the dependency) is OPEN.\n- The shape is \"PROPOSED/unverified\".\n- The corpus architecture cannot carry this feature (jq).\n- The language is unary, so the shape needs redesign.\n- Full concurrent streaming is a large multi-backend implementation.\n\nThe most honest and useful deliverable I can produce reliably, that is \"implementation complete\" for the parts that are settled, green, and commit-ready, is:\n\n**A complete design + a working implementation on the backends where subprocess streaming is tractable, with clean refusals elsewhere, all tests green, all decisions documented and escalated.**\n\nI'll aim for: py, go, rs, js working with concurrent streaming; lua + native + jq refusing cleanly at emit time with clear messages (documented why: Lua's stdlib has no bidirectional subprocess; LLVM's C runtime would need fork/exec/pipes that the runtime doesn't have yet; jq has no subprocess at all). The agreement test runs over py/go/rs/js.\n\nHmm, but \"Cover all backends\" from the brief... The brief's done-state is about check green + committed work + provenance. It also says adapt when commands are denied etc. I think refusing cleanly where the runtime genuinely can't do it, with a documented rationale, is the honest reading of \"cover\" — every backend has defined, tested behavior.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider Lua once more. Lua IS the default backend (`run` uses Lua). And Lua's embedded (mlua) host injection is well-established. I could make Lua work via host injection. That's actually not hard: in `run_lua`, register a `tl_pipe_through(program, args, feed_fn)` where the generated code passes a feed function that returns the next input line (or nil). The host implementation (Rust) spawns the child, spawns a thread to write lines from the feed, and returns a function that lazily returns the next stdout line. This makes Lua fully streaming and it's the same host-injection pattern as `print` and `next_input`. \n\nBut hold on — for the FUSED case with Lua: the fused_main for a pipe_through stage would emit code calling tl_pipe_through with a feed function that reads stdin... and then iterate the returned function. This is doable.\n\nAnd native (LLVM): the C runtime would need fork/exec/pipe + a thread (pthread) — the runtime currently has no threading. That's genuinely hard and risky. I'll refuse native cleanly.\n\nHmm, wait, actually, let me reconsider whether I even need the FUSED pipe_through for the first implementation, or whether I can require collect. Let me re-examine: what does the fused machinery actually require?\n\nThe invariant: \"for a stream-typed live program, the eager fallback path is unreachable -- if the types say stream and no loop is emitted, that is a bug\". So if `jsonlines(lines | pipe_through(...))` typechecks (it should — jsonlines takes Stream<T>), fusion MUST handle it. If I don't implement PipeThrough in fusion, `flatten` hits `unreachable!` → panic. So I MUST either (a) implement PipeThrough fusion support, or (b) prevent pipe_through from being the direct arg of a sink without collect (checker rule), or (c) refuse pipe_through-in-sink at check time.\n\nOption (b)/(c): require that a pipe_through stream is consumed by `collect` (making it eager). I.e., the checker refuses `jsonlines(... | pipe_through(...))` unless collected. That's a real language rule but arbitrary-ish. Hmm. But actually — it has a rationale: a pipe_through is a \"barrier\" stage; its output can't be fused with its input (the subprocess is the boundary). Whether jsonlines can consume pipe_through's output stream directly (with fusion splitting into feed/drain phases) is the \"proper\" design; requiring collect is the conservative v1.\n\nGiven the risk, I'll go with **(a) fusion support** because that's what \"full streaming\" genuinely requires (the streaming liveness probe needs `jsonlines(lines | pipe_through(...))` to stream), and it's the honest design. If it proves too hard on a given backend, that backend refuses the fused-pipe_through shape cleanly.\n\nUgh, but (a) is a big lift across 6 emitters. \n\nOK let me make the FINAL decision and stop dithering:\n\n**Plan:**\n1. ESCALATION.md documenting the open decisions and my conservative picks.\n2. Design docs: `docs/reference/builtins/pipe_through.md`; note in streams guide; possibly an ADR (I'll add ADR 0011 for the pipe_through ruling interpretation). Update questions.md Q35 pointer? No — \"do not touch plans/\". plans/ is off-limits. So no questions.md update. Docs under docs/ only.\n3. Checker + TIR: Kind::PipeThrough, reserved name, subject-from-pipe typing, fusion Stage::PipeThrough + flatten support, linearity.\n4. Emitters: py, go, rs, js implement fused + eager pipe_through with concurrent streaming. lua, native, jq refuse cleanly at emit time (with rationale in code comments).\n5. Tests: \n   - tests/pipe_through.rs: agreement over capable backends (ALL minus jq/lua/native), jq/lua/native refusal snapshots, checker rejections.\n   - streaming liveness probe for pipe_through on capable backends.\n   - Wait — the streaming probe needs to spawn the compiled binary for each backend (like tests/streaming.rs). For lua/native/jq that refuse, no probe.\n6. Docs harness: every builtin needs a reference page (docs.rs). Add the page.\n7. tags.rs: builtin_tag for PipeThrough.\n8. fmt/clippy clean on touched code.\n9. Commit with provenance.\n\nWait, actually — hold on, let me reconsider (5) agreement test backends: if lua/native/jq refuse at EMIT time, then `run_on` for those backends returns an Err (emit fails). The agreement test over ALL-minus{refusers} runs py/go/rs/js and checks they agree. And separate tests pin the refusers' messages.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider whether to refuse Lua too, or implement via host injection. Let me try host injection for Lua — it's the default backend and it'd be a shame to refuse it. Actually, you know what, let me reconsider: the cleanest thing for Lua that IS streaming is host injection, and it's genuinely not much code (a Rust closure registered in mlua). Let me attempt it and only refuse Lua if it fights me.\n\nSimilarly native: I'll refuse cleanly (C runtime threading is too risky). And document.\n\nLet me also reconsider — do I even need FUSED pipe_through for the liveness claim, or can the streaming probe use the eager example? The example `lines | pipe_through([...]) | collect` is eager. Its streaming claim: cat's output... no, collect waits for the whole stream anyway. The eager case can't demonstrate \"output before EOF\". So the streaming claim REQUIRES the fused case `jsonlines(lines | pipe_through([\"cat\"]))`. So fusion support is needed for the liveness probe. OK.\n\nAlright, let me also reconsider the SIZE of the fused-stage work per backend, now with a concrete plan:\n\nThe fused pipeline with a pipe_through stage splits into feed phase and drain phase:\n```\njsonlines(lines | map(A) | pipe_through({program, args}) | select(B))\n```\n- Feed: read stdin line → map(A) → write line to child stdin.\n- Drain: read child stdout line → select(B) → print.\n\nIn the fused_main of each backend, a PipeThrough stage is a separator: stages before it go in the feed loop, stages after it go in the drain loop. And the feed+drain run concurrently (thread) for streaming.\n\nThat's the structure. Each backend's fused_main currently emits a single loop with per-stage Map/Select. I'll restructure to: if the stage list contains a PipeThrough, emit a feed loop (in a thread or inline) and a drain loop.\n\nGiven the complexity, maybe I should simplify the v1 fusion rule: **a pipe_through in a fused program is legal only as the FIRST stage** (right after the source). I.e., stages before pipe_through are refused (or the map/select-before is just... hmm). Actually, for the probe I need `jsonlines(lines | pipe_through([\"cat\"]))` — pipe_through as the only/first stage. Requiring it first simplifies: feed phase = read line → write to child; drain phase = read child line → stages after → print. No pre-stages to handle in the feed phase. That's a defensible v1 restriction (\"a pipe_through must follow the source directly in a fused pipeline\") — but it's a real language restriction. Alternatively allow pre-stages (they're easy: apply before write). Pre-stages are honestly easy, let me allow them.\n\nOK. I've deliberated enough. This is a big task. Let me set up a todo list and START, iterating with `just check`.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the very real possibility that this is too much to do reliably in one session, and that the correct done-state is design + checker + docs + jq refusal + rejection tests + a proven runtime on ONE backend, with the rest escalated. The brief says \"never commit broken work to look finished\" and \"If you genuinely cannot continue at all, ESCALATION.md plus exiting IS the correct done-state.\"\n\nLet me start with the checker + docs + jq refusal + rejection tests (these are fully in my control, verifiable, green), then implement runtime backend-by-backend, committing incrementally, and stop when I've done as much as I can do green and honest. That respects the brief: keep going as far as I can, never commit broken work.\n\nLet me set up todos and begin. First, let me verify `just check` is green on the baseline (cold compile).\n\nActually the brief says a cold worktree compiles first — give it time. Let me start the baseline check in the background while I read the remaining pieces (checker's linearity module, how BUILTIN_NAMES is used for redefinition, the docs.rs builtin test, jsonlines.md reference page format, an example corpus case).\n\nLet me kick off the baseline build/check now (it'll take a while on cold start).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_054e1915f001ZyV3R90r3t4W3T","sessionID":"ses_fab2d85e2ffebDq1A7tlFaR4vv","messageID":"msg_054de8922001ThzaDFLa8GDtnG"}}
